    pred:description "Select a node and its related triples as a transient working SET (1-3 hops, default 1), filter the set, and summarize it. Filters NARROW THE SET SCOPE (the summary covers only matching members) while the canvas keeps DIMMING rather than removing — the existing force-stability doctrine at src/routes/(app)/+page.svelte is load-bearing and must not be broken. The working set reuses multiSelected and only persists via the existing 'group as set' -> buildEntitySet. Adds ONE new force (set cohesion, members attract so a selection visibly clumps) rather than exposing the hardcoded REPEL/SPRING/CENTER/DAMP constants, which destabilize the layout at bad values. Summary is delivered as a Shelly chat message via requestShellyChat, ephemeral, with '[keep as pending]' on non-grounded claims only." ;
    pred:principle "A summary is EDUCATION, not a fact. It streams into the Shelly chat and is not persisted — many set summaries are read once and discarded, like story mode. Only where the summary asserts something the graph does not already contain is a triple offered, and then only as pending. Auto-queueing every emergent claim floods review, which is the triage-cost trap F74.3 warns about." ;
    pred:depends-on kb:archive-foundation ;
    pred:open-question "Does descending into a set's sub-graph reuse the KBLeap mechanism (which today switches graphs entirely) scoped to a subgraph, or does it become a view-state zoom that keeps one graph loaded? Leap semantics carry a stable-id lookup and a full store switch, which is heavier than a within-graph descent needs — but two navigation mechanisms that look identical to the user and behave differently is its own trap." ;
    pred:principle "HNSW ALL THE WAY DOWN (Matt, 2026-07-19). A graph must offer JUMPS INTO SUB-GRAPHS that carry the specific detail, exactly as a hierarchical navigable small world searches a coarse upper layer and descends. Two halves of this already exist and do not meet: kb:hierarchy-nav gives LAYERS WITHIN a graph (skos:broader trees, hnav:order / hnav:layer, sibling links), and kb:KBLeap gives jumps BETWEEN WHOLE graphs (urn:reckons:leap stable-id -> switchToKb). The missing middle rung is a jump into a sub-graph WITHIN the current graph. A summarized set is the natural upper-layer node for that descent — which reconciles the ephemeral-summary decision with kb:graph-legibility's SUMMARIZE/condense idea: the summary does not REPLACE the detail, it is the layer you navigate FROM. Coarse layer to orient, descend for specifics." ;
    pred:has-status "planned" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:review-attention ;
    pred:decision "Set controls live in the existing 'Filters & layout' AdaptivePanel — this IS F96's 'the review overlay-type panel JOINS filters and forces in one place', so it ships there rather than as a new surface. Mobile inherits the Sheet path free." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:shelly-graph-control .

