kb:poseidons-council
    pred:open-question "Do we attempt independence weighting (discounting models of shared lineage)? Statistically it is the right correction, but the independence estimates are themselves unverifiable guesses — a number that looks rigorous and is not. Recorded UNDECIDED rather than shipped as false precision." ;
    pred:principle "LEAD WITH DISAGREEMENT (kb:review-attention: 'spend attention on disagreement, not agreement'). The council's valuable output is the SPLIT, not the consensus. Unanimous statements flow through quietly as ordinary pending facts; contested ones get the spotlight, the per-model reasoning, and an 'ask a source' action. A UI that leads with the agreed list trains the eye past the only rows carrying information." ;
    pred:decision "STORAGE NEVER BLENDS, DISPLAY MAY (2026-07-19, reconciling two of Matt's answers that conflicted). Asked whether agreement produces a truth score he said NO — it is a corroboration record; asked how sources and models weight together he chose a single blended confidence. Both cannot hold: a signal that is not a truth score cannot be an input to a blended truth score, and blending destroys the ability to tell which did the work. Reconciliation: source evidence and council split are stored as SEPARATE triples and never merged; the UI may show ONE derived indicator, always decomposable on click ('0.8 — source cites it, council 4/5'), computed on the fly and never stored; and NOTHING AUTOMATIC may read that number — no auto-approve threshold keys off it, per kb:control-model (the instant an agent can edit the graph to raise its own score, the score measures nothing)." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:source-validation ;
    pred:principle "SOURCES OUTRANK THE COUNCIL. One cited passage beats 5/5 unanimity. The council is a HYPOTHESIS GENERATOR; a source is evidence. Where the council splits and a source settles it, the source wins and the split is recorded as history." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:review-attention ;
    pred:honest-note "Nothing is built. The plumbing (providers, merge, review queue) exists; the council does not. Note also that this feature can only ever reduce a KNOWN failure mode — models agreeing wrongly — it cannot detect a fact that every model and every available source gets wrong together. That limit should be stated in the UI, not buried here." ;
    pred:description "Convene N high-capability models over the same text; each returns triples; merge the results and record the vote. Configuration is a list of provider/model pairs — the plumbing largely exists (9 providers incl. OpenRouter, which fronts many models under one key; per-task backends; src/lib/integrations/llm/providers.ts). Merge reuses the product's own machinery: a council member's output is a GRAPH, and merging independent graphs with provenance and per-fact review status is what this product does (see kb:loop-subagent-graphs, which already establishes sub-agent-returns-a-graph). A council statement carries kpred:asserted-by per model and a recorded split; its kpred:verifiable-by is 'unknown' unless a source grounds it — and per kb:verifiability-axis, UNCLASSIFIED FAILS TOWARD THE HUMAN, so a council verdict is never machine-approved however unanimous." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:entity-sets ;
    pred:remaining "Not built. Council config (provider/model list + convene policy); parallel fan-out with per-member graph namespaces; merge + vote recording; the disagreement-first review surface; the escalation trigger from the cheap first pass." ;
    pred:feature-id "F102" ;
    pred:has-status "planned" ;
    rdfs:label "Poseidon's Council — multi-model corroboration" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:control-model ;
    pred:constraint "COST: a council call is N times one call. Default remains single-model extraction. The council escalates only on statements a cheap first pass flags low-confidence or contested (~10-15% in practice), or when convened deliberately over a selected set (kb:entity-sets). Cost is then ~1x + Nx on the minority, not Nx on everything." ;
    pred:depends-on kb:verifiability-axis ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:principle "MODEL AGREEMENT IS NOT INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE. Corroboration requires independent error, and models sharing corpora and lineage do not have it. A council vote is a record of WHO ASSERTED WHAT — provenance — and never a probability that the fact is true. Source diversity is the defensible half of the idea: independent SOURCES can have independent errors; independent MODELS mostly cannot." ;
    pred:depends-on kb:llm-providers ;
    pred:open-question "Does 'council' warrant a new kpred:verifiable-by value, or stay 'unknown'? A new value is honest — it is genuinely not code/test/source/user — but every added value weakens the axis's routing guarantee unless the gate treats it exactly as unclassified. Leaning: no new value; record the vote in provenance and leave verifiability 'unknown' until a source lands." .

