kb:int-google-sheets
    pred:constraint "A REJECTED ROW WILL COME BACK ON EVERY POLL UNLESS THIS IS FIXED FIRST — found in the code while planning this feature (2026-07-31), and it is a REAL EXISTING GAP, not a hypothetical. indexExisting() in src/lib/rdf/diff.ts SKIPS statements whose status is 'rejected' or 'superseded' when building its lookup index, so an incoming statement identical to one the user already threw out matches nothing and is classified as new. Under today's user-triggered source refresh that is a re-proposal and an annoyance. Under an automatic poll it is a LOOP: the user rejects a row, the watcher re-reads the unchanged sheet, and the same rejected fact returns for triage every interval, forever. pending-dedup.ts does not save us — it collapses duplicates WITHIN the pending queue, and a rejected statement has already left it. The rejection memory does exist (rejected statements are retained with that status rather than deleted), so the fix is available: a re-proposal must be checked against rejections and suppressed or surfaced as 'this came back' rather than re-queued as news. FIX BEFORE PHASE 2, because phase 2 is what makes it a loop." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Tool> ;
    pred:decided "THE COLUMN MAPPING IS A HUMAN ACT, DONE ONCE PER SHEET. Header text is not a predicate — 'Amt', 'Cat.' and a blank third column produce garbage relations if promoted automatically. Before a sheet is watched the user confirms which column keys the row, which columns become predicates (reusing kb:predicate-manager's vocabulary rather than minting a new predicate per header), what each numeric column's unit is (the F118 units question), and which columns to ignore. This is F95's confirmation gate applied to a data table: showing the interpretation back before it writes is the difference between a power tool and a way to fill a graph with wrong facts at speed." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:control-model ;
    pred:description "Matt (2026-07-31): watch a Google Sheet for INCOMING ROWS and add facts about them. Mostly ingest, with agents potentially able to update tables too. The first concrete instance of F118, and mostly composition rather than greenfield — src/lib/integrations/google/auth.ts already runs the GIS token flow (token in memory only, never persisted), drive.ts already does folder listing and upload, settings.googleClientId already exists, and drive-sync.svelte.ts already runs a 45s background pull with a content-hash loop guard. A watched sheet is a CURRENT (F29): rows arrive as pending facts and drift translucently in the pod until accepted, never bypassing add/merge/review." ;
    pred:principle "GOOGLE SHEETS HAS NO STABLE ROW IDENTITY, AND THIS IS THE HARD PART. A row is addressed by its INDEX, and the index moves the moment anyone inserts, deletes or sorts — so a naive watcher re-reads a re-sorted sheet as a page of brand-new rows and duplicates the whole thing into the graph. Identity must therefore be DERIVED: the user names a key column, or the connector hashes a stable subset of columns, and either way the derived key is recorded on the row entity so the choice is inspectable rather than implicit. Grist (F119) hands us stable row ids for free, and this is the concrete reason the two connectors are not interchangeable despite serving one purpose." ;
    pred:feature-id "F121" ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 3 — OVERNIGHT WATCH via the user's n8n Sheets trigger posting into the pending queue, so the watch survives a closed browser without the app growing a backend." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:n8n-cloud-sync ;
    pred:principle "AGENT WRITES ARE OUTWARD-FACING, AND THE TABLE IS THE MASTER (F118). An agent updating a sheet is not editing our store — it is mutating the user's system of record, live, where colleagues and other tools read it. So the F52 boundary applies at full strength and then some: an agent PROPOSES a row or a cell and a human approves it before it lands; agent output goes to a designated agent-owned column or a separate tab and NEVER overwrites a human-entered cell; every write is recorded in the graph with the agent, the activity and the prior value, so it can be found and undone. Read is the feature; write is a privilege with an audit trail." ;
    pred:decided "IT IS THE SCRAPE-THEN-DIFF PIPELINE WITH A DIFFERENT FETCHER, NOT A NEW ONE (Matt, 2026-07-31). Verified against the code the same day: src/lib/stores/source-refresh.ts already re-fetches a source, creates a NEW source entry linked to the same URI so the compare/diff pipeline shows what changed, and preserves the old one — for kinds url (Jina/Firecrawl re-scrape), repository and calendar. A watched sheet is a FOURTH REFRESHABLE KIND. Everything downstream — src/lib/rdf/diff.ts, pending facts, the review spine, src/lib/rdf/pending-dedup.ts collapsing repeats through the merge band — is already built and already tested. Build the fetcher and the row mapping; inherit the rest." ;
    pred:honest-note "PLANNED, NOTHING BUILT (2026-07-31). There is no Sheets code, no Picker integration, and no sheets scope in auth.ts — SCOPES is drive.file plus calendar and nothing else. The Google OAuth, Drive polling and pending-fact review spine this would compose from are all real and shipped; the connector on top of them is not. API specifics above (Picker grants, drive.file coverage for Sheets, absence of a cell-level push) are recorded from general knowledge as of the May 2026 cutoff and need verifying against current Google documentation before any of them is planned around." ;
    pred:scope-decision "A BLANK CELL IN A DATA TABLE IS A QUESTION; A BLANK CELL IN F95'S RELATION MATRIX IS AN ABSENCE OF EDGE. The two features both read grids and must not share the rule. Here a missing value on a known row and a known column is precisely a partial fact (F32) — subject known, predicate known, object open — and should arrive as one rather than being skipped, because that is the whole reason structured data belongs in this product." ;
    pred:priority "high" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:currents ;
    pred:principle "A TABLE DIFF IS NOT A TEXT DIFF, AND THIS IS WHERE THE ANALOGY STOPS. A scraped page diffs as a document, so a content hash over the whole thing is a reasonable first gate. A sheet re-sorted by a different column has identical content, zero semantic change, and a completely different hash and row order — a text or hash differ reports a total rewrite, and a row-index differ reports every row changed. The diff must therefore be KEYED on the derived row identity above, comparing row-to-row by key, and must distinguish row added / row removed / cell changed / column added from mere re-ordering, which is not a change at all." ;
    pred:scope-decision "DIFF AGAINST THE LAST OBSERVATION, NOT AGAINST THE GRAPH — and this is the F113 observation graph earning its keep on a real feature. What the sheet said at the last poll is an observation with a method and a timestamp, and it is the correct thing to compare against. Diffing against the accepted graph instead conflates 'the table changed' with 'the human decided something different in review', which are different events with different consequences: the first is news, the second is a DISCREPANCY to route (F114), never something to silently overwrite in either direction." ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 4 — AGENT WRITES, gated as above: proposal, human approval, agent-owned column or tab, full write log in the graph. Not started until phases 1-3 are trusted, because a write path over an identity model that is still wrong will corrupt someone's spreadsheet." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:partial-facts ;
    rdfs:label "Google Sheets — watch incoming rows, add facts about them" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:int-google ;
    pred:relates-to kb:shelly-matrix-builder ;
    pred:has-status "planned" ;
    pred:depends-on kb:source-reference-copy ;
    pred:relates-to kb:drive-folder-sync ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 1 — READ ONE SHEET ONCE. Picker-based file selection, a header-to-predicate mapping the user confirms, derived row keys, and rows landing as pending facts with blanks as partial facts. Proves the mapping and the identity problem before anything watches anything." ;
    pred:depends-on kb:structured-data ;
    pred:open-question "Does the agent write path go direct through the Sheets API, or through the user's n8n like every other side effect? Direct is fewer moving parts and keeps the credential in one place; via n8n keeps ALL outward mutation on one reviewable, user-owned surface and matches the stance that the app owns visual/semantic building while the automation layer owns side effects. Leaning n8n for writes and direct for reads, but it is unsettled." ;
    pred:decided "THE ROW SET IS A REFERENCE COPY ON DISK (F122, Matt 2026-07-31), not a document in the graph. The last reviewed rows are retained in the workspace sources/ directory and the poll compares against them by derived row key; the graph holds the facts, the mapping and a hash. This is what makes the keyed diff above affordable on a 45s interval — an unchanged sheet costs a fetch and a comparison and never reaches an extractor." ;
    pred:scope-decision "PICKER PLUS drive.file, NEVER THE ACCOUNT-WIDE SHEETS SCOPE. auth.ts today requests drive.file (app-scoped files only) and calendar. Reading a sheet the user chooses should extend that per-FILE grant through the Google Picker rather than adding the broad spreadsheets scope, which would hand the app every spreadsheet in the account to read one budget. The narrower grant is also the honest one to show on a consent screen. VERIFY FIRST that the Sheets API honours a drive.file grant for a picked file — the whole scope decision rests on it." ;
    pred:principle "THE APP CANNOT RECEIVE A PUSH, SO SAY WHAT 'WATCH' MEANS. Reckons.AI is a static local-first client with no public endpoint, and Google's change notifications need somewhere to POST. Two honest options and no third: (a) POLL while a tab is open, reusing the drive-sync interval and loop guard — cheap, needs no new infrastructure, and is NOT a watch when the browser is closed, which must be stated in the UI rather than implied away; (b) the user's own n8n holds the trigger and posts arrivals in as pending facts, which is the contact-form pattern exactly and the only version that works overnight. A UI promising to watch a sheet while only polling an open tab would be the kind of overclaim kb:honest-status exists to prevent." ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 2 — WATCH IT. Poll on the drive-sync interval with the content-hash loop guard, detect genuinely new rows against the derived keys rather than the row count, and surface arrivals as currents in the pod. UI states plainly that polling runs only while the app is open." .

kb:release-pipeline
    pred:has-status "functional" ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/test-bench> ;
    pred:relates-to kb:git-analysis ;
    pred:tested-by "tests/bench/scoring.test.ts" ;
    pred:enforcement-gap "The review gates are MODELED in the graph but NOT ENFORCED on GitHub (audited 2026-07-11: main, dev, AND staging have branch-protection objects with NOTHING enforced — no required PR reviews, no required status checks, enforce_admins off, force-pushes allowed). Demonstrated live: a merge intended for dev landed on MAIN and deployed to production with no gate stopping it (the base-branch re-target silently failed and nothing blocked the merge). Constraint: only ONE collaborator (Data-Insight-Solutions), so GitHub required-human-approval would deadlock solo merges (can't approve your own PR)." ;
    pred:in-dev "2026-07-07"^^xsd:date ;
    pred:depends-on kb:ci-cd-kb-watch ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    rdfs:label "Release Pipeline & Branch Alignment" ;
    pred:feature-id "F33" ;
    pred:description "Model the release pipeline in the graphs and check branches against it. Three environments with review gates (static/reckons-production.ttl): kb:env-dev (branch dev, DEVELOPER technical review), kb:env-staging (branch staging, STAKEHOLDER business review), kb:env-production (branch main, both approvals; deploys on push). Features carry promotion predicates: kpred:in-dev / kpred:in-staging / kpred:in-production (dated), plus kpred:dev-approved / kpred:stakeholder-approved sign-offs; has-status 'production' implies in-production. scripts/branch-align.ts parses these claims and compares them to real git state (branch existence, main..branch merged PRs), reporting the pipeline per environment and flagging drift: features marked in-dev but status 'production' (contradiction), 'functional' features not marked in any env (untracked), in-dev features already on main (should promote to production status), missing env branches, and merged PRs on a branch not reflected by any tracked feature (unplanned). Run: npm run branch-align. Complements the code<->Graph alignment in scripts/kb-align.ts (F26) by adding the environment/branch dimension. Development lifecycle — each gate reviews against a graph: (1) BUSINESS DEFINITION of the feature in the roadmap graph (business-goal level, stakeholder-owned); (2) developer + Opus orchestrate the technical spec in the CODEBASE graph; (3) local agents do the task work; (4) PR to dev; (5) DEVELOPER gate — collaborative review of the dev deploy (dev.reckons-ai.pages.dev) with visual-testing agents (VLM), confirming the implementation against the codebase graph -> kpred:dev-approved; (6) merge to staging; (7) STAKEHOLDER gate — business review on staging.reckons-ai.pages.dev of the built feature against the ORIGINAL roadmap goal (does the result meet the intent) -> kpred:stakeholder-approved; (8) agent-assisted DOCUMENTATION generation, reviewed by the stakeholder; (9) merge to main (production, reckons.ai). Env nodes carry kpred:deploy-url and kpred:reviews-graph (dev->codebase technical spec, staging->roadmap business intent); production requires-documentation." ;
    pred:enforcement-plan "DECISION 2026-07-11: allow merges for NOW (velocity during massive change); ENFORCE at LAUNCH (public announcement of Reckons.AI), once the review process is smooth and fully tested. Near-term scope: main only. At launch, enable branch protection on main (then dev + staging): require PR + required status checks green (Type check + Unit tests + Build, Reckons.AI Graph Alignment Check, E2E (Desktop Chrome), Deploy Gate — Graph Render Smoke) with strict/up-to-date, and enforce_admins ON (no bypass) — the automated F33 CI gates become the enforced review. Add required human approval once a 2nd reviewer / review bot exists. INTERIM GUARDRAIL (behavioral, agents): always target dev, verify a PR's resolved base branch BEFORE merging, never merge to main without explicit production intent — so an intended-dev merge can't hit production again." .

