kb:digital-twin-orchestration
    pred:relates-to kb:personal-assistant ;
    pred:scope-decision "SILENCE IS A DESIGNED OUTCOME, and the reason this is a graph problem. 'Animals are expected on these cameras' is a FACT about the property, not a toggle in six apps, and it belongs beside the cameras, their coverage, and the animals that live there. Notification rules derived from that graph stay correct when a camera moves or a pet is added; six independently-configured toggles do not." ;
    pred:principle "THE PRODUCT IS ALREADY THIS SHAPE. Reckons.AI is controllable orchestration of agents running privately and locally, and a camera network is that with a physical substrate — many imperfect observers reporting on one artifact (the yard), needing correlation rather than merging. Every hard part is a problem this codebase already has a position on: several observers of one thing (F113), disagreement is a discrepancy to investigate rather than a verdict (F114), and the reduction of many arrivals into one meaningful event is exactly kb:currents." ;
    pred:has-status "speculative" ;
    rdfs:label "Digital twin orchestration — many sensors, one coherent event" ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:principle "LOCAL, AND THIS ONE IS NOT NEGOTIABLE. Continuous video of a person's home is the most sensitive stream this project could ever touch. RTSP handling and identification run on the user's own hardware; the integration boundary rules apply in full (opt-in, per-user, behind settings, never a hard dependency), and no frame leaves the property to make a feature work." ;
    pred:remaining "A HomeAssistant integration (the natural hub — it already speaks to the cameras); RTSP frame sampling with local identification; event correlation across overlapping cameras; notification rules derived from graph facts rather than per-device settings; and only then any 3D/Blender view of the property." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:design-observed-archive ;
    pred:scope-decision "THE UNIT IS THE EVENT, NOT THE DETECTION. Six cameras detecting one dog is six observations of ONE event, and the whole value is in that fold. Correlating them needs time, space and identity — overlapping timestamps, known camera coverage, and 'is this the same animal' — which is entity resolution against a graph the user owns, not a per-camera threshold. A per-camera notification setting can never express 'tell me once'." ;
    pred:honest-note "SPECULATIVE — recorded as a goal, with nothing built and no commitment implied (2026-07-30). Reckons.AI has no video pipeline, no RTSP handling, no HomeAssistant integration, and F90's Blender path is GLB export only, not an agent-drivable renderer. This entry exists so the direction is written down and so the pieces that DO serve it (currents, graph sets, discrepancy handling) are built in a shape that fits." ;
    pred:description "Matt (2026-07-30): orchestrate physical-world sensors into a live graph of what is actually happening. The worked example is his own: several ReoLink cameras, HomeAssistant, custom OpenCV or similar identification over RTSP streams, and Blender. A dog crossing the yard currently produces SEVERAL separate phone notifications, one per camera; it should produce ONE tracked event, correlated across cameras, and silenced entirely where those cameras are configured to expect animals." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:graph-sets ;
    pred:relates-to kb:integration-purposes ;
    pred:feature-id "F115" .

