kb:blender-mcp
    pred:description "An MCP tool surface over `blender --background --python`: render a scene described in the graph, export GLB, inspect the result. Runs LOCALLY — Blender is free, offline and installed on the user's machine, which fits the thesis exactly: no operator, no API key, no upload of your work to someone else's renderer. The existing GLB asset viewer already consumes the output, and qwen2.5vl is already the visual gate." ;
    pred:principle "Local by default, and the reason is the thesis, not thrift. A cloud renderer means your unreleased work sits on someone else's disk — the same objection as hosted sync (kb:avoid-hosted-sync)." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Phase> ;
    rdfs:label "Phase 1 — headless Blender over MCP" ;
    pred:remaining "mcp-server: blender_render (scene entity -> image/GLB), blender_inspect (what is actually IN this file). Scene vocabulary: ktype:Scene, kpred:has-object / material / camera / light. Generate the .py FROM the graph; never hand-maintain the script. Then the app path: write the GLB into the workspace as assets/models/<slug>.glb + a kasset:model triple, which kb-assets already round-trips and the 3D view already renders — so no new backend is required for a first cut." ;
    <urn:reckons:nav/order> "1"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:part-of kb:content-orchestration ;
    pred:has-status "scaffolded" ;
    pred:honest-note "SCAFFOLDED, not functional. Render AND GLB export both work — scene -> deterministic Python -> headless Blender, verified against real Blender 5.2. NOT yet an MCP tool and NOT yet driven from the graph (scene is a plain object, not a ktype:Scene subgraph), and NOT reachable from the app: a browser cannot spawn Blender, so in-app generation still needs an agent/MCP path or a localhost sidecar. Those are the remaining work." ;
    pred:tested-by "scripts/content/__tests__/blender-render.test.ts" ;
    pred:has-file "scripts/content/blender-render.ts" ;
    pred:progress "2026-07-23: GLB export built (`--format glb`), with checkGlb as its acceptance gate. The predicted failure reproduced exactly — an empty scene made Blender write a VALID .glb and exit 0; the guard rejected it on zero meshes. checkGlb reads the glTF JSON chunk and rejects bad magic, non-2.0 versions, truncated files (declared length > bytes on disk), zero meshes, and meshes with zero POSITION vertices. It does NOT validate binary buffer contents — an accessor could still lie about its data. Exported models carry NO camera and NO lights: the app lights its own scene. 18 tests." .

kb:integration-plugin-sdk
    pred:description "Architecturally separate the integrations (today ad-hoc under src/lib/integrations/<company>/) behind a plug-in SDK: a typed contract each integration implements — id/label, declared CAPABILITIES (ingest / sync / storage / generation / auth), a config schema (keys, endpoints) that renders its own Settings UI, an auth lifecycle, and typed entry points. A registry discovers plug-ins and the app drives them by capability rather than by name. The LLM provider backends (claude/openai/gemini/ollama/wasm/…, already per-task-selectable) are the pattern to generalise across ALL integration kinds. Enables: swap GitHub<->GitLab (F55.3), plug-in asset-gen tools (F58.8 orchestration), a game-engine plug-in (F59.1), and cloud-storage providers (F55.2) without touching the core." ;
    pred:principle "The integration count is the product surface — it must scale by CONVENTION, not bespoke wiring each time. Separate integrations behind one plug-in contract so a new one is 'implement the interface + register', and the app discovers capabilities generically." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:llm-providers ;
    rdfs:label "Integration Plugin SDK" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:gen-orchestration ;
    pred:description "DEVELOPMENT PROCESS (to be documented as part of this): (1) scaffold from an integration template; (2) implement the capability interface(s); (3) declare config schema + auth; (4) register the plug-in; (5) capability-level tests against a fake backend (the drive-sync fake-Drive test is the model); (6) a Settings→Integrations entry renders automatically from the config schema. Documented in the Architecture KB, not a new docs/*.md (TTL-first policy)." ;
