kb:shape-validation
    pred:honest-note "REPORTS ONLY — nothing is blocked by a shape in the product today. It runs at script tier over EXPORTED graphs and is registered --warn-only while the two pre-existing violations in a stale export are cleared; failing the build on day one is how a new check gets disabled. It is NOT wired into the in-app review gate, which is the step that would make it a gate rather than a report." ;
    pred:has-status "functional" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:work-tiering ;
    pred:measured "FIRST RUN FOUND MY OWN SHAPES WERE WRONG BEFORE IT FOUND ANY DRIFT: 78 violations, of which 76 were the shape's fault. 38 were deliberate kpred:moved-to tombstones (a relocated feature carries no status by design) and the rest were a feature-id regex that rejected the real ids F8b, R3, R4 and R5. Corrected — has-status is now required via sh:or with moved-to, which is what the rule actually IS rather than an exemption bolted onto a wrong one — the corpus reports 2 violations, both genuine, both in a stale app export under static/kbs/." ;
    pred:actual-files "3"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:description "Validate incoming facts against declared shapes for their type, so 'is this well-formed' is answered by a rule rather than by a human reading a review queue. A grant-seeker profile, a WebPage node and a Feature entity all have a knowable shape today that exists only as prose." ;
    pred:principle "SHACL DOES NOT REPLACE graph-lint, AND SAYING SO MATTERS. graph-lint already enforces dead-link, bad-status, duplicate-id, dangling-ref, egress-gate and incomplete — every rule hardcoded TypeScript about THIS repo's vocabulary. That is right for this project's invariants and useless for anything else: a user's graph, a shared package or a grant-seeker profile cannot state what well-formed means for them, because the rules live in our source rather than in the data. Shapes are DATA — they travel with a graph, are authorable without a code change, and can be checked by any conforming tool." ;
    pred:remaining "BUILT: static/shapes/reckons-shapes.ttl (Feature, PendingProposal, GrantSeeker, GrantCall), scripts/offline/shacl-validate.ts, 19 tests asserting each shape catches what it claims AND leaves valid data alone. NOT BUILT: the in-app review gate; shapes travelling inside a shared graph package; and any way for a USER to author shapes for their own graph — which is the whole argument for shapes-as-data and is currently unavailable to the people it was argued for." ;
    pred:feature-id "F128" ;
    pred:principle "THIS IS THE WORK-TIERING DOCTRINE APPLIED TO INGEST. A shape check is script tier: zero tokens, no hallucination, right by construction. It decides whether a fact reaches a human at all, the same way F122's document-hash gate decides whether the extractor runs. The review queue is 725 entries deep and has never been drained; a share of that is malformed rather than merely unreviewed." ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/shacl-shapes.test.ts" ;
    pred:open-question "Which shapes first — the WebPage frontmatter contract (already implicitly validated by scripts/docs-pages.ts), or kb:grant-seeker-profile (currently prose in a TTL comment, and the thing F73's scrape must fill)? And does a shape violation block the fact, or land it in review flagged, which is the softer and probably correct answer for a local-first tool." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:evidence "SCOPING IS AS LOAD-BEARING AS THE RULE. kb:review-workflow carries has-status \"pending -> confirmed/rejected/refined\", a state machine rather than a lifecycle; because the shape targets ktype:Feature it correctly leaves that alone. A shape that targeted has-status wherever it appeared would have reported a false positive on the one entity using the predicate legitimately in another sense." ;
    pred:decided "REAL W3C SHACL, NOT A SUBSET WE WROTE. rdf-validate-shacl is a conforming implementation and is a devDEPENDENCY used only at script tier in Node, so the browser bundle of a local-first app pays nothing for it. Implementing eight constraint components and calling the result SHACL would have been exactly the overclaim kb:honest-status forbids." ;
    pred:evidence "THE GAP IS NAMED BY THE GRAPH ITSELF: this roadmap mentions DCAT four times and PROV-O three times, both ad hoc, and SHACL zero times before this entry. open-metadata/OpenMetadata (14.9k stars, Apache-2.0) ships DCAT/DPROD, PROV-O, OpenLineage, ODCS 3.1 and RDF/OWL/JSON-LD/SHACL together with an MCP server, which is a working precedent for shape validation over a governed graph rather than a theoretical one." ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/rdf> ;
    pred:relates-to kb:grant-seeker-profile ;
    rdfs:label "SHACL shape validation — a deterministic gate before the LLM" ;
    pred:actual-deps "2"^^xsd:integer .

