kb:mcp-leap-traversal
    pred:relates-to kb:question-router ;
    rdfs:label "MCP/CLI follow leaps — cross-graph query (leap-depth)" ;
    pred:depends-on kb:question-router ;
    pred:principle "A leap hop is not a normal hop — it crosses into another party's graph, so provenance must record WHICH leap was followed and DEPTH must be bounded and explicit. Following a leap is consent-and-accountability territory (the F91 thesis): the answer must carry the chain of graphs it walked." ;
    pred:remaining "leap-depth param on kb_subgraph/kb_search/kb_get_entity/kb_reckoning; a resolver that follows urn:reckons:leap edges to the target graph's stable-id and continues the query there (reusing the app's leap → switchToKb stable-id lookup); per-hop provenance on returned triples; a CLI default-depth setting; a bound so depth cannot explode the query." ;
    pred:feature-id "F94" ;
    pred:description "The MCP reads across graphs but no tool FOLLOWS a leap. Add a LEAP-DEPTH parameter to the traversal/query tools (kb_subgraph, kb_search, kb_get_entity, kb_reckoning): follow urn:reckons:leap edges up to N hops into connected graphs, so a query can gather context across the jumps a user would take by hand — like kb_subgraph's `hops`, but the hop crosses a GRAPH boundary via a leap rather than an in-graph edge. Exposed as an MCP tool parameter AND a CLI setting (a default depth, overridable per call). Depth 0 = current behaviour (this graph only); depth 1 = this graph + graphs it leaps to; and so on, bounded so a query cannot walk the whole federation by accident." ;
    pred:priority "high" ;
    pred:has-status "planned" ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> .

