    pred:principle "An agent that cannot proceed should ASK THE GRAPH and pick up different work — never idle, never guess. A guess silently entered into a knowledge graph is worse than a stalled task." ;
    pred:depends-on kb:partial-facts ;
    pred:landscape "vs the new open-source agent SDKs (analysed 2026-07-16). Microsoft Agent Framework (Azure AI Foundry; AutoGen + Semantic Kernel; multi-agent orchestration, memory modules, Entra Agent ID) and AWS Strands + AgentCore (Bedrock; model-driven — system prompt + tools + model let the LLM DECIDE autonomously) are both LLM-DRIVEN and CLOUD-TIED: trust the model, run it on the vendor's cloud. Reckons.AI's framework is the near-opposite bet — GRAPH-NATIVE (the queue, schedules, provenance and outcomes ARE triples in the user's own file, not a bolted-on memory store), LOCAL-FIRST (no cloud lock; Ollama local + optional cloud), and deliberately LLM-DISTRUSTFUL: route to the CHEAPEST tier that is correct (deterministic script > local-model PROPOSAL a human gates > Opus), enforce done-when (an agent never judges its own work done), and treat provenance as ACCOUNTABILITY (agents propose, humans settle — F52/F88). Differentiators to KEEP: graph-native state, the tiering ethos, done-when independent verification, ask-the-graph-not-the-human (F80/F91). Worth ADOPTING (ergonomics, not philosophy): MAF's multi-agent orchestration + memory patterns to enrich orchestrate.ts (our orchestration is nascent); Strands' typed system-prompt+tools contract to cut the local-agent harness boilerplate — but NEVER let the local model decide freely (proposals only). Map, don't adopt: Entra Agent ID -> our F84 RBAC / publisher-identity. Interop, not replacement: the MCP server is the surface to expose our graph-native agents to MAF/Strands and consume theirs, rather than becoming one of them." ;
    pred:has-phase kb:async-backlog ;
    pred:has-phase kb:async-ask ;
    pred:principle "The user is the bottleneck. Do not make them a blocking call." ;
    pred:tested-by "scripts/agent/__tests__/async-loop.test.ts" ;
    pred:feature-id "F80" ;
    pred:progress "QUEUE ROUTING HARDENED 2026-08-16 after auditing the real 421-row file. src/lib/rdf/pending-entry.ts now validates the JSONL boundary and drainWorkspacePending consumes only a schema-valid row that explicitly names the active graph. Other-target, unscoped, malformed and invalid rows are retained verbatim; `medium` remains a supported legacy priority normalized to `normal`. Repository audit producers now target roadmap by default, kb-align targets production, and npm run brief reports total/valid/invalid/malformed/untargeted counts. Focused evidence: 49 tests passed. LEGACY DATA IS NOT MIGRATED: 418 schema-valid current rows still lack kb and are deliberately held until assigned; 3 rows have invalid type values. No current queue row was rewritten or deleted." ;
    pred:description "Agents stop blocking on the human. When an agent needs a decision it EMITS A QUESTION AS A PARTIAL FACT (subject + predicate known, object '?') into knowledge.pending.jsonl, tags the work that question blocks, and moves to the next unblocked task. Matt answers whenever he likes — in the Review tab's entity picker, in Shelly chat, or by editing the triple directly — and recordAnswer() flows it back via knowledge.answers.jsonl, where the waiting agent resumes. Findings accumulate into a ROLLING DIGEST entity in the graph rather than a chat summary per task, so an AFK session produces one report that grew, not twenty that interrupted. The user becomes asynchronous; the pipeline never stalls on them." ;
    pred:has-phase kb:async-desk ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:honest-note "Corrected 2026-07-14: this said `planned` while phases 1 and 3 were already SHIPPED and passing 15 tests (scripts/agent/ask.ts, answers.ts, digest.ts — used daily). kb:honest-status cuts BOTH ways: a roadmap that understates is still a roadmap you cannot trust, and it was about to be the source for public marketing copy. Still genuinely outstanding: phase 2 (blocked-vs-unblocked work) and phase 4 (the visual test of the ask/answer loop)." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:review-workbench ;
    pred:relates-to kb:work-tiering ;
    pred:benchmark "BENCHMARK THE FRAMEWORK vs plain Claude Code + Opus, ongoing (Matt, 2026-07-16). As the agent framework grows, keep measuring it against the baseline of 'just ask Opus to do it in Claude Code': tokens (npm run session:tokens), task success, and triage cost. The tiering thesis (F74.3) is only TRUE if the framework beats that baseline on cost-for-correctness — a local job that emits 30 findings of which 25 are noise moved cost to triage, it did not remove it. So the benchmark is the honesty check on the whole design: if graph-native + local-first + tiered does NOT beat plain Opus on a task, plain Opus wins that task. Wire this into bench:agentic / bench:cost as a standing comparison, not a one-off." ;
