kb:design-observed-archive
    pred:relates-to kb:design-vs-output ;
    pred:scope-decision "THE ARCHIVE LEG IS OFTEN ALREADY IMPLEMENTED, AND THIS MUST NOT DUPLICATE IT. For anything text and committed, GIT IS THE ARCHIVE, and building a parallel journal for source files or content/*.md would be worse than useless. What git does NOT give is queryable history of the OBSERVED graph — 'this site was 100% aligned last week and is 60% now' is a question about a derived measurement over time, which no diff answers. So: adopt git as the archive where it fits, and add a real archive graph only where the artifact is binary, generated destructively, or where observed-state history is the question." ;
    pred:decided "INSTANCES THAT ALREADY EXIST, named so the pattern is recognised rather than reinvented: CODE — reckons-roadmap.ttl (design) / reckons-production.ttl + reckons-codebase.ttl (observed) / git (archive), compared by scripts/kb-align.ts. WEBSITE — static/website.ttl (design) / static/website-status.ttl (observed, derived by npm run website:align) / git for content, NO observed-history archive yet. GRAPH EDITS — the F97 archive journal is a pure archive leg with full pre-operation snapshots and retention, and has no design or observed counterpart. So of three domains, none has all three legs, which is the finding." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:ci-cd-kb-watch ;
    rdfs:label "Design / observed / archive — three graphs for anything generated" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:verifiability-axis ;
    pred:principle "THE THREE GRAPHS ARE NOT PEERS. Design is AUTHORED and is the only one a human edits. Observed is GENERATED and hand-editing it is a category error — it would be editing the measurement to match the plan. Archive is APPEND-MOSTLY: retention may drop payloads, but never the record that something happened, because losing the ability to revert is recoverable and losing the knowledge that a change occurred is not (F97's rule, generalised)." ;
    pred:open-question "Where does an agent's PROPOSAL live? An agent generating a page or an asset produces a candidate that is neither intent nor observed reality. Today proposals go to knowledge.pending.jsonl regardless of kind, which is why that queue is 586 deep and undrained. A fourth position — proposed — may be warranted, or proposals may belong in design-with-a-status. Deciding this badly is how the review queue becomes the bottleneck for every generative feature at once." ;
    pred:principle "OBSERVED MUST BE DERIVED FROM THE ARTIFACT, NEVER DECLARED BESIDE IT. A status graph written by the same process that produced the thing agrees with itself by construction and proves nothing — the renderer certifying its own render, which is the thesis violated at the last step. This is the rule that makes the paradigm worth anything, and it is the one most easily lost, because declaring is cheap and observing is not." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:auto-archive ;
    pred:principle "THE ARCHIVE IS WHAT SEPARATES 'NEVER BUILT' FROM 'BUILT THEN REGRESSED'. Design versus observed gives only the CURRENT gap, and those two states produce an identical gap while needing opposite responses. Without the third graph, every regression reads as unfinished work and every unfinished feature reads as a regression." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Tenet> ;
    pred:scope-decision "AN OBSERVATION METHOD THAT CAN LIE MAKES 'OBSERVED' JUST ANOTHER DECLARATION, and this is the paradigm's real failure mode. F90 records the case exactly: headless Blender renders a BLACK FRAME AND EXITS 0, so a naive observer reports success on an empty artifact. Every observed graph must therefore state HOW it observed and what that method cannot see — the website status graph does this, recording that it reads the content layer and not the rendered DOM. Where the method is weak, F88 verifiable-by decides who must confirm it." ;
    pred:principle "THE ARCHIVE IS ALSO WHAT MAKES DESTRUCTIVE REGENERATION SAFE. npm run docs:compose deletes 269 files to write 8; that is only defensible because both generators are reversible and hand-authored content is untouched. Where reversal is not that cheap — a rendered asset, a published page someone edited — the archive is the thing standing between regeneration and loss." ;
    pred:open-question "Does DESIGN always precede the artifact, or may it be adopted FROM a mature observed state? The docs went the second way — 271 pages existed and the design was written afterwards to describe eight. That worked, but it means design can be back-fitted to bless whatever exists, which is how an intent graph quietly stops constraining anything. A rule may be needed: adopting design from observed is allowed once, at introduction, and never as a way to resolve a divergence." ;
    pred:description "Matt (2026-07-30): make this a consistent paradigm rather than a thing rebuilt per feature. Anything an agent GENERATES — code, web pages, documents, digital assets — gets three graphs. DESIGN is authored intent: what this should be. OBSERVED is derived reality: what it actually is, extracted from the artifact itself. ARCHIVE is what it used to be: a journal of transitions carrying enough to answer 'when did this change and what was it before'. The paradigm is not new machinery; it is a name for a shape this repository already builds ad hoc, so the missing leg becomes visible instead of merely absent." .

