kb:tier-script-ci
    rdfs:label "Script tier in CI — blocking" ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Phase> ;
    pred:part-of kb:work-tiering ;
    pred:principle "A finding is a CLAIM. A tool that reports a violation is a party with an interest in having found one. Verify the finding before you act on it — especially when acting on it means weakening a control." ;
    <urn:reckons:nav/order> "4"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:correction "CORRECTED 2026-07-14, and the correction is the lesson. This shipped ADVISORY for one day because published-graph-guard reported '166 test-harness terms' in the published graph. THE FINDING WAS FALSE: the guard banned `urn:reckons:story/`, which is the PRODUCT'S OWN guided-story vocabulary (src/lib/rdf/story.ts, used by the landing page and TurtleChatPanel, declared in reckons-production.ttl). The real harness namespace is `urn:reckons:test/`, and it appears ZERO times. The published graph was clean the entire time. An unverified finding, believed and repeated across three commit messages, kept a real gate switched off — an unverifiable claim treated as evidence, which is the exact failure this product exists to refuse. We committed it against ourselves." ;
    pred:description "The script tier runs on every push/PR (ci.yml, job `script-tier`) and FAILS the build when a job marked blocking:true fails. jobs.json carries the `blocking` flag; run-all.ts has a `--ci` mode that exits non-zero. Blocking today: published-graph-guard (static/knowledge.ttl IS the public graph — the one failure you cannot take back once pushed) and build-guard (a dev-mode artifact must never deploy). Everything else reports and queues findings without gating, because a diagnostic that fails the build on every finding gets switched off, and then it protects nothing." ;
    pred:correction "CORRECTED 2026-08-17: the honest-header check compared the exporter's app-Statement header with every RDF triple parsed by N3. Because toTurtle emits two provenance decorations per Statement, the honest 221-statement header was falsely compared with 663 RDF triples. The guard now reports both units, compares the header with 221 asserted Statements, excludes the derived content-advisory triple, and applies a recalibrated 100-Statement catastrophic-truncation floor to the logical unit instead of relying on provenance inflation. This is regression-covered against the checked-in public graph." ;
    pred:has-status "functional" .

kb:prune-analysis
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/prune.test.ts" ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:has-status "functional" ;
    pred:description "What does this graph carry that it does not need? Two lenses, one idea (src/lib/rdf/prune.ts). MAIN-GRAPH prune (analyzeNodePrune): a node earns its place by RELATIONAL STRENGTH (degree — how connected it is to the rest) and by RELEVANCE (fit to the graph's title + description); a node that is weakly connected AND off-theme is clutter and is flagged — as a REVIEWABLE suggestion, never a silent delete, because pruning a node the user cared about destroys the one thing this app protects. REVIEW-MODE prune (analyzeSuggestionPrune): scores each pending SUGGESTION for pruning — re-derivable (a check regenerates it), empty/malformed, or stale (old, blocks nothing, low priority) — sharing the triage classifier so 're-derivable' means the same everywhere. Both cores are DETERMINISTIC and model-free (degree + lexical overlap), so they run offline/in CI; embedding relevance is an optional plug-in (opts.relevance) exactly as current-ranking.ts anchors on top-degree entities. `npm run prune` applies the review-mode prune to knowledge.pending.jsonl (dry-run by default; --apply backs up first). First run cleared 17 re-derivable graph-lint dupes." ;
    pred:feature-id "F98" ;
    pred:has-file "src/lib/rdf/prune.ts" ;
    pred:remaining "REVIEW UI: surface node-prune as a Review analysis tab (candidates ranked most-prunable first, each a reviewable suggestion), alongside incoming/deletions/merges/align — the primitive + suggestion-prune CLI are built, the node-prune UI tab is not. Wire the in-app embedding relevance (embed.ts) as opts.relevance so the main-graph lens uses semantic, not just lexical, fit. One-time normalize of pre-existing competitor-scan/code-review entries mis-typed 'question' -> 'suggestion' (emitters already fixed at source)." ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/rdf> ;
    pred:relates-to kb:review-workbench ;
    pred:has-file "scripts/agent/prune-queue.ts" ;
    pred:decided "Matt (2026-07-16): the app needs a prune analysis in review. MAIN-graph prune weighs all nodes by relational strength to each other AND relevance to graph title/description; REVIEW-mode prune prunes suggestions. Prune proposes; the human disposes — a node prune is a suggestion, not a deletion." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:async-backlog ;
    rdfs:label "Prune analysis — declutter, two lenses" .

