kb:finding-taxonomy
    pred:decided "DRIFT MUST NEVER BLOCK A BUILD, and that is encoded rather than left to habit. Blocking forces a fast answer to the question that most needs a slow one — which side is wrong. FORM and DEFECT may block, because neither requires judging intent." ;
    pred:decided "SCOPE IS A SECOND AXIS, NOT A FLAVOUR OF DRIFT (Matt, 2026-08-14: 'scope increase (aligned) — like new dependency or touching other features that were not predicted to be needed' versus 'scope increase (not aligned, drift)'). The test mirrors the first: does the extra work serve the STATED INTENT? If yes it is EXPANDED-ALIGNED — legitimate discovery, and the response is to AMEND THE PLAN, since F114 already records that design completeness is relative to the use cases known at the time. If no it is EXPANDED-UNALIGNED, which is drift at the PLAN level and needs a decision: split it out, or revert." ;
    pred:principle "THE CLASSES DIFFER IN WHO CAN SETTLE THEM AND WHERE THE FIX LANDS, which is why this is routing rather than tidiness. FORM: malformed artifact, decidable from the artifact alone, one right answer, SAFE TO BLOCK A BUILD ON. DRIFT: the claim disagrees with reality, TWO possible fixes (correct the graph or correct the world), so choosing is a judgement that must reach a human and must never auto-resolve. DEFECT: the world is broken while the graph is right, so the fix never touches the graph." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:shape-validation ;
    pred:has-status "functional" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:honest-status ;
    pred:honest-note "TWO EMITTERS RECLASSIFIED, THE REST NOT. shacl-validate now emits findingClass 'form' (it was typed drift-warning on its first day, by me) and server-health emits 'defect'. claim-audit is correctly drift and unchanged. The remaining 54 historical drift-warning entries in the queue are NOT reclassified — they predate the field and would need re-reading one by one, which is triage rather than a migration." ;
    pred:description "Matt, 2026-08-14: 'can we clearly classify drift from other syntax or functional errors?' Two orthogonal axes: a FINDING class (form / drift / defect) about a discrepancy, and a SCOPE class (planned / expanded-aligned / expanded-unaligned) about a change." ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/finding-class.test.ts" ;
    pred:decided "THE DISCRIMINATING TEST: if you repaired the world, would the graph become TRUE without editing it? Yes means DEFECT — the claim was right and reality had not caught up. No means DRIFT — the claim itself was wrong. That test separates the two cases this project actually hit: 'repo-ingest is functional' while CSP blocked every request LOOKED like drift, but adding the origin made the graph true without touching it (defect); SAFETY.md claiming a publishing gate that was never built could not be repaired by building anything, so the sentence had to change (drift)." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:ci-cd-kb-watch ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:measured "MEASURED BEFORE BUILDING: no. 'drift-warning' was emitted by claim-audit (a claim contradicting reality), server-health (a server being down) and shacl-validate (a node missing a label) — three different kinds of wrong under one label, 54 entries from NINE agents, and NO entity anywhere defined what drift is. CLAUDE.md said only 'Drift detected: use type drift-warning', an instruction with no definition." ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/rdf> ;
    rdfs:label "Classify what KIND of wrong a finding is — and what kind of scope change" ;
    pred:priority "high" ;
    pred:remaining "BUILT: src/lib/rdf/finding-class.ts (both axes, discriminating tests, blockable/flag routing), 23 tests including the real cases from this session; findingClass threaded through PendingEntry, the drain and Statement; both classes enumerable in SHACL so a misclassification is catchable rather than a matter of author attention. NOT BUILT: kb-align does not consume the scope axis; the review UI does not display or filter by class; and no job yet emits a scope-class at all, so that axis is defined and unused." ;
    pred:feature-id "F130" ;
    pred:known-issue "kb-align CANNOT EXPRESS THIS AND CURRENTLY PENALISES THE HONEST CASE. scripts/kb-align.ts computes scopeScore = 1 - (unmatched / changed) * 0.8, docking every unmatched change identically — so discovering a dependency you genuinely needed scores the same as wandering off the plan. That rewards under-reporting what a change actually touched. Wiring the scope axis into that score is the obvious next step and is NOT done." .

kb:local-orchestration
    pred:progress "MCP CONTEXT CONSUMING PROOF (2026-08-17). The corrected standing kb_compress query returned F81/local-orchestration, 8,561 characters, SHA-256 b26d3ab851e98c02e5558f62fad9520327bd4f8a35f7be2a36628a4d9a410c63, and no F108.3. qwen3-coder then consumed exactly those bytes while reviewing runner.ts and tasks.ttl: 2/2 files over five complete bounded chunks, zero omitted/failed/truncated, and the independent report verifier recomputed both source and consumed hashes. QUALITY LIMIT: it emitted 13 candidate lines; the cap retained one false positive claiming the already-present --graph guard was missing. Transport/evidence is now credible; proposal precision is not." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:work-tiering ;
    pred:has-status "in-progress" ;
    pred:remaining "PARALLEL LOCAL-AGENT RUNNER (F81, next build): dispatch up to N concurrent local-agent tasks across the two 3090 Ti — e.g. two Ollama instances pinned per GPU (CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0/1 on ports 11434/11435) or Ollama's own multi-GPU, with the runner assigning a due task to a free GPU (prefer keeping each GPU on one model to avoid reload thrash, the model-batching rule per lane). done-when: two different-model tasks measurably overlap in wall-clock. Extends scripts/agent/runner.ts (today serial)." ;
    pred:principle "DO NOT SCHEDULE — DRAIN. Invert the model: a task carries a due-time, and whenever the app is open the Worker drains everything unblocked and due. 'Run at T' becomes 'the next time you are here, this is ready'. That is strictly MORE reliable than the cron that fired and silently did nothing, because the queue and its outcomes are visible in the app: the user can SEE what ran, what is waiting, and what it is waiting on." ;
    pred:principle "A scheduler you cannot observe is not a scheduler, it is a rumour. Every run writes its outcome back to the graph — including 'I did nothing, and here is why'." ;
    pred:constraint "HONEST LIMIT — A WORKER CANNOT BE A SCHEDULER. A Web Worker lives only while a tab is open. A Service Worker survives page close but browsers kill idle ones in ~30s, and true timed wakeup (Periodic Background Sync) is Chromium-only, needs an INSTALLED PWA, and is gated behind engagement heuristics — Safari and Firefox do not implement it. So 'fire at 3:40 with nothing open' is not something the browser will honestly deliver on every device, and designing as if it will is how you get another silent failure." ;
    pred:correction "PRECISION, 2026-07-13: the cron did NOT fail. It fired on time (last_fired_at 20:40:18Z, exactly as scheduled). What failed was the RUNNER (the cloud agent produced no commits) and the OBSERVABILITY (nothing reported that). Blaming the scheduling primitive would lead to fixing the wrong component. The lesson is about runners and reporting, not about cron." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:async-orchestration ;
    pred:decided "WEB WORKER IN THE APP (Matt, 2026-07-13), for cross-device reach. The browser is the most portable runtime there is, and it is already open all day — so the local tier lives where the user already is: desktop, laptop, tablet, phone, same code. WASM models (transformers.js) run inside a Worker, so the whole local tier ships with the app and needs nothing installed." ;
    pred:feature-id "F81" ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:progress "WAVE 0 HARDENING IMPLEMENTED, UNCOMMITTED (2026-08-17). Success is commandExit=0 AND verificationExit=0; button/visual/review tasks verify schema-versioned fresh reports rather than mtime; receipts bind HEAD plus staged, unstaged and untracked worktree state; state claims and the host pending queue use short flock transactions plus fsync/atomic rename; claim tokens and live leases fence stale completions; WAITING survives pending-row drain through durable subject|predicate keys. The focused evidence is 12 files / 139 tests, including deterministic two-process runner and queue races; npm check is 0 errors / 4 pre-existing warnings. New helper/test files are still untracked in the broad patch, so formal has-file/tested-by edges wait for the commit that first tracks them." ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/agent-task.test.ts" ;
    pred:has-file "scripts/agent/runner.ts" ;
    pred:constraint "AN INSTALLED PWA IS STILL NOT A SCHEDULER. Periodic Background Sync unlocks on Chromium once installed, but the BROWSER decides whether and how often it fires, from engagement heuristics — commonly no more than once every ~12h, and never at a time you choose. Safari and Firefox do not implement it. So treat it as an OPPORTUNISTIC BONUS on top of drain-when-open, never a foundation. Building on it would reproduce exactly the failure we just had: a scheduler that looks armed and quietly does nothing." ;
    pred:principle "Silence must never look like success. The cloud cron fired, produced nothing, and reported nothing; that is the failure mode to design against." ;
    pred:proof "Schedules live in the GRAPH (reckons-workspace/schedules.ttl, ktype:Schedule). `npm run schedule` reads the TTL, reports what is due, runs the due ones, and writes last-run/next-due back — verified end to end: a schedule executed `orchestrate --json` and its state was tracked. kpred:every is an INTERVAL, not cron: the next due time is computed from when it actually ran, so a machine asleep for a week wakes owing one run, not seven. Whatever triggers it (cron, systemd, unsnooze, a human) reads the graph to know what to do — the plan is not baked into a crontab nobody can diff. MODEL BATCHING (2026-07-15): a task declares kpred:model, and orderForModelBatching runs same-model local-agent tasks CONSECUTIVELY — Ollama + RAM cannot switch models cheaply, so keeping one model warm across its whole batch avoids a reload between every task. Script tier stays first (free, no model); the sort is stable so priority within a model is preserved. 5 tests; the runner logs each model-batch boundary because every boundary is a real reload." ;
    pred:description "Tasks, schedules and outcomes live in the graph and run locally. A task is an entity (what to do, which TIER it belongs to, what it depends on, what question blocks it); a schedule is a triple; a run writes its result back as facts. That makes the whole loop QUERYABLE: what ran, what it found, what it is waiting on, what it never got to. Local Ollama/WASM agents drain the unblocked queue continuously; Claude Code takes what needs frontier reasoning; the human answers questions whenever they like. Nothing waits on anything it does not have to." ;
    pred:open-question "For users who genuinely need wake-when-CLOSED at a chosen time: that is an OS capability, not a browser one. An optional desktop companion (or the existing MCP server) could do it. But for a local-first tool, 'work happens when the app is open' may simply be the honest bargain — and the app is open all day anyway." ;
    pred:decided "INSTALLABLE PWA (Matt, 2026-07-13). Already configured (@vite-pwa/sveltekit, vite.config.ts) — installable, offline-capable, on the home screen. Worth it on its own merits regardless of scheduling." ;
    pred:decided "OPPORTUNISTIC LOCAL-AGENT SCHEDULING + PARALLEL GPUs (Matt, 2026-07-16). The local-model host (a desktop with TWO RTX 3090 Ti) is shut down or suspended in the evenings but UP for several multi-hour windows per day. So: (1) local-agent jobs run a FEW TIMES PER DAY (cadence bumped 24h->8h for visual-regression + local-code-review; 7d->24h for describe-entities) and take the BRUNT of testing/heavy work when the host is up; (2) they must WAIT, never fail, when the host is down — already true (the runner's ollamaUp check marks a local-agent task WAITING when Ollama is unreachable); (3) do NOT force the orchestrator to block on a slow offline job — a quick test (<=1 min) runs INLINE (CI / the run), only heavy/local-model work is queued as offline; (4) TWO GPUs => run MULTIPLE local agents SIMULTANEOUSLY: different-model tasks in parallel (qwen3-coder on one GPU, qwen2.5vl on the other — no reload thrash across separate GPUs), or two same-model workers. The serial model-batching above is the ONE-GPU strategy; parallel-per-GPU is the two-GPU strategy." ;
    pred:has-file "scripts/agent/schedule.ts" ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/rdf> ;
    pred:has-file "src/lib/rdf/agent-task.ts" ;
    pred:remaining "WAVE 0 REMAINING: effects are declared/gated but not an OS capability sandbox; add artifact-write so report/screenshot jobs stop overstating source-write; the browser File System Access drain and shell alignment-sweep cannot participate in host flock; complete the due 19-file standing local review through the runner so the corrected MCP/report proof is tied to a full Git-state receipt; sample and rule on proposal yield before widening local-agent cadence or WIP. The queue still needs an in-app observability/steering surface." ;
    rdfs:label "Local task orchestration & scheduling" ;
    pred:principle "THE QUEUE IS THE CONTRACT; THE RUNNER IS PLUGGABLE. Do not choose between cron and the browser — they optimize different axes. cron gives RELIABLE TIMING on a NARROW set of machines (Unix-only; Windows needs Task Scheduler; it does not exist on phone or tablet; the machine must be awake). A Web Worker gives BROAD REACH with OPPORTUNISTIC timing (every device, one codebase, but only while the app is open). Put the task queue in the GRAPH, and let any runner drain it: the in-app Worker by default (all devices), an optional desktop process for wake-when-closed (driven by cron/launchd/Task Scheduler), the MCP server when an agent is already talking to it. Each runner claims tasks and writes outcomes back. No runner is load-bearing, so none of them failing silently can stop the system — and the queue tells you which ones are actually doing anything." ;
    pred:remaining "WEB RUNTIME STILL UNBUILT. The host runner/task vocabulary now exists, but no Web Worker drains AgentTask entities, no cross-runtime atomic claim store coordinates browser and host, and the queue/outcome/WAITING state is not surfaced in-app. Do not transfer the host flock correctness claim to the browser." ;
    pred:principle "Tiers by reach, not by preference. WASM-in-Worker is the only tier that runs on EVERY device, so it is the default local tier. Ollama is desktop-only (it needs localhost) and is an upgrade, not a requirement. Claude Code is the frontier tier. A task declares the weakest tier that can do it correctly, and the strongest available one takes it." .

kb:ttl-private-side
    pred:description "Offline, local-first, plain text, editable by hand, exportable always (export is a RIGHT — kb:data-egress-model). The user is the sole authority over his own graph and needs no permission from anyone. There is no meaningful provenance here and none is claimed: g is a bookkeeping convenience, not evidence. This is the freedom half of the product, and it must stay uncompromised — the moment we require an account to reason privately, the thesis is dead." ;
    rdfs:label "TTL — the private side (full power to the user)" ;
    pred:part-of kb:ttl-trig-split ;
    pred:decided "EXPORT / MOVE to another machine is plain TTL and requires NO owner/publisher — it is the user's own graph being relocated, making no claim on anybody, so it needs no provenance and no attribution. Only PUBLISHING (kb:trig-published-side) requires that. This keeps the private product frictionless: you can always pick up your graph and carry it elsewhere. (Matt, 2026-07-15.)" ;
    pred:principle "Full power to the user, and no pretence of authority. A private graph makes no claim on anybody." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Concept> .

kb:archive-restore-on-reference
    pred:description "When an ingest mentions an entity that currently lives in the archive, offer to restore it rather than minting a duplicate node. REQUIREMENT, not an enhancement: without it, archiving actively worsens the duplicate problem that the merge analysis exists to solve, and every archive sweep seeds the next round of duplicates." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:proof "VERIFIED END TO END IN THE RUNNING APP, 2026-08-14 — which is why the status moved off scaffolded. The test archives an entity through the REAL archive path (runArchive, so the journal rows are whatever the app actually writes rather than something hand-seeded and possibly wrong), then ingests two facts: one mentioning the archived entity, one unrelated. Result: the detector matched, ONE of the two statements was written, and the restore prompt appeared. The 1-of-2 is the load-bearing observation — it proves the guard holds only the matching fact and lets the rest of the batch through, which is the property that stops a single archived match blocking an entire import." ;
    pred:remaining "Call the storage-backed detector from every ingest commit boundary; pause duplicate-producing writes when matches exist; show a user-confirmed restore-or-keep-separate choice; restore the selected entity and its touching facts into the working graph with a reversible journal event; add end-to-end coverage across all ingest entry points." ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/storage> ;
    pred:depends-on kb:archive-foundation ;
    pred:estimate-basis "BLIND, written 2026-08-14 from the design alone with no code read beforehand — deliberately, because the F75 estimate was written by someone who had already read that code and so proved nothing. Reasoning: the detector already exists (scaffolded), so the work is (a) calling it from every ingest commit boundary, (b) pausing duplicate-producing writes, (c) a restore-or-keep-separate choice in the UI, (d) the restore action with a reversible journal event, (e) end-to-end coverage. STATED RISK, and it is the whole variance: 'every ingest entry point' is unknown to me. If writes funnel through ONE chokepoint this is small and 8 is generous; if ingest paths each commit independently, the wiring multiplies and 8 is badly low. I am guessing there is a chokepoint because a graph app that accepted writes from five places without one would have worse problems than this feature." ;
    pred:honest-note "SCAFFOLDED, not functional: detection now reaches real archive storage, but no ingest path calls it and there is no restore offer or confirmed restore action. Runtime archive moves remain blocked on completing this guard." ;
    pred:has-file "src/lib/rdf/archive.ts" ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/storage/__tests__/archive-store.test.ts" ;
    pred:has-file "src/lib/storage/archive-store.ts" ;
    pred:decided "IT PAUSES RATHER THAN AUTO-RESTORING. The underlying matcher is a conservative direct-IRI and normalized-label comparison — a heuristic, not proof of identity — so auto-restoring on a label collision would silently resurrect something the user deliberately archived. Agents propose, humans settle, applied to restores." ;
    pred:has-status "functional" ;
    pred:proof "Scaffolded 2026-07-28: the archive adapter now resolves a parent's archive by stableId, reads only original archived graph facts (excluding journal and snapshot wrapper rows), and composes that read with the conservative direct-IRI / normalized exact-label matcher. Exact archived identities come from journal affectedEntity rows, so an active neighbour carried into the archive by an inbound edge is not misidentified as archived. Rejected and superseded facts cannot seed restore prompts. The boundary is read-only and creates no database when the parent has no archive registry entry." ;
    pred:progress "WIRED 2026-08-14. The detector and the restore action both already existed and were unreachable because nothing called the detector at a commit boundary; this is that call. src/lib/rdf/archive-guard.ts holds the decision layer (pure, 11 tests) and addStatements now runs it as one more gate beside the currents type gate, the content-safety gate and the F52 agent boundary. Only MATCHING statements are held — a 200-fact import mentioning one archived entity writes 199 and pauses one — and the prompt reuses the existing notification action surface: 'restore it' restores and replays the held facts, DISMISSING writes them as they arrived (keep-separate). Best-effort throughout: any failure writes the batch unchanged, because a guard that blocks an ingest when the archive is unreadable is worse than the duplicate it prevents." ;
    pred:honest-note "ITEM 5 OF THE REMAINING LIST IS NOT DONE: 'add end-to-end coverage across all ingest entry points'. The DECISION layer is unit-tested and the wiring type-checks, but nothing yet proves the guard fires in the running app — which is precisely the trap this session hit twice (indico-verify green while no browser reached the server; toTriG fully covered while broken for its first real caller). Status therefore stays SCAFFOLDED rather than functional until that test exists, even though the code path is complete." ;
    pred:feature-id "F97.3" ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/archive.test.ts" ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/rdf> ;
    pred:known-issue "THE END-TO-END RUN FOUND A UX DEFECT THE UNIT TESTS COULD NOT. The prompt read '\"urn:kbase:concept/old-supplier\" is in the archive' — technically correct and useless to read. Cause: findArchivedReferences sets label to the ENTITY IRI when the archived node has no rdfs:label (archive.ts: `archivedLabels.get(entity) ?? entity`), so label is never absent and a plain fallback-to-tail never fires. The unit tests passed because they supplied either a real label or none at all — never the IRI-as-label case the real detector actually produces. Fixed by treating an IRI-shaped label as no label; 3 regression tests, including a non-urn scheme." ;
    rdfs:label "Restore-on-reference — archived entities are not silently duplicated" ;
    pred:estimated-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:actual-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:estimated-files "8"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:actual-files "3"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:remaining "End-to-end coverage proving the guard fires on a real ingest and that both branches — restore and keep-separate — land correctly. Everything else in this list is now built." .

