kb:subscription-cost-awareness
    pred:relates-to kb:honest-status ;
    pred:description "Matt (2026-07-31): when someone points currents at a LARGE source, they should be considering local agents and scripts rather than expensive API-based services — and the app should warn about heavy token usage if they set it up that way. Subscription is the one place in the product where a single configuration choice commits a user to a RECURRING cost they did not have to type again, so it is the place the warning belongs." ;
    pred:remaining "A size-and-price estimator at subscription setup that states what it cannot yet know; observed change-rate tracking per subscription that replaces the estimate with a measurement; a per-subscription backend override reusing the existing per-task backend plumbing; visible spend-to-date; and an optional ceiling that pauses loudly." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:loop-safety ;
    pred:principle "THIS IS CAPABILITY AWARENESS POINTED THE OTHER WAY, AND THAT IS EXACTLY WHY IT IS TRUSTWORTHY. F117 has to be handled carefully because telling a user they are missing a paid integration is advice we might benefit from — 'an unverifiable claim, made by the party it benefits, is not evidence'. This warning steers a user AWAY from spending, toward the local open-source path, and we earn nothing from it. It is the same capability model with the conflict of interest removed, and it should ship FIRST for that reason: it is the version of the feature nobody has to take on faith." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:local-offload ;
    pred:relates-to kb:source-reference-copy ;
    pred:constraint "A DISMISSED WARNING MUST NOT BECOME A SILENT MONTHLY BILL. kb:loop-safety's failure modes apply directly: a subscription that quietly runs at 3am is worse than none because the user trusts it. So pair the one-time warning with visible per-subscription spend to date, and offer an optional ceiling that PAUSES the subscription and says so rather than continuing past it. Pausing loudly is recoverable; overspending silently is not." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:source-discovery ;
    pred:relates-to kb:capability-awareness ;
    pred:relates-to kb:currents ;
    pred:feature-id "F124" ;
    pred:decided "ESTIMATE COST PER CHANGE, NOT PER POLL — get this wrong and the warning is worse than none. F122's ladder means an unchanged source costs a conditional request and no extraction at all, so multiplying document size by poll frequency would overstate the real figure by orders of magnitude and teach users that our warnings are noise. The honest quantity is (size of the CHANGED REGION) x (observed change rate) x (the configured backend's price), which for a blog that posts weekly is a rounding error and for a large sheet edited hourly is not." ;
    pred:honest-note "PLANNED, NOTHING BUILT (2026-07-31). There is no per-source token accounting, no cost estimate anywhere in the subscription or currents flow, and no per-subscription backend override — settings carry per-TASK backends only. The measurement infrastructure that exists is developer-facing and adjacent rather than reusable as-is: npm run bench:cost (tests/bench/run-cost-bench.ts) and npm run session:tokens (scripts/session-tokens.ts). Prices for cloud backends are external, change without notice, and must be treated as a maintained table that can go stale rather than as a fact." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:decided "OFFER THE FIX IN THE WARNING, NOT JUST THE NUMBER. Settings already carry per-task backends (ingestBackend, analyzeBackend and sub-task overrides in src/lib/storage/db.ts), so the natural remedy is a PER-SUBSCRIPTION backend override: pin this heavy watch to ollama or to a script-tier extractor while the rest of the app keeps using a cloud model. A warning that only says 'this will be expensive' leaves the user to go and find the lever; one that offers the lever costs nothing more to build and is the difference between advice and an obstacle." ;
    pred:has-status "planned" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:work-tiering ;
    pred:decided "MEASURED, NOT ASSERTED — AND THIS ONE ACTUALLY BECOMES MEASURED ON ITS OWN. At setup we can only estimate from the fetched size and a published price, and must SAY that the change rate is still unknown. After a handful of polls the change rate is OBSERVED for that specific source, so the estimate turns into a real number without any benchmark harness — the rare case where F117's measurement discipline is satisfied by simply waiting. Report the observed figure once it exists and stop showing the guess." ;
    rdfs:label "Cost-aware subscriptions — warn before a watch becomes a bill" ;
    pred:principle "THE ESTIMATE IS SCRIPT TIER AND MUST STAY THERE. Bytes, a characters-per-token ratio and a price table produce the number; no model is consulted to tell a user a model would be expensive. Zero tokens, no hallucinated pricing, runs before the first poll — the work-tiering doctrine applied to its own warning." .

kb:environmental-impact
    pred:relates-to kb:mission ;
    pred:constraint "MEASURE WHAT WE CAN, CITE RANGES FOR WHAT WE CANNOT, NEVER PUBLISH A POINT ESTIMATE WE DID NOT MEASURE. We CAN measure first-party: wall-power draw and duration for a real local task on real hardware (we already have bench infrastructure and local models), giving Wh per task with the machine and model named. We CANNOT measure hosted per-query water or energy — nobody publishes it. So: our own numbers are measured and attributed to a specific device; the hosted side is presented as a published RANGE with citations and an explicit statement that it is a third-party estimate, not our measurement." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:principle "THE THESIS IS RIGHT AND THE OBVIOUS MARKETING FOR IT IS NOT. 'Reckons.AI saves N litres of water' is unverifiable by us: per-query water and energy figures for frontier hosted models are not published by their operators, vary by one to two ORDERS OF MAGNITUDE with region, cooling design, grid mix, hour of day and model size, and the widely-quoted numbers derive from a small number of contested 2023 estimates about older models. Publishing a confident figure would be an unverifiable claim, made by the party it benefits — which is the precise failure kb:mission exists to oppose. We would be committing our own thesis's cardinal sin, in our own marketing, about ourselves." ;
    pred:open-question "How much of this belongs on the HOMEPAGE versus a dedicated, heavily-caveated methodology page? A homepage cannot carry the caveats that make the claim honest, and a claim stripped of its caveats becomes the overclaim. Leaning: homepage carries the STRUCTURAL argument (your device, already on, already yours — no numbers), with a linked methodology page holding measurements, ranges, sources and limits." ;
    pred:remaining "Not built. (1) scripts/offline/energy-bench.ts — measure wall power + duration for representative local tasks (ingest, chat, embed) on named hardware; (2) a cited range table for hosted inference with sources and dates; (3) methodology page generated from this graph; (4) homepage copy carrying the structural argument only; (5) legal review before publication." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:aggressive-local-offload ;
    pred:relates-to kb:honest-status ;
    pred:measured "TRAJECTORY, MEASURED 2026-07-19 (Matt: a powerful device is a reach toward more capable models, but as capability packs into fewer parameters and less RAM, phones and smaller devices are enhanced over time). scripts/offline/energy-bench.ts across three model sizes on one machine: llama3.2:3b ~0.68-1.00 Wh per 1k output tokens, mistral-nemo 12.2B ~2.66-3.09, qwen3-coder 30.5B ~1.31. The phone-class 3B is 3-4x more efficient per token than the 12B, and the 30.5B beats the 12.2B because it is mixture-of-experts with far fewer ACTIVE parameters. So the honest framing of the marketing claim is not 'local is greener today' but 'the hardware threshold is falling, and it is falling because ACTIVE parameters are falling faster than total ones'. That is a claim we can measure and re-measure as models ship, rather than one we assert once. CAVEAT: one machine, two short tasks per model, 4.6x spread — directional, not publishable." ;
    pred:decision "LEAD WITH THE ARGUMENT THAT NEEDS NO CONTESTED NUMBERS: MARGINAL vs PROVISIONED capacity. The user's device already exists, is already powered on, and is idle most of the time. Inference on it consumes marginal energy from hardware already manufactured and already running. A hosted query instead draws on capacity that must be BUILT, cooled and kept warm for peak demand. That argument is structural, survives an order-of-magnitude error in any per-query figure, and is honest without a single disputed statistic. The accessibility framing is the same argument in human terms and is independently true: the hardware someone already owns is enough, so capability is not gated behind a subscription or a credit balance." ;
    pred:principle "LOCAL IS NOT ZERO. A laptop running a 7B model draws real power and its manufacture carried real embodied carbon. The honest claim is never 'free', it is a COMPARISON — and a comparison is only valid where the local model can actually do the task. Comparing a 0.5B WASM model against a frontier model on quality-blind 'per query' terms would flatter us by measuring the wrong thing." ;
    pred:constraint "GREEN CLAIMS ARE LEGALLY REGULATED. Environmental marketing is governed by the FTC Green Guides in the US and the EU's green-claims regime, both of which require substantiation for comparative environmental claims. An unsubstantiated 'saves water' headline is not merely a credibility risk, it is a legal exposure — and per SAFETY.md's precedent, overclaiming a benefit we cannot evidence is itself a liability. Legal review before any comparative figure goes on a public page." ;
    pred:description "Quantify and communicate the environmental case for running inference on hardware the user already owns. Two halves, deliberately unequal: a MEASURED half (first-party watt-hours for real local tasks on named hardware, which we can substantiate) and a CITED half (published ranges for hosted inference, which we cannot measure and must not present as our own). The public claim leads with the structural argument — a device already manufactured and already powered draws marginal energy, while a hosted query draws on capacity that must be built and cooled — because that argument survives an order-of-magnitude error in any disputed per-query figure. The accessibility framing is the same point in human terms: the Mac someone already owns is enough to help with the shopping or to start a business, with no subscription and no credit balance in the way." ;
    pred:has-status "planned" ;
    pred:feature-id "F105" ;
    rdfs:label "Environmental impact of local-first inference" ;
    pred:honest-note "Nothing is measured yet. Until scripts measure real Wh on real hardware, this feature has NO quantitative claim to make, and the honest position is to say the qualitative thing only. Also worth stating in the copy rather than hiding: Reckons.AI itself calls hosted models when the user chooses one, and this repo's own development runs on a frontier model — a local-first tool built with cloud compute. Claiming otherwise would be false, and admitting it costs nothing next to being caught." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:local-offload .

