    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/threed> ;
    pred:open-question "What arranges the members — the SET's declared order (F113 roles: design, observed, archive), the leap density between them, or the user dragging them? Density is tempting and probably wrong: a layout that moves when facts change makes spatial memory useless, which is the main thing a separated view buys." ;
    pred:touches-file "src/lib/3d/KnowledgeGraph2D.svelte" ;
    pred:evidence "THE PARTS EXIST AND DO NOT DO THIS. src/lib/rdf/multi-graph-parse.ts already models several graphs at once (GraphDef, OverlayData, GRAPH_COLORS) and the overlay route already loads a selection of them — but it renders a VENN-STYLE OVERLAY, merging them into one shared space to show intersection. Separation is the opposite arrangement and nothing does it: verified 2026-08-14, no offset or per-graph positioning exists anywhere in multi-graph-parse.ts or the overlay components." ;
    pred:estimated-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:description "Matt, 2026-08-14: 'as I consider many leaps between nodes of PAIRED graphs — such as Product Design and Code graphs — I see the need to make leaps easier by being able to open graph SETS. The graphs should be displayed the same way, but separated by distance within the plane of the viewport. Leaps could then avoid any reload, if both (or multiple) graphs are already preloaded.' Open a set, render every member in one scene at its own position, and let a leap be a camera traversal rather than a graph swap." ;
    pred:feature-id "F131" ;
    pred:touches-file "src/lib/3d/KnowledgeGraph.svelte" ;
    pred:constraint "AT MOST THREE ACTIVE GRAPHS IN A SET BROWSE (Matt, 2026-08-14). A hard cap, not a guideline: each member carries its own force simulation and the 3D view already warns at 19fps on ONE starter graph, so a fourth is where the view stops being usable rather than where it starts being crowded. It also bounds the arrangement problem — three regions can be laid out legibly in a plane without a layout algorithm nobody asked for." ;
    pred:priority "high" ;
    pred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/multi-graph-parse.ts" ;
    pred:open-question "How many graphs before this stops helping? Each member carries its own force simulation, and the 3D view already warns at 19fps on a single starter graph. A set of six may need members to render statically until focused." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:multi-kb ;
    pred:remaining "Per-graph origin offsets in the layout so members occupy distinct regions; a set-aware loader that preloads every member of a graph set; leap edges drawn ACROSS the gap and traversed by camera rather than by switchToKb; and a decision on arrangement before any of it, since the answer changes the data the layout needs." ;
    pred:evidence "A LEAP CURRENTLY COSTS A SWAP. kb-leap.ts resolves the target by stable id and the caller switches the active graph, with switchToKb as an explicit reload fallback in the leap handler. If both graphs are already in the scene there is nothing to switch — the leap becomes movement, which is what makes a densely cross-linked pair (design <-> code) usable rather than a sequence of context losses." ;
    pred:principle "SEPARATION AND OVERLAY ANSWER DIFFERENT QUESTIONS, so this does not replace the overlay. Overlay asks WHAT DO THESE GRAPHS SHARE and needs one coordinate space to show it. Separation asks HOW DO THESE GRAPHS RELATE and needs distinct spaces so a leap edge is visibly a crossing rather than an ordinary edge. Building separation as a mode of the overlay would blur the one thing each is for." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/rdf> ;
    pred:relates-to kb:graph-sets .

kb:camera-angle-control
    rdfs:label "Aimable 3D camera (?cam=) so visual tests can see depth" ;
    pred:description "Matt (2026-08-13): 'can we control the angle of the viewport in our visual tests? currently our forces are flat even in 3D, but we may want more 3d forces later.' The camera is now aimable via ?cam=iso|top|side|front or an explicit azimuth,elevation[,distance] triple, matching the existing ?layout= / ?sel= / ?types= convention the visual tests already drive the app with." ;
    pred:part-of kb:spatial-force-layout ;
    pred:remaining "BUILT: src/lib/3d/camera-presets.ts (parseCameraSpec, cameraPosition, revealsDepth), 15 tests, ?cam= parsed alongside the existing view params and passed to the 3D renderer. revealsDepth() exists so a 3D visual test can ASSERT its camera is not blind to z. NOT DONE: no visual test yet uses an angled camera — the capability exists and the suite has not been extended to exercise it; and elevation is clamped short of the poles rather than supporting a true top-down orbit." ;
    pred:evidence "THE FORCES REALLY ARE FLAT, MEASURED: of the six 3D layout builders, type, hub and timeline place EVERY anchor at z=0; only focus (3), hierarchy (2) and source (1) use the third axis at all. The camera was a hardcoded literal — position={[0, 0, 18]} — looking straight down the z axis, with OrbitControls that only respond to a real pointer." ;
    pred:actual-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:actual-files "4"^^xsd:integer ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:has-status "functional" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:deep-testing ;
    pred:principle "A HEAD-ON CAMERA OVER A FLAT LAYOUT MEANS THE 3D RENDERER WAS UNTESTED AS 3D. Every z value projects to the same screen point from that angle, so a regression that flattened the remaining depth would have passed every visual check identically. Aiming the camera is therefore a PREREQUISITE for adding genuinely 3D forces, not a convenience for the ones that exist: a force that uses depth cannot be regression-tested from an angle that cannot see depth." ;
    pred:decided "SPHERICAL, AND ABSENT MEANS UNCHANGED. Azimuth/elevation in degrees survive a URL and read clearly in a test name, where a coordinate triple does not. The front preset reproduces the historical [0, 0, 18] exactly and is the default when ?cam= is absent, so every existing screenshot baseline is unaffected. A malformed value returns null rather than a default — a typo that quietly moved the camera would produce a baseline captured from an unintended angle, which is broken but still passing." ;
    pred:feature-id "F59.3" ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/threed> ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/3d/__tests__/camera-presets.test.ts" ;
    pred:evidence "PROVEN IN THE RUNNING APP, and the proof is the useful part. Shot against the real starter graph: ?layout=focus&cam=iso renders a visibly different three-quarter view from cam=front, and ?layout=timeline&cam=side COLLAPSES THE WHOLE LAYOUT INTO A SINGLE VERTICAL LINE — every node at z=0 seen edge-on, with all three date ticks stacked in one column. Flatness is now a visible, screenshot-testable property instead of an invisible one." .

