kb:rdf-vocabulary-hygiene
    pred:has-status "in-progress" ;
    pred:open-question "urn:kbase: is not a registered URN namespace (RFC 8141 requires a registered NID). It is a syntactically valid IRI so Turtle accepts it, but it is neither conformant nor dereferenceable. Migrate to https://reckons.ai/kb/ — worth the churn?" ;
    pred:part-of kb:rdf-dataset-migration ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:honest-note "in-progress means DETECTED, not FIXED (2026-07-12). graph-lint now catches every defect class below, but ZERO of the defects are repaired: the 3 duplicate feature-ids, 6 dangling refs, 4 dead has-file links, the kb:design-system IRI collision, the prefix aliasing, the untyped numerics, and urn:reckons:leap all still stand. The lint fails on them today, by design. (kb:honest-status)" ;
    pred:feature-id "F75.1" ;
    rdfs:label "RDF vocabulary hygiene" ;
    pred:description "Defects found by the 2026-07-12 spec audit. Turtle CONFORMANCE is fine (all 19 graphs parse under N3, the reference parser; 353 RDF tests green including a W3C suite) — the problems are semantic. (a) kb:design-system is one IRI naming two different things: a ktype:Concept (fonts/colors/components) and a ktype:Feature (the shadcn migration), with two labels and two conflicting statuses, so RDF merges them into one contradictory entity. (b) urn:kbase:predicate/ is bound to THREE prefix names (kpred:, pred:, p:) and urn:kbase:meta/ to two, and the app's DEFAULT_PREFIXES exports pred:/meta: while every hand-authored TTL uses kpred:/kmeta:. (c) 15 cardinality violations — single-valued predicates repeated on one subject. (d) kpred:order, kpred:pace, kpred:test-count are untyped strings where 95 other integers are xsd:integer, so they sort lexically ('10' < '9'). (e) urn:reckons:leap is used as a predicate but has no / or # separator, so it cannot be prefix-shortened and defeats the PREDICATE_PREFIX convention that decides whether a predicate renders as a graph edge. Each defect class gets a graph-lint check so it cannot recur." .

