kb:source-reference-copy
    pred:constraint "SIZE CAP WITH AN HONEST DEGRADE. A large sheet, a long page or a big repo will exceed any sensible per-source cap, and the fallback is to keep the hash alone. That fallback must SAY what it costs: with only a hash you know THAT the source changed and not WHAT changed, so the refresh has to re-extract wholesale and the phantom-churn problem returns for that source. Reporting 'changed, contents not retained' is correct; silently behaving as though the diff were precise is not." ;
    pred:constraint "WRITING THE TIMESTAMP MUST NOT COST MORE THAN THE POLL IT SAVES. Auto-sync pushes on every graph mutation, so a naive last-polled-at written on each poll turns ten subscriptions on a 45s interval into a graph write and a sync push every few seconds — trading a duplicate fetch for continuous sync churn, which is worse. Because the value is advisory, precision is not needed: write it coarsely (rounded to the interval, or at most every few minutes), or advance it only alongside a change that was going to be synced anyway. Store it as a graph-native meta statement on the subscription, following the currents.ts pattern, so it travels with the existing sync rather than needing a channel of its own." ;
    pred:decided "THE REFERENCE IS THE LAST REVIEWED COPY, NOT THE LAST FETCHED ONE (Matt's wording: 'the old already reviewed source'). The distinction is load-bearing. If the baseline advanced on every fetch, a change that arrived and was never reviewed would silently become the new baseline and vanish from the queue — the graph would forget a fact it had never been shown. Advancing the reference copy only when the review is settled makes unreviewed changes ACCUMULATE into the next diff instead of disappearing, which is the same discipline as diffing against the last observation rather than against the graph (F121)." ;
    pred:decided "RETAIN AND DIFF THE NORMALIZED EXTRACTION, NOT THE RAW HTML — a correction to the obvious first instinct (2026-07-31). A saved HTML page diffs terribly: navigation, ads, analytics tags, CSRF and session tokens, 'posted 3 days ago' relative dates and server-side A/B markup all change between two fetches of an IDENTICAL article. Diffing raw HTML would therefore report a change on almost every poll and hand the extractor a page that has not meaningfully moved — reintroducing exactly the phantom churn this feature exists to remove. The reference copy is the reader-mode/markdown extraction (Firecrawl or Jina, already integrated), which is also what the extractor actually consumed, so passage grounding re-verifies against the same text the quote came from. Keep the raw bytes only where fidelity is the point, and never as the diff baseline." ;
    pred:has-status "planned" ;
    pred:estimated-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:evidence "LINK ROT DOES NOT BREAK THE LAST-KNOWN-ONLY RULE, which is worth recording so it is not re-litigated. If an origin disappears, the retained copy is the last version — exactly the one the current facts were derived from — and quotes grounded in EARLIER versions survive independently, because the excerpt text itself lives in the statement and in the archive journal. Keeping old document versions would add nothing that is not already held more compactly." ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/source-cache.test.ts" ;
    pred:principle "THE FREE CHECK GATES THE EXPENSIVE ONE — this is the work-tiering doctrine applied to ingest instead of to our own tooling. A byte or hash comparison is script tier: zero tokens, no hallucination, right by construction. It decides whether the agent tier runs at all, and on a changed document it narrows the extractor's input to the changed region rather than the whole text. An automatic poll over a rarely-changing source should cost nothing on the vast majority of its runs." ;
    pred:principle "THE TIMESTAMP IS ADVISORY AND MUST NEVER BE DEPENDED ON, for two reasons that both bite in a local-first system with no authoritative clock. CLOCK SKEW: a device running fast writes a future timestamp and a device running slow may never think enough time has passed, so the comparison needs a generous margin and must not be the only thing keeping a subscription alive. SYNC LAG: the timestamp only helps once it has PROPAGATED, and with a 45s Drive pull two devices polling seconds apart will not see each other at all. So this reduces steady-state duplication; it does not prevent races, and the UI must not claim it does." ;
    pred:evidence "IT ALSO REPAIRS PASSAGE GROUNDING, which cannot currently be re-checked. Statement.excerptVerified is documented in src/lib/rdf/types.ts as 'undefined = not checked (NO SOURCE TEXT)' — the document is discarded after extraction, so a quote can only be verified at ingest and never again. Retaining the reviewed copy makes grounding durably re-verifiable, and that matters beyond convenience: a claim whose evidence has been thrown away is in the same position as an unverifiable one, which is precisely what this product exists to refuse (kb:thesis)." ;
    pred:decided "SYNCING IS NOT PUBLISHING, AND THE CACHE MUST NOT TRAVEL IN A SHARED GRAPH PACKAGE. Copying retained third-party documents to the user's OWN cloud is ordinary personal use; embedding them in a graph package that is exported, shared by link or published to a site is REDISTRIBUTION of somebody else's content, and it would happen invisibly to a user who only meant to share their facts. sources/ is therefore inside the sync boundary and outside the publish boundary (kb:publish-safety-gate), and the packager must exclude it by construction rather than by remembering to." ;
    pred:decided "THE GRAPH GETS A POINTER AND A HASH, NEVER THE DOCUMENT. Whole documents as literals bloat every export, every sync payload and the 3D view, and they make a graph package unshareable for reasons that have nothing to do with its facts. Storage goes in the workspace directory the user has already granted — the showDirectoryPicker handle in src/lib/stores/workspace.svelte.ts, persisted in IndexedDB — under a sources/ subdirectory, falling back to OPFS when no workspace is linked." ;
    pred:progress "PHASE 1 BUILT 2026-07-31; PHASES 2-4 NOT. Retention and the change gate exist and are tested; NOTHING CALLS THEM YET, so no refresh path is any cheaper today than it was. src/lib/rdf/source-cache.ts holds the pure policy (SHA-256 hash, size cap, change classification, honest degrade) and src/lib/stores/workspace.svelte.ts holds the IO (sources/ directory, one slot per source, content-policy gate, corrupt-file warning). 28 tests. The wiring into source-refresh.ts — the part that actually saves an extraction — is phase 2." ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 1 — DONE (2026-07-31), except the OPFS fallback for users with no linked workspace." ;
    pred:estimated-files "6"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:constraint "A DOCUMENT THE SAFETY CLASSIFIER BLOCKED MUST NOT BE QUIETLY CACHED TO DISK. src/lib/safety/content-policy.ts filters blocked content out of addStatements today; writing the raw source to the user's folder is a NEW retention path that the existing filter does not cover, and it would persist exactly the material the filter exists to keep out. The cache write has to sit behind the same classification, and refusing to retain is a legitimate outcome that falls back to hash-only." ;
    pred:constraint "THE USER BOUNDS THE COUNT, NOT THE SIZE — so the per-source cap above is what actually holds the total. 'Only what I chose to watch' bounds how MANY sources exist; it says nothing about a sheet with fifty thousand rows or a long-form site, and every one of those bytes now crosses sync rather than sitting on one disk. The cap and its honest hash-only degrade are therefore load-bearing for this decision, not a nicety attached to it." ;
    pred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/source-cache.ts" ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/stores/__tests__/workspace-source-cache.test.ts" ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 3 — re-verify passage grounding against the retained copy, so excerptVerified stops being a one-shot ingest-time check." ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 2 — gate refresh on the document diff: unchanged hash short-circuits with zero extraction; changed document produces a text diff whose changed regions are the only input handed to the extractor. Advance the reference copy only when the resulting review is settled." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:currents ;
    pred:relates-to kb:passage-grounding ;
    pred:principle "HISTORY IS FOR WHAT CANNOT BE RE-DERIVED — this is the rule the asymmetry follows from, and it is the same trade Matt already made in the other direction for the archive. A rejected fact, a merge decision, a review judgement: gone forever if not kept, so src/lib/rdf/archive.ts keeps FULL pre-operation snapshots and accepts the storage (2026-07-18, with retention as the bound). A source document is a DIFF BASELINE and is re-fetchable from its origin, so keeping every version buys nothing but bytes. Same question, opposite answer, because the artifacts differ in kind rather than in size." ;
    pred:principle "THE STRONGEST ARGUMENT IS NOT COST, IT IS THAT LLM EXTRACTION IS NON-DETERMINISTIC. Re-extracting an UNCHANGED document produces slightly different triples every run — different phrasing, different granularity, an entity split one way this time and another way last time — and the triple-level diff faithfully reports all of it as change. That is phantom churn: the document did not move, the model did, and the human pays for it in triage. Diffing the document first removes the entire false-positive class, because an unchanged document never reaches the extractor at all." ;
    pred:done "PREREQUISITE FIXED FIRST (2026-07-31): a rejected fact used to come back as NEWS on every re-read. indexExisting() in src/lib/rdf/diff.ts dropped rejected and superseded statements entirely, so a re-read of an unchanged source classified an already-dismissed triple as 'new'. Settled statements are now indexed separately and surface as a 'returned' DiffEntry carrying the prior status, and ingest DOES NOT PERSIST them — the review card acts on the statement already in the graph, so 'accept after all' revives the existing row instead of creating a second one. Classification alone was not enough and the first commit was incomplete: it labelled the loop without stopping the pile-up. The two tests that asserted the old behaviour had encoded the bug, which is why it survived." ;
    pred:decided "A LADDER OF GATES, CHEAPEST FIRST, EACH ONE ABLE TO END THE REFRESH. (1) HTTP CONDITIONAL REQUEST — send If-None-Match / If-Modified-Since and a 304 ends it having downloaded nothing; this is a free server-side diff and there is currently NO ETag or conditional-request handling anywhere in src/lib. (2) FEED ITEM IDS where a feed exists (F123), so 'anything new?' is answered without fetching articles. (3) CONTENT HASH of the normalized extraction — unchanged ends it. (4) TEXT OR KEYED DIFF, handing the extractor only the changed regions or genuinely new items. (5) TRIPLE DIFF into pending facts, which is where the existing pipeline already starts today. Steps 1-4 are all script tier; the LLM is reached only when something actually changed." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:touches-file "src/lib/stores/source-refresh.ts" ;
    pred:description "Matt (2026-07-31), for currents AND tables: keep the SOURCE DOCUMENT in a local directory rather than representing the whole thing in the graph. On refresh, diff the newly fetched document against the retained reviewed copy FIRST, and only offer pending triples for what actually changed. The graph holds facts and a pointer; the directory holds the bytes." ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/stores> ;
    rdfs:label "Reviewed source copies — diff the document, then extract only what changed" ;
    pred:decided "SOURCES SYNC, AND THEY KEEP ONLY THE LAST KNOWN VERSION (Matt, 2026-07-31 — settles the open question this entry was filed with). Currents does add real weight to sync and local storage, but the set is user-configured and limited to what someone actively chose to watch, so it is bounded rather than extreme. No history in the cache: one slot per source, overwritten. The GRAPH carries full history and stays revertable, and it is far more compact than the documents it was derived from." ;
    pred:priority "high" ;
    pred:principle "A DERIVED ARTIFACT CAN TOLERATE LAST-WRITER-WINS; A HISTORICAL RECORD CANNOT. Two devices advancing one baseline slot will collide, and the correct resolution is simply to let the later write stand — worst case the loser re-fetches and re-diffs, which costs a request. That is why the single-slot design is safe to sync at all, and it is precisely what could NOT be said of the archive journal. The existing content-hash loop guard in drive-sync.svelte.ts already covers the echo case." ;
    pred:decided "LIMIT THE POLL WITH A SYNCED LAST-POLLED-AT TIMESTAMP, NOT A LEASE (Matt, 2026-07-31). Where sync is set up, another device can see when the last poll happened and skip its own. Chosen over the F87 claim/lease shape deliberately: a lease can get STUCK — the holder goes offline mid-hold and the subscription stops being polled by anyone until it expires — whereas a stale timestamp degrades in the safe direction, because any device seeing an old one simply polls. Nothing to release, nothing to reap, and a device that disappears is silently covered by the next one." ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/rdf> ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 4 — extend to the keyed row diff for tables (F121), where the reference copy is the last reviewed row set and the comparison is by derived row key rather than by text." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:content-safety ;
    pred:touches-file "src/lib/stores/workspace.svelte.ts" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:graph-sets ;
    pred:decided "SHOW LAST-POLLED AND LAST-CHANGED, NEVER A PROMISED NEXT RUN. The same value that coordinates devices is the one the user needs to see, and kb:loop-safety already records why the framing matters: a cron once fired on schedule, produced nothing, reported nothing, and still displayed a confident future run time. 'Last checked 3 minutes ago, last changed 6 days ago' is a report; 'next check in 42s' is a promise the app cannot keep once a tab is closed or a device is asleep." ;
    pred:evidence "THE REPOSITORY KIND ALREADY WORKS THIS WAY, which is the argument that this generalizes rather than invents. Source.repoHeadSha plus the GitHub Compare API gives a document-level delta for free, so a repo refresh already extracts only from changed files. url and table sources get no delta from the server, so the reference copy has to be local — same architecture, self-hosted. Source.hash also already exists ('optional checksum of source content for change detection'), so the intent is in the type today with nothing to compare against beyond the hash." ;
    pred:honest-note "OPFS FALLBACK NOT BUILT (2026-07-31) — with no workspace connected there is no retention at all, and every refresh reports 'no-baseline' and behaves exactly as it does today. That is a real gap for anyone who has not linked a folder, not a graceful equivalent. Source in src/lib/rdf/types.ts still has no content field and the sources Dexie table stores none; the baseline lives beside the graph rather than on the Source record." ;
