kb:agent-context-dogfooding
    pred:feature-id "F68" ;
    rdfs:label "Coding-agent context dogfooding" ;
    pred:measured "CORRECTED 2026-07-12 — the 60-70% figure conflated two different things and overstated the format. MEASURED (mcp-server/src/__tests__/compress.test.ts): the compression FORMAT saves ~18% vs grouped Turtle (what a real .ttl looks like) and ~29% vs flat one-triple-per-line Turtle — NOT 60-70%. The large saving comes from SUBGRAPH SELECTION, not the encoding: a compressed subgraph feeds ~6x fewer input tokens than the raw graph (~50x cheaper per query than the whole ~116k-token graph raw) at EQUAL-or-BETTER grounding (raw scored 60% fact-coverage vs 80% compressed — needle-in-haystack). Grounded generation: compressed-context 79% cheaper; local-draft + Opus-review 77% cheaper on long outputs, same quality." ;
    pred:description "Uses compressed, pre-reviewed subgraphs to speed up coding-agent sessions. The saving comes overwhelmingly from SELECTING a relevant subgraph rather than from the compact encoding (the format itself is worth ~18% vs grouped Turtle — measured, see kb:context-compression). Rather than re-reading the code and re-deriving the plan each session, an agent loads a dense, cross-linked graph carrying status, dependencies, tests, and decisions per feature — 80-90% fewer context-establishment tokens, and 77% fewer output tokens when drafting is delegated locally. Compressed subgraphs ground equal-or-better than the raw graph while costing far less, for cloud and offline alike." ;
    pred:principle "The graph is condensed context tracking for coding agents: instead of re-reading the codebase and re-deriving the plan each session, an agent loads a dense, cross-linked, pre-reviewed subgraph. Every feature captured here (status, deps, tests, decisions) is reusable instruction for the next session — cloud OR local/offline." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:local-offload ;
    pred:honest-note "The 2026-07-19 evidence has three limits that belong beside it. (1) The ~93K token figure sums CITED ESTIMATES of builds that did not happen — unfalsifiable by construction, which is why each ledger entry names the entity that prevented it so the reasoning can be audited even when the number cannot. (2) One feature-dense session is not a rate. (3) The graph only did half the work: it supplied the PLAN (decided/planned/forbidden) while grep and file reading supplied the CODE — it could say F65 already specified set-grouping, but not that buildFocusAnchors() computes a hop map and discards it. Also: kb_stats reports ~3.9K triples over 6 KBs from mcp-workspace while the full static/ corpus is ~7.3K quads over 23 graphs, so the MCP server sees roughly HALF the corpus and 'not in the graph' was never safely concluded from kb_search alone." ;
    pred:has-status "functional" ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:estimate "For coding-agent assistance (this build session as the sample): establishing task context via a compressed feature-area subgraph (~5-8k tokens) vs re-exploring code + re-deriving the plan (~30-60k tokens/feature) is a ~80-90% cut in context-establishment INPUT tokens per feature; output tokens drop ~77% when drafts are delegated to local models (Opus orchestrates + reviews). Compounding: this session added ~20 cross-linked feature entities — dense instruction a future/offline agent loads instead of re-deriving. CAVEAT: directional estimate; the reckons MCP was not connected this session, so the realized win was plan-grounding via the TTL graphs directly (no feature re-derivation, consistent status/deps), not the full kb_compress pipeline." ;
    pred:honest-note "No automated test covers this feature (audited 2026-07-12, scripts/offline/status-evidence.ts). The status is still accurate — it IS shipped and in use; test coverage and deployment maturity are orthogonal, and pretending otherwise would be its own dishonesty. This is a declared RISK, not a hidden one: kb:honest-status requires the gap be visible rather than silent." ;
    pred:test-coverage "none" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:context-compression ;
    pred:evidence "FIRST CITED EVIDENCE, 2026-07-19 — previously this feature rested only on the directional kpred:estimate below, taken in a session where the MCP was not even connected. In one session five requests arrived framed as new work and all five were already decided: F65+F96+graph-legibility (node summarization), loop-job-grouping (review set grouping), hierarchy-nav+KBLeap (HNSW sub-graph jumps), routeQuestion (ingest routing), loop-subagent-graphs (council merge). Twenty MCP calls, the pattern being kb_search to find the name then kb_get_entity to read the ruling — the decisive text (a corrected measurement, a principle forbidding an approach) only ever appeared on the full entity, never in the search fragment. NO prior-session conversation history was read or available; the continuity came entirely from the graph. Recorded in reckons-workspace/graph-economics.jsonl and documented for users at cw:CaseStudyOneSession." .

