    pred:has-status "planned" ;
    pred:principle "THE SCENE IS A GRAPH, NOT A SCRIPT. A generated .blend or .py is a derived artifact; the graph is the source. Otherwise the asset pipeline becomes exactly the thing this product exists to replace — a binary blob whose reasoning is lost." ;
    pred:principle "A GREEN EXIT CODE IS NOT A GOOD IMAGE. Blender will happily render a black frame and exit 0. Any `done-when` that only checks the process succeeded is a lie the loop will tell itself at scale." ;
    pred:description "Headless Blender (`blender --background --python`) exposed as an MCP tool, so an agent can generate, render and inspect 3D content the same way it queries the graph. The scene is DESCRIBED IN THE GRAPH — objects, materials, camera, lights as entities — and the Blender script is GENERATED from it. That means an asset has provenance: you can ask why a thing is the colour it is, diff two scenes, and regenerate one from a graph rather than from a .blend nobody can read." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:verifiability-axis ;
    pred:has-phase kb:visual-acceptance ;
    pred:depends-on kb:verifiability-axis ;
    pred:depends-on kb:agent-orchestration .

kb:provenance-model
    pred:principle "A named graph on a single-source KB is ceremony carrying zero information. Ceremony that conveys nothing is cost without benefit." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Concept> ;
    pred:part-of kb:rdf-dataset-migration ;
    pred:description "A named graph carries information only when a KB has multiple sources. SINGLE-SOURCE graphs (the hand-authored static/*.ttl docs KBs — their one source IS the file) gain nothing from a graph term: default-graph Turtle is the correct model, not a compromise. MULTI-SOURCE graphs (an ingested user KB mixing three PDFs, a repo, two URLs, and the user's own typing) make g load-bearing, and dropping it destroys real information. Hence: TriG in, Turtle out, .trig only on genuine multi-source." ;
    rdfs:label "Provenance model (when a named graph earns its keep)" ;
    pred:principle "The source of manual input is the USER — the highest-trust source in the system — not 'nothing'. Human-asserted vs machine-extracted is the provenance distinction that actually pays rent." .

kb:scope-creep
    pred:remaining "BUILT: src/lib/rdf/scope.ts (measureCreep, creepSeries, creepShape, shouldWarnOnCreep, describeCreep), 20 tests including this session's own F129 attribution work as a worked gradual-creep example. NOT BUILT — and this is the whole point of the feature, so it is the honest gap: NO DESIGN CARRIES AN ESTIMATE YET. Nothing in the roadmap states expected files or dependencies, no task records observations during implementation, and kb-align still docks every unmatched change equally. Until an estimate exists on a real task this measures nothing." ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/rdf> ;
    pred:decided "A DRIFT WARNING HERE MEANS THE DESIGN WAS NOT REFINED ENOUGH — Matt's framing, and it settles what the signal is FOR: 'drift warnings are a soft, non-blocking warning system that the initial graph was not refined enough. The design didn't account for everything.' Creep is evidence about the DESIGN, never about the implementer, so describeCreep is phrased that way and is tested to contain no blame language." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:agent-orchestration ;
    pred:decided "AN ESTIMATE IS NEVER BACK-FILLED ONTO FINISHED WORK. estimated-* and actual-* are separate predicates precisely so the two cannot be confused: an 'estimate' written after the fact is not a prediction, and scoring one would make every calibration figure a lie. scope-check --calibration therefore reports that estimate quality is UNKNOWN — zero designs carry both — and says why, rather than manufacturing a flattering number from six retrofitted guesses." ;
    pred:feature-id "F130.1" ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/scope.test.ts" ;
    pred:known-issue "THE IN-FLIGHT CHECK OVER-COUNTS ON A MIXED BRANCH, and the first real run proved it: against a branch carrying five unrelated concerns it reported 39 source files against every estimate, so F75's 5-file estimate appeared to be over by 34. git diff cannot attribute a file to a feature. The report prints this caveat rather than hiding it, and the real remedy is one branch per feature — which this working tree is currently a counter-example to." ;
    pred:priority "high" ;
    pred:evidence "SECOND SCORED ESTIMATE, AND THE FIRST BLIND ONE (F97.3, 2026-08-14). Predicted 8 files from the design alone with no code read beforehand; actual 3. The estimate recorded its own risk in advance — 'if writes funnel through ONE chokepoint this is small and 8 is generous; if ingest paths each commit independently, the wiring multiplies' — and the chokepoint existed: addStatements is reached by 32 call sites, so one hook covered every ingest path. The bet was right in direction, which is why the number was high. TWO DATA POINTS NOW, BOTH UNDER: F75 predicted 5 actual 3, F97.3 predicted 8 actual 3. Too few to call a bias, and worth watching for whether estimates here run consistently generous — an estimator that is reliably 2x high is as unusable as one that is reliably low." ;
    pred:constraint "NOTHING HERE BLOCKS, and that is load-bearing rather than lenient. Blocking on creep would make the honest estimate the dangerous one and push every estimate upward until it meant nothing. A tolerance exists for the same reason: a design that said 'about three files' and cost four has revealed nothing, and warning on it would train people to ignore the signal." ;
    pred:progress "ESTIMATES NOW EXIST ON REAL DESIGNS (2026-08-14). Three forward estimates were written BEFORE their work, chosen from the roadmap-readiness READY TO BUILD list: F122 reviewed source copies (6 files, 0 deps), F123 source discovery (7, 0), F75 RDF dataset capability (5, 0). Six ACTUALS were recorded for work this session finished — F126 3 files, F127 4, F128 3 files and 2 deps, F129 5, F59.2 4, F59.3 4 — as calibration input for future estimates." ;
    pred:known-issue "THE ACTUAL COUNT IS UNRELIABLE ON A DIRTY TREE, MEASURED WHILE SCORING F75. Two methods, both wrong in opposite directions. Diffing against a BRANCH POINT reported 42 source files because the branch carried five unrelated concerns. Diffing the CHANGED-FILE LIST before and after reported 1, because two of the three files F75 touched — kb/+page.svelte and rdf/serialize.ts — were ALREADY dirty from earlier work and so never appeared as new. The true figure, 3, was known only because the same session did the work and could count it by hand. NEITHER AUTOMATED METHOD SURVIVES A MIXED BRANCH, which turns one-branch-per-feature from hygiene into the precondition for measuring anything at all." ;
    pred:remaining "BUILT: the estimate vocabulary (kpred:estimated-files / estimated-deps / actual-files / actual-deps) validated by SHACL as non-negative integers; scripts/offline/scope-check.ts in both calibration and in-flight modes, registered in the offline suite, never failing a build. NOT BUILT: no task records OBSERVATIONS DURING implementation, so creepSeries and the gradual-vs-step distinction are still unexercised by real data — the series needs a harness taking readings as it works, which is F87. And estimate quality stays unknown until one of the three estimated features is actually built." ;
    pred:remaining "Also needed: measure the actual from a COMMIT RANGE attributable to the feature rather than from working-tree state, which requires the branch discipline the tracking has so far only argued for." ;
    pred:part-of kb:finding-taxonomy ;
    pred:evidence "THIS IS WHAT AGENT ORCHESTRATION NEEDS IN FLIGHT (F87: 'a task is a triple ... schedules, dependencies, tiers, prompts and outcomes live in the graph'). An estimate is one more fact on a task, and creep is the running signal that a harness is diverging from it. It is the explanatory half of what plasma-ai/fractal bounds with hard caps on iterations, depth, children, cost and wall-clock: a cap STOPS the work, creep EXPLAINS it by naming the dependency or file count the design failed to predict." ;
    pred:principle "SILENCE IS NOT A CLAIM OF ZERO. An estimate that never mentioned dependencies is not the assertion that there would be none, so unmentioned dimensions are skipped rather than scored — otherwise partial estimates are penalised out of existence and nobody writes one." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:loop-safety ;
    pred:has-status "functional" ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:description "Matt, 2026-08-14, correcting a first model that treated scope as a CATEGORY: 'scope should be the estimated work required for the implementation of the design. going over scope may be an unexpected dependency, or touching more files than expected. scope is the expectation, and scope creep is the gradual growing of that expectation, through deeper discovery or during implementation.' Scope is therefore a QUANTITY on a design, and creep is a SERIES." ;
    pred:decided "CREEP IS A SERIES, NOT A VERDICT, because 'gradual' cannot be expressed by comparing once at the end. Observations are recorded during implementation, each carrying what was DISCOVERED there, and the shape of the series is the actionable part: growth across several readings means the design was systematically under-refined and wants revisiting whole, while a single jump means one specific thing was missed and naming it is the entire fix." ;
    rdfs:label "Scope as an estimate, and creep as its gradual growth" .

kb:vocabulary-grounding
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/vocabulary-context.test.ts" ;
