    pred:touches-file "src/lib/integrations/llm/extractor.ts" ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:has-status "planned" ;
    pred:open-question "How many predicates fit, and which ones? A mature graph holds 333 predicate types (graph-lint, 2026-08-15) and that will not fit in a small model's prompt. Selecting by embedding similarity to the source text is the obvious route and reuses kb:context-compression, but it is unmeasured — and picking the wrong subset grounds the model in the wrong vocabulary, which is worse than no grounding." ;
    pred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/normalize-entities.ts" ;
    pred:constraint "GROUNDING MUST NOT BECOME A CAGE. A prompt that lists existing predicates and demands the model choose one would suppress genuinely new relations — and a well-formed absence is the most valuable node in the graph (kb:thesis). The list has to be offered as preference, not enumerated as law, and minting a new predicate must stay available and visible in review." ;
    pred:description "Put the graph's EXISTING predicates into the extraction prompt so a local model reaches for a word the graph already uses instead of minting a synonym. Today extraction is vocabulary-blind: the model sees the source text and a schema, never the graph it is writing into, and the cleanup happens afterwards by cosine similarity — a repair job for a problem that could have been avoided at the point of asking." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:predicate-manager ;
    pred:known-issue "AUDIT 2026-08-16: exact-token relevance does not mark the motivating has-heart-count predicate as mentioned; it survives the 16-predicate fixture only because the whole filtered vocabulary fits. The candidate entity list also mixes entities with literal-like values such as blue, while the scorer ignores objectIsLiteral/datatype. A vocabulary score can therefore improve while object typing regresses undetected." ;
    pred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/vocabulary-context.ts" ;
    pred:feature-id "F136" ;
    pred:priority "high" ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/rdf> ;
    pred:known-issue "AUDIT 2026-08-16: run-ollama-bench catches a failed model run as null, drops it from the report and can exit 0 with no score. --ground covers baseline mode only, while production Ollama ingest defaults to structured extraction. Make missing requested results fail loudly, score object kind/direction, and measure the production-shaped path." ;
    rdfs:label "Vocabulary grounding — tell the model our words before it guesses its own" ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/llm> ;
    pred:measurement "MEASURED 2026-08-15, qwen3.6 structured extraction on the octopus fixture. Strict ingest F1 26.3%. Decomposed: FACT RECALL 44.4% (8 of 18 golden facts found by subject+object) against VOCABULARY AGREEMENT 62.5% (5 of those 8 also used our predicate). The three disagreements were is-found-in twice (model said has-habitat) and has-heart-count (model said has-number-of-hearts). So roughly a third of what the strict score called failure was the model finding the right fact and calling it something else — and strict F1 charges each of those TWICE, once as a missed golden and once as an unmatched output." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:predicate-economy ;
