kb:entity-sets
    pred:principle "Entity TYPES describe ONE node's kind; entity SETS describe a reusable GROUP of nodes + the relationships among them. A set is a customizable grouping — optionally with premade triples relating its members — that a user can define once and instantiate, the way types are reused." ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/rdf> ;
    pred:remaining "DERIVE A SET FROM A PREDICATE, rather than only from manual multi-select (2026-08-13). F65 membership is authored by Ctrl+click on the canvas — this entity's own remaining list calls that gesture clunky — so grouping N nodes by a fact they already state means hand-selecting them and keeping the selection in sync forever. Given a predicate, the app should mint one ktype:EntitySet per distinct object with has-member filled in, and regenerate when the facts change. Verified by hand: three sets derived from pred:eligible-entity over a real graph produced 10, 5 and 4 members correctly from a ~30-line script, and it generalises free to group-by-funder, group-by-status and group-by-source." ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/entity-sets.test.ts" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:meta-visual-generation ;
    pred:has-status "functional" ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:relates-to kb:entity-types ;
    pred:honest-note "THE DETECTOR MISSED THIS, and a human found it (2026-07-19). shared-deps.ts reported 67 overlap pairs and none was entity-sets vs loop-job-grouping. Two causes, and the second is the real one. (1) It only saw ktype:Feature entities with kpred:has-file, so loop-job-grouping — a ktype:Phase with no files — was invisible. Fixed: Phases are now included. (2) FUNDAMENTAL: section 3 pairs entities that share a dependency HUB, and these two share none. Conceptual duplication does not announce itself as a shared dependency. A new lexical check (vocabulary overlap) was added and DOES now flag the pair — but that result is CIRCULAR and must not be read as vindication: verified by stashing the TTL edits and re-running, the check does NOT detect the pair on the pre-edit graph. It only works now because the unification was already written into both descriptions." ;
    pred:feature-id "F65" ;
    pred:evidence "A SECOND RECORDED INSTANCE OF THE SYNONYM-GAP DETECTOR FAILURE, and it is worth counting because one is an anecdote. On 2026-08-13 Opus proposed a 'facet layout' to cluster nodes by the object of a chosen predicate — the same concept as this feature in different vocabulary, sharing no dependency with it. Neither graph-lint nor shared-deps flagged it; Matt did, immediately, with 'I think facets may just be Sets?'. That is exactly the class this entity's honest-note predicts ('SAME CONCEPT, DIFFERENT VOCABULARY, NO SHARED DEPENDENCY ... found by humans reading the graph, which is not a control'), now observed twice, which strengthens the case for the proposed embedding-similarity check over entity descriptions." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:arrange-layout-builder ;
    pred:priority "high" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:sets-xr ;
    pred:open-question "The undetected class is: SAME CONCEPT, DIFFERENT VOCABULARY, NO SHARED DEPENDENCY. Before the edits, one entity said 'group nodes into a set' and the other said 'group the queue into one decision' — synonyms, zero lexical overlap. No keyword rule can bridge a synonym gap, so this needs EMBEDDING similarity over entity descriptions — the same BGE-small machinery kb:entity-normalization already uses for duplicate entities (0.90 threshold). That is still SCRIPT tier: embeddings are local and deterministic, no cloud tokens and no judgment call. Until it exists, this class is found by humans reading the graph, which is not a control." ;
    pred:principle "ONE GROUPING PRIMITIVE, THREE CONSUMERS (Matt, 2026-07-19: 'job grouping may be the same thing as sets'). ktype:EntitySet + has-member is not a graph-canvas feature that happens to group nodes — it is THE grouping primitive, and three separate features need it: (1) kb:entity-sets, grouping ENTITIES on the canvas; (2) kb:loop-job-grouping, grouping PENDING STATEMENTS into one review decision; (3) kb:agent-orchestration, grouping TASKS into a batch a harness claims and a human steers. They differ only in member TYPE. All three need the same things: membership, a label, scoping/spotlight, a batch action, and provenance. Building three grouping mechanisms would be exactly the 'separate code for nearly the same task' that scripts/offline/shared-deps.ts exists to catch — so review grouping and task batching REUSE src/lib/rdf/entity-sets.ts rather than reimplementing it." ;
    pred:open-question "Storage: is a set a first-class node (ktype:EntitySet) whose members are linked by a 'has-member' predicate, or a saved selection/template? First-class node makes it queryable + shareable and round-trips in TTL." ;
    pred:remaining "FIRST CUT SHIPPED (2026-07-10): batch multi-select in the graph -> '⬡ group as set' creates a first-class ktype:EntitySet node linked to members via urn:kbase:predicate/has-member (src/lib/rdf/entity-sets.ts; rendered via the new EntitySet built-in type; deletion-protected as a group). Term 'set' used in the UI. SHIPPED select→act (2026-07-10): selecting a set node dims the rest and spotlights the set + members; the set panel offers '⬡ select N members' to act on the group. REMAINING: (1) instantiate a set as a TEMPLATE (drop the whole cluster + premade relating triples up front); (2) extra ANALYZE options on a grouped selection; (3) less-clunky multi-select (lasso; the entry gesture is still canvas Ctrl+click); (4) minor: a set isn't findable by name in search (the label surfaces as a literal node, not the set entity)." ;
    pred:description "Two entry points to the same concept: (1) MULTI-ENTITY CREATION — define a 'set' up front (members + optional premade relating triples), a template you instantiate to drop a whole related cluster at once (e.g. a 'trip' set = 2 people + 2 options + routes, like the starter graph's shape). Lives alongside entity types. (2) MULTI-NODE GROUPING IN THE GRAPH — select several nodes (extends the existing Ctrl+click multi-select) and GROUP them; a grouped selection unlocks additional ANALYZE options (analyze the subgraph as a unit) and a 'save as entity set' action that captures the members + their inter-relationships as a reusable set. Grouping also feeds F58.5 asset-assembly (a set is a natural generation subgraph) and F60 arrange (a set is a natural page section)." ;
    rdfs:label "Entity sets (multi-entity groupings)" .

kb:work-tiering
    pred:relates-to kb:offline-align-review ;
    pred:has-status "in-progress" ;
