kb:orch-control-layers
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Phase> ;
    pred:relates-to kb:entity-sets ;
    pred:description "Names the control surface the loop already implies but never gathered in one place. LAYER 1 DIRECTION — the ideas graph (kb:loop-idea-inbox): humans create new direction with zero-friction capture, diffed against the roadmap; promotion to a planned feature is a USER-AUTHORITY act (F88 — minting a feature is authorship, not observation). LAYER 2 FRAMING — a task is a triple (F87), so a human edits the task itself: its prompt, tier, dependencies and definition-of-done, in the Review tab, before or during the run. LAYER 3 SCOPE — work is claimed and steered as a SET (kb:entity-sets), not one task at a time: approve a batch, re-aim a batch, park a batch. LAYER 4 TIER — which harness runs it (kb:work-tiering), overridable per task. LAYER 5 LANDING — the existing merge gates; a sub-agent returns a graph and Opus merges it (kb:loop-subagent-graphs). Autonomy modes (kb:shelly-curation: suggest / auto-safe / auto-all) apply PER LAYER, not globally." ;
    pred:has-status "planned" ;
    <urn:reckons:nav/order> "5"^^xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "Phase 5 — the layers a human steers the orchestrator by" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:shelly-curation ;
    pred:open-question "Does a human intervention at layer 2 or 3 become a FACT in the graph (an event with actor=human, joining the archive journal alongside actor=agent edits), or is it ordinary task-state mutation? Recording it makes the loop auditable — 'who re-aimed this, and when' — and costs one triple. Not recording it means the loop's own history is the one thing it cannot answer questions about." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:loop-idea-inbox ;
    pred:relates-to kb:co-orchestration ;
    pred:part-of kb:agent-orchestration ;
    pred:relates-to kb:work-tiering ;
    pred:remaining "Not built. The layer vocabulary on the task model; per-layer autonomy settings; a Review-tab surface for editing a task's framing mid-flight; batch (set-scoped) steering actions." ;
    pred:principle "CONTROL IS LAYERED, NOT BINARY (Matt, 2026-07-19). 'Approve / don't approve' is not steering — by the time a diff arrives the direction was already chosen. A human must be able to intervene at the level where the decision actually lives: what gets worked on at all, how a task is framed, which tier runs it, and only lastly whether the result lands. Autonomy settings that only guard the LAST layer feel safe and change nothing." ;
    pred:constraint "Whatever the autonomy level, layers 1 and 2 stay human-writable at all times. An orchestrator that can rewrite its own direction and its own definition-of-done is not being supervised, it is being watched." ;
    pred:principle "EACH LAYER IS THE SAME VERB AT A DIFFERENT ALTITUDE: guide (set direction), refine (reshape a task in flight), redirect (pull work back and re-aim it). The lower the layer a human can act at, the less rework the loop generates — which is the same compounding argument kb:graph-economics makes about avoided duplication." .

