    pred:priority "medium" ;
    pred:description "A bundled reference graph describing the optimized post dimensions for common platforms — one entity per platform PLACEMENT (not per platform, since a single platform has many: feed square, feed portrait, story, reel cover, link card), carrying pixel width and height, aspect ratio, accepted encodings, and any maximum file size. Importable as pre-configured nodes so a user's own content graph can point at a format rather than restate it, and so a generated or composed asset (F58 / F71) can be checked against a target it can name." ;
    pred:open-question "SCOPE OF 'CONTENT OPERATIONS' — Matt's call, and it changes the plan substantially. Narrow reading: a format reference graph and nothing else (what is planned here). Broad reading: the whole pipeline — compose from a set (F71), size to a target format, schedule, publish via the user's own n8n (F20), and track what went out. The broad version is several features and needs its own decomposition; this entity deliberately plans only the reference layer, because that layer is useful on its own and is a dependency of every version of the broad one." ;
    pred:estimated-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:relates-to kb:content-orchestrator ;
    pred:relates-to kb:entity-sets ;
    pred:relates-to kb:shape-validation ;
    pred:open-question "WHICH PLATFORMS AND PLACEMENTS in v1, and who decides when one is wrong? A short, well-sourced list beats a long, half-checked one — every entity is a maintenance obligation with a staleness clock attached." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:graph-publishing ;
    pred:decided "TIER THE MAINTENANCE, DO NOT DO IT AT OPUS TIER (F74.3, and Matt 2026-08-15: \"lets make sure we are looking for local agents to run, for non-deterministic repeat actions\"). This feature generates exactly the recurring workload the tiering rules exist for, and it splits cleanly. SCRIPT TIER, because a rule decides it: does every format entity carry dcterms:source and dcterms:modified, is the checked-on date within the freshness window, do width and height agree with the stated aspect ratio, does every entity pass the SHACL shape, and is every catalogue URL reachable and parseable. That check is right by construction, costs nothing, and fails loudly in CI. AGENT TIER, because it is judgment over language: reading a platform's own spec page and proposing that a number has changed. It emits a PROPOSAL into knowledge.pending.jsonl for a human to accept — never a direct edit to the reference graph, because an unattended local model rewriting the dimensions we publish is precisely the failure this feature is most exposed to. OPUS TIER: only triaging what those two produce." ;
    pred:honest-note "PLANNED, NOTHING BUILT (2026-08-15). Verified against the code today: there is no format data anywhere in src/, and no catalogue of importable graphs — src/lib/stores/official-kb.svelte.ts hardcodes ONE read-only graph, OFFICIAL_KB_FILE = '/starter-guide.ttl'. So 'pre-configured nodes available for import' has no mechanism to be offered through yet; that catalogue is the real prerequisite and is the larger half of this work." ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/storage> ;
    pred:relates-to kb:meta-visual-generation ;
    pred:constraint "A HOSTED CATALOGUE IS A CSP CHANGE AND A PROMISE. Fetching the manifest means adding the docs origin to connect-src in src/app.html — the exact class of omission that left repo ingest, site export and Indico dead in the browser while green in Node, and which scripts/offline/csp-origin-audit.ts now checks. It also makes those URLs a published interface: once a user has imported from a catalogue entry, moving or deleting it breaks their expectation, so entries need stable paths and removals need to be deliberate." ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/static> ;
    pred:has-status "planned" ;
    pred:feature-id "F134" ;
    rdfs:label "Content operations — post formats as importable reference nodes" ;
    pred:constraint "THESE FACTS ROT, AND WE WOULD BE THE ONES ASSERTING THEM. Platform specs change without notice and without a changelog. A node that flatly says Instagram feed portrait is 1080x1350 is a claim MADE BY US about somebody else's product, and if it goes stale the user does not get an error — they get a correctly-generated, wrongly-sized asset and no reason to doubt it. This is the single hardest constraint in the feature and it shapes the whole design: every format entity MUST carry dcterms:source (the platform's own spec page) and dcterms:modified (the date WE checked), the UI must show how old that check is, and nothing may present these as authoritative. A reference graph that cannot say when it was last true is worse than no reference graph, because it converts the user's uncertainty into misplaced confidence." ;
    pred:open-question "Video and audio, or images only? Duration limits, frame rates and codecs are the same SHAPE of fact but a much larger surface, and they go stale faster." ;
    pred:decided "DO NOT REUSE kpred:has-dimension. It already means a DIMENSION OF THE ALIGNMENT SCORE (coverage, status, dependencies, scope). Pointing it at pixels would create exactly the homograph the graph already carries as a known defect in kb:design-system — one IRI naming two unrelated things, which RDF then merges into one contradictory entity." ;
    pred:estimated-files "9"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:principle "A FORMAT IS A NODE, NOT A CONSTANT. The point of putting these in the graph rather than a lookup table in code is that a user's post can be RELATED to the format it targets, so the graph can answer 'which of my assets are the wrong size for where they are going'. A constant buried in a component answers nothing and cannot be extended by a user with a platform we never heard of." ;
    pred:constraint "THE AGENT-TIER REFRESH IS AN OUTBOUND FETCH AGAINST SOMEBODY ELSE'S SITE, so it inherits the F123 politeness rules — honor robots.txt, identify the client, keep the interval conservative, back off on errors. It is also opt-in via OLLAMA_BASE_URL like every other agent job, and must degrade to 'not checked' rather than silently falling back to a cloud model." ;
    pred:decided "BUNDLED COPY *AND* A HOSTED CATALOGUE, in that order of trust. Matt, 2026-08-15: \"I think we could host a catalog of ttls for import from our documentation site?\" — which corrects an earlier call recorded here that said bundled ONLY, on the reasoning that anything fetched is a backend by another name. That reasoning was wrong for this case and is left visible rather than quietly deleted: the documentation site is already static files on Cloudflare Pages built from content/, so serving a .ttl from it adds no server, no state and no per-user anything. It is the same static-hosting model the app already runs on. What the earlier objection DOES still buy is the failure mode: the bundled copy ships with the app so the catalogue degrades to a working offline list rather than an empty screen, and the remote copy is the fresher one when it is reachable. The app must say WHICH it is showing and how old that copy is." .

kb:question-router
    pred:honest-note "in-progress (2026-07-15): phase 1 scaffolded, phase 2 (throw-and-forget) functional — the local answer-return loop closes with opt-in consent and external-graph provenance. NOT done: a UI to set consent, real cross-boundary transport, and phase 3 RBAC (gated on F84). One process's pending queue stands in for two graphs today." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:cross-kb-alignment ;
    pred:feature-id "F91" ;
    pred:has-status "in-progress" ;
    pred:depends-on kb:async-orchestration ;
    pred:has-phase kb:qr-relevance ;
    pred:depends-on kb:cross-kb-alignment ;
    pred:has-phase kb:qr-throw-forget ;
    pred:principle "A question is addressed by RELATEDNESS, not by broadcast. Sending every question to every graph is spam and a privacy leak; sending it to the graph whose knowledge overlaps the subject is routing. The router's whole value is picking WHO." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:publisher-subscriptions ;
    pred:principle "DAISY-CHAIN ONLY ALONG ALLOWED EDGES. A non-public Reckoning gathers its facts privately. A hop that policy does not permit does not happen — a question must never leak to a graph its owner did not authorize. RBAC is the routing table, not an afterthought." ;
    rdfs:label "Question router (throw a question to whoever can answer it)" ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:principle "Every hop records provenance. A question that arrived via A→B→C carries that chain, and the answer returns along it. Provenance is an accountability relation (the thesis): you must be able to say WHERE a gathered fact came from and by whose leave." ;
    pred:description "A question the local graph cannot settle is addressed OUTWARD, to the graphs most related to its subject. Relatedness is computed (shared entities, shared predicates, subject overlap) so 'who could answer this?' is a ranking, not a hunch. The asker throws and forgets; the answer returns asynchronously along the F80 answer-loop, extended across graphs. Across enterprise boundaries it daisy-chains under RBAC: each hop is an ALLOWED edge, authorized and provenance-stamped, because a proposed Reckoning is a private deliberation, not a broadcast." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:async-orchestration ;
    pred:principle "THROW AND FORGET. Decision-making must not stall on whether the answerer is reachable right now. The question is durable (a partial fact); the answer arrives when it arrives; the asker never blocks." ;
    pred:has-phase kb:qr-rbac-chain .

kb:visual-acceptance
    pred:remaining "kpred:done-when accepting a non-command criterion; deterministic image checks (non-uniform, non-empty, parses); VLM scoring harness against kb:web-uiux-rubric; asset review in the queue, gated by F88." ;
    pred:has-status "scaffolded" ;
    pred:proof "The deterministic image check (checkImage) is built and tested: it DECODES the PNG pixels and catches the flat/black frame that Blender renders while exiting 0. Verified against a real black render (variation 0.000, rejected) and a real scene (39% of pixels vary, accepted). Its first version sampled COMPRESSED bytes and passed a black frame as a real image — the exact confidently-wrong-checker failure, fixed by decoding actual pixels and measuring against the DOMINANT colour, not the first pixel. VLM scoring and user-taste routing (the other two rungs) are not built." ;
    pred:principle "Taste routes to the user, and it must be ALLOWED to. The verifiability axis exists precisely so a domain like this can say `user` out loud instead of dressing a preference up as a passing check." ;
    pred:principle "Push as much as possible into the deterministic check FIRST. A black frame, an empty scene and a 0-byte export are RULES, and rules cost nothing to triage. Sending those to a VLM moves cost from generation to review rather than removing it (F74.3)." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Phase> ;
    <urn:reckons:nav/order> "2"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:description "The generalization the whole orchestration needs. A content task's acceptance criterion cannot be a test, so it is one of: a DETERMINISTIC check (the image is not uniformly one colour; the GLB parses; the file is not 0 bytes; the frame is not empty) — cheap, script tier, and it catches the black-frame class outright; a VLM JUDGMENT (qwen2.5vl scores the render against the brief and the design rubric) — agent tier, a PROPOSAL, never an auto-accept; or THE USER (verifiable-by `user` — taste is not delegable, and pretending otherwise is how you ship forty assets nobody likes)." ;
    pred:has-file "scripts/content/blender-render.ts" ;
    rdfs:label "Phase 2 — done-when for things that have no exit code" ;
    pred:part-of kb:content-orchestration .

