    pred:description "Give the arrange mode a VERTICAL-SCROLL, FULL-SCREEN mode: instead of a fixed canvas grid, nodes flow top-to-bottom in a scrollable column, and can be grouped horizontally (side-by-side) to form rows. You accomplish both a vertical layout (stacked sections) and a horizontal layout (columns within a row) by arranging + ordering. That arrangement is captured on the nodes (order + row/column grouping, extending the existing nodeOrder / hnav ordering) and maps to the published page's block structure. Bridges F27 graph-publishing (WebPage nodes -> content/*.md) and F55.4 custom pages: the arrange canvas becomes a lightweight page-layout builder where blocks are graph nodes." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> .

kb:discrepancy-backpropagation
    pred:scope-decision "A HUMAN IS AN OBSERVER AND CAN BE WRONG THE SAME WAY (Matt). A person reporting a manual test can mis-report a pass or a fail by misunderstanding what the test intended — which is not carelessness but the same design-ambiguity fault in category (3), surfacing through a person instead of a tool. So a human verdict enters the set as an observation WITH its observer recorded, not as ground truth that ends the investigation. This is F88 verifiability read carefully: authority decides who may SETTLE a fact, and it never made anyone incapable of being mistaken." ;
    pred:principle "AN OBSERVATION IS NEVER REWOUND, AND THIS IS THE LIMIT OF THE REWIND IDEA. An observation records what was true at a time; rewriting it would make the graph able to lie about its own past, which is the one thing the archive exists to prevent (F97: losing the ability to revert is recoverable, losing the record that something happened is not). So a rewind restores the DESIGN and regenerates the ARTIFACT, and then RE-OBSERVES it. The fresh observation is the evidence that the rewind worked; the old observations stay, and the pair of them is the record that a reversal happened at all." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:design-observed-archive ;
    pred:relates-to kb:verifiability-axis ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 2 — backpropagation: let an investigation conclude AGAINST THE DESIGN, writing a missing-fact or ambiguity finding onto the design entity itself, so the gap is visible to roadmap:readiness rather than being fixed at the artifact level and recurring." ;
    pred:scope-decision "THREE PLACES THE FAULT CAN LIE, and naming them is most of the work. (1) THE OBSERVER — the VLM misjudged, the selector was stale, the check tested the wrong thing. (2) THE ARTIFACT — the page really is broken and the observer is right. (3) THE DESIGN — the intent was ambiguous or incomplete, and BOTH the artifact and the observer are faithfully implementing different readings of it. The third is the one nothing currently looks for, and it is the one that recurs, because a design gap regenerates the same disagreement after every fix applied at the other two levels." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:archive-revert ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:open-question "Does a discrepancy BLOCK anything? Blocking publication on an unresolved disagreement makes the site hostage to a flaky observer; not blocking means shipping while two graphs say incompatible things about what shipped. The publish gate's precedent is to refuse and explain rather than proceed quietly, but that gate covers content safety, where the cost of a false negative is much higher." ;
    pred:has-status "planned" ;
    rdfs:label "Discrepancies flow back through the set — and the set can be rewound" ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 3 — use-case-driven reopening: adding a use case to an existing design re-evaluates its missing facts, so a previously clean feature can legitimately become under-defined." ;
    pred:honest-note "PLANNED, NOTHING BUILT (2026-07-30). No discrepancy record exists, nothing routes a finding backwards to a design, and there is no set-level rewind. The primitives are real and unconnected: multi-graph-query.ts detects cross-graph disagreement and refuses to resolve it; archive.ts restoreSnapshot reverts ONE graph from a full pre-operation snapshot and journals the revert as itself revertible; F113 would give the set its roles. A set-level rewind is the archive primitive applied across members in dependency order, which nothing coordinates today." ;
    pred:description "Matt (2026-07-30): when observations disagree, that is a discrepancy to investigate, and the fault may lie anywhere in the set — including in the ORIGINAL DESIGN. Findings must therefore flow BACKWARDS from observation to artifact to design, not only forwards. And the more completely the set is archived, the more capable a REWIND becomes: on a change of design intent, the design and the observable outputs should return, as far as possible, to a coherent earlier state." ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 1 — discrepancy as an entity: two or more observations disagreeing about one artifact becomes a recorded node carrying both verdicts, both methods, and the artifact, routed for investigation rather than scored. Reuses multi-graph-query's existing conflict detection." ;
    pred:principle "DESIGN COMPLETENESS IS RELATIVE TO THE USE CASES KNOWN AT THE TIME. A long-standing feature whose design was fully sufficient becomes under-specified the moment a new use case is added, with nothing having broken and no test having failed. This is why npm run roadmap:readiness measuring zero missing facts is a statement about a rubric and a moment, not a permanent property — adding a use case must be able to REOPEN missing facts on a design that previously scored clean. A readiness measure that cannot go backwards will quietly certify stale designs." ;
    pred:principle "REWIND IS BEST-EFFORT AND MUST SAY WHERE IT FELL SHORT. A design and a generated artifact can be restored exactly. Anything hand-edited, externally published, or already consumed downstream cannot — a page a reader has seen, a graph another party leaped into, an asset someone embedded. A rewind that silently restores what it can and stays quiet about the rest reports success for a partial reversal, which is the same failure shape as a partial archive move reporting completion." ;
    pred:depends-on kb:graph-sets ;
    pred:depends-on kb:archive-foundation ;
    pred:feature-id "F114" ;
    pred:open-question "How far back can a rewind reach before the design's own vocabulary has moved? Restoring a design from before a predicate was renamed gives a graph whose generators no longer understand it. Either the archive carries the migration path, or rewind has a horizon that must be stated rather than discovered when it fails." ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 4 — set rewind: restore design members from the archive in dependency order, regenerate artifacts, re-observe, and REPORT what could not be reverted — hand edits, published output, anything already consumed by another party." .

kb:mvg-output-types
    pred:scope-decision "MATT 2026-07-23: an asset output LANDS AS PENDING, never overwriting the target node's existing slot. This keeps one review model for everything the system proposes — a generated asset is a proposal exactly as a generated fact is, and the graph's existing pending/confirmed lifecycle already carries it. CONSEQUENCE, and it is real work rather than a detail: the review queue currently renders TEXT triples, so a pending binary asset needs a preview affordance (thumbnail for PNG, model viewer for GLB, page-1 render for PDF) that does not exist today. A pending asset a reviewer cannot SEE is a fact they cannot judge, so the preview is part of the feature, not a polish item after it." ;
    pred:description "The generation box lets the user select an output type, aiming for maximal coverage: FACTS (the existing text proposal), PNG (image), PDF (composed document — could reuse graph facts + images as a laid-out sheet), and GLB (3D). The chosen type routes to the matching backend (image-gen for PNG, a doc composer for PDF, Meshy or local Blender for GLB). Facts remain the default so the existing Reckoning behaviour is one output type among several, not a special case." ;
    rdfs:label "Maximal output types (PNG / PDF / GLB)" ;
    pred:feature-id "F58.4" ;
    pred:remaining "Output-type selector on the Reckoning surface (FACTS default); target-node picker shown only for asset outputs; asset-aware review-queue rows with per-type previews; routing to the matching backend per type." ;
    pred:scope-decision "MATT 2026-07-23: the output-type selector is THE generation surface, and it is what makes generation semi-consistent — one place, one control, every backend behind it. When the selected output is a DIGITAL ASSET (PNG/PDF/GLB) rather than facts, the user must also SELECT A TARGET NODE to receive the output; the asset attaches to that node's asset slot (kasset:icon / preview / model per kb-assets). This REPLACES the earlier 'outputs land back as a NEW entity's asset' wording: a user-chosen target is explicit and reviewable, where an auto-created entity is a second thing to clean up. Asset outputs therefore need a node picker in the Reckoning UI, and the selector must disable/hide the picker when output type is FACTS." ;
    pred:part-of kb:meta-visual-generation ;
    pred:has-status "speculative" ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> .

