kb:device-graph
    pred:constraint "REUSE src/lib/integrations/llm/device-capability.ts. It already detects iOS WebKit, memory constraint via navigator.deviceMemory, and whether an in-browser model should be avoided (TINY_WASM_MODEL, CONSTRAINED_MODEL_MB_CAP). That is a narrow GATE today, not a profile — extend it into one rather than writing a second detector." ;
    rdfs:label "One-click device graph (and device-to-device comparison)" ;
    pred:measured "Method proven 2026-07-19 by scripts/offline/energy-bench.ts on 2x RTX 3090 / Ryzen 9 3900X, marginal GPU energy above idle, warm model, three sizes: llama3.2:3b ~0.68-1.00 Wh per 1k output tokens; mistral-nemo 12.2B ~2.66-3.09; qwen3-coder 30.5B ~1.31. Two findings. (1) The phone-class 3B is 3-4x more energy-efficient per token than the 12B — the capability/energy frontier moving down-market is measurable, not just assertable. (2) The 30.5B beat the 12.2B, almost certainly because it is mixture-of-experts with far fewer ACTIVE parameters — which is Matt's trajectory thesis (capability packed into fewer active params) showing up directly in the numbers. Spread across tasks is 4.6x, so no mean is quotable yet." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:local-offload ;
    pred:has-status "planned" ;
    pred:feature-id "F106" ;
    pred:description "One click produces a graph describing the user's current device and what it can actually run: static capability (GPU/VRAM/CPU/cores/RAM/WebGPU/browser) plus MEASURED facts (tokens per second and watt-hours per 1k tokens, per model actually run). Because it is a graph it round-trips as TTL, syncs through the existing workspace folder sync, and compares device-to-device through the existing compare/align path — so a user can answer 'will this model run well on MY machine' from real measurements on similar hardware rather than vendor claims." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:relates-to kb:environmental-impact ;
    pred:depends-on kb:workspace-sync ;
    pred:constraint "A device profile is FINGERPRINTABLE. GPU model, VRAM, core count, screen and browser together are close to a unique identifier, so this is opt-in, local by default, and sharing is an explicit act — never background telemetry. Reckons.AI is local-first and does not phone home; a feature that quietly built a device census would contradict the product. Sharing a profile must also be reviewable before it leaves the device, like any other export." ;
    pred:principle "A DEVICE PROFILE IS A GRAPH, SO IT NEEDS NO NEW MACHINERY. Facts about this machine — GPU, VRAM, cores, RAM, WebGPU support, and MEASURED throughput and energy per model — are triples like any other. Which means sharing a profile is folder sync, comparing two profiles is the existing cross-graph compare (F16), and a corpus of them is just a multi-graph registry. Inventing a bespoke 'benchmark format' would be building a private version of the product." ;
    pred:remaining "Not built. Extend device-capability.ts into a profile emitter; a one-click 'describe this device' action writing a device graph; in-browser throughput measurement (WebGPU/WASM) since nvidia-smi is desktop-only; opt-in share via folder sync; a device-comparison view over the existing compare path." .

