    pred:evidence "GRIST'S STABLE ROW IDS ARE THE CONCRETE REASON THIS IS NOT INTERCHANGEABLE WITH GOOGLE SHEETS (2026-07-31). F121 has to DERIVE a row identity — a user-named key column or a hash — because a Sheets row is addressed by an index that moves on every sort. Grist gives identity for free, which removes the single largest source of duplicate ingest. Two connectors serving one purpose with a real difference between them is the F116 case argued from evidence rather than assertion." ;
    pred:description "Matt (2026-07-31) proposes Grist as the structured-data partner. It fits the stance better than any spreadsheet-in-the-cloud: open source and self-hostable, a document is a plain SQLite file the user owns, columns can be formulas, rows carry stable identities, and it has an HTTP API — so it is a real store the app can talk to rather than a file it has to guess at. It plays the role for calculation that self-hosted n8n plays for web side-effects." ;
