kb:sveltia-cms-setup
    pred:alt "Local editing without the worker: Sveltia has no proxy-server backend (@sveltia/cms-proxy-server does not exist on npm) and ignores local_backend (a Decap-only option). On localhost it auto-offers \"Work with Local Repository\" (File System Access API, Chromium-family browsers only) with no config change needed." ;
    pred:setup-step "4. backend.base_url in static/admin/config.yml points at https://admin.reckons.ai. DONE." ;
    pred:note "RESOLVED (2026-07-02): Pages collection listed zero entries after login, even though content/docs/welcome.md and content/releases/v0-1-0.md exist on main with matching frontmatter. Root cause: Sveltia CMS does not implement Decap/Netlify's `nested` collection option at all (confirmed against the pinned @sveltia/cms@0.169.1 bundle and its docs — nested collections are tracked for a future 2.0 release). The committed config's `nested: { depth: 4 }` was silently ignored, so the folder collection fell back to scanning only the immediate content/ directory and found nothing, since both pages live one level down (content/docs/, content/releases/). Fix: replaced `nested: { depth: 4 }` with `path: '{{fields.section | slugify}}/{{fields.slug}}'` in static/admin/config.yml and in the generated-config string sveltiaConfig() in src/lib/publish/site-export.ts (kept in lockstep). `path` is the option Sveltia does support for subfoldered entries: it recursively discovers existing files matching the template and places new ones, with no per-entry index file required. The `slugify` filter mirrors contentPath()'s `slugify(page.section)` so the folder segment matches what's on disk (e.g. section \"Docs\" -> content/docs/). Verified against the real pinned Sveltia bundle via Playwright + the CMS's built-in `test-repo` backend (OPFS-backed, no GitHub auth needed): with the original `nested` config the console logged \"Nested collections are not yet supported in Sveltia CMS\" and the collection showed 0 entries on the exact same seeded files; with the `path` fix it showed 2 entries (Welcome, Reckons.AI v0.1.0) with correct titles/status. Also verified creating a new page (title \"Test Page\", section \"Sandbox\") through the real CMS UI wrote content/sandbox/test-page.md, matching site-export.ts's contentPath() layout exactly. Note: Sveltia's own frontmatter serialization (quoting style, empty-string fields) differs cosmetically from pageToMarkdown()'s canonical form, so scripts/md-align.ts correctly flags freshly CMS-saved files as drifted until they are re-exported from the graph — this is by design, not a bug." ;
    pred:note "/admin is a standalone static page, so the app's strict CSP does not apply; Sveltia reaches api.github.com and the worker directly." ;
    pred:setup-step "3. Set the worker's GITHUB_CLIENT_ID / GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET vars and ALLOWED_DOMAINS to the OAuth App credentials. ALLOWED_DOMAINS is a comma-separated allowlist (`*` matches one subdomain level); it is 'reckons.ai,*.reckons-ai.pages.dev' so the production custom domain AND the dev branch alias + hashed preview deploys (dev.reckons-ai.pages.dev, <hash>.reckons-ai.pages.dev) can all authenticate. TOOLED — paste the OAuth App's Client ID/Secret into SVELTIA_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID / SVELTIA_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET / SVELTIA_ALLOWED_DOMAINS in .env (see .env.example), then run scripts/sveltia-auth-secrets.sh, which pushes them to the worker via `wrangler secret put`. VERIFIED 2026-07-09: GET /auth?provider=github&site_id=dev.reckons-ai.pages.dev (and a preview hash) both 302 to github.com/login/oauth/authorize; a disallowed domain does not." ;
    pred:note "static/admin/index.html now loads a version-pinned Sveltia bundle (@sveltia/cms@0.169.1) instead of an unpinned \"latest\" CDN URL, so upgrades are deliberate. static/media/ (config.yml media_folder) was missing and has been created with a .gitkeep." ;
    rdfs:label "Sveltia CMS — Setup (GitHub OAuth)" ;
    pred:setup-step "1. Deploy sveltia-cms-auth: github.com/sveltia/sveltia-cms-auth (Cloudflare Worker reckons-sveltia-cms-auth, mapped to admin.reckons.ai). DONE." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Concept> ;
    pred:setup-step "5. Visit https://reckons.ai/admin/ and Login with GitHub" ;
    pred:part-of kb:graph-publishing ;
    pred:description "Wire the /admin content manager. The CMS entry (static/admin/index.html) + config (static/admin/config.yml) ship in the app and are served at Reckons.AI/admin. GitHub login uses sveltia-cms-auth, a small Cloudflare Worker." ;
    pred:setup-step "2. Create a GitHub OAuth App: Settings > Developer settings > OAuth Apps. Homepage https://reckons.ai; Authorization callback URL https://admin.reckons.ai/callback. MANUAL — a human must do this in the GitHub UI." ;
    pred:note "RESOLVED (2026-07-02): the earlier bare-404 readiness finding was a missing route — the worker was deployed with the correct sveltia-cms-auth code but had workers.dev previews disabled and no custom domain attached. Fixed by attaching admin.reckons.ai as a Workers custom domain via the Cloudflare API (PUT /accounts/{id}/workers/domains). OAuth App credentials pushed via scripts/sveltia-auth-secrets.sh. Verified: GET /auth?provider=github&site_id=reckons.ai returns 302 to github.com/login/oauth/authorize. Setup steps 1-4 complete; only step 5 (login smoke test at reckons.ai/admin/) remains." .

kb:all-previews-modifier
    pred:touches-file "src/lib/3d/KnowledgeGraph.svelte" ;
    rdfs:label "All previews — a force MODIFIER that shows every asset at once" ;
    pred:decided "IMPLEMENT AS A COLLISION CONSTRAINT, NOT AS REPULSION TUNING. The simulation in KnowledgeGraph.svelte is entirely SIZE-BLIND: pairwise repulsion is f = REPEL / d2 with REPEL a single constant (1.6), so every node pushes exactly as hard regardless of what it renders. Scaling REPEL for preview nodes would spread the graph without ever GUARANTEEING non-overlap, because inverse-square falls off and settles wherever the springs balance it. A positional pass that separates any pair closer than r_a + r_b guarantees the property the feature is named for. This is the same lesson as the timeline x-lock: when a property must HOLD, assert it directly instead of hoping tuned constants converge on it." ;
    pred:tested-by "tests/visual/preview-collage.test.ts" ;
    pred:open-question "What happens to nodes with NO preview while the modifier is on — keep their glyph at normal size (mixed graph stays readable, which matches how F59 wants unlocated nodes handled), dim them, or hide them so the view is purely a wall of images? Matt's call." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:spatial-force-layout ;
    pred:open-question "Which nodes count as HAVING a preview — image only, or GLB and video too? nodeAssetFor already resolves glb / video / image in priority order, so the modifier could billboard a still for all three, or show only images and leave 3D nodes as they are. Not guessed." ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/storage/__tests__/preview-mode.test.ts" ;
    pred:constraint "THE TIMELINE X-LOCK MUST SURVIVE THE MODIFIER. Timeline mode pins x to the date anchor every frame precisely because repulsion and springs used to negotiate the axis away. A collision pass that pushes along an arbitrary axis would reintroduce that bug from a new direction — the separation must act in y and z only when the x-lock is on, so a preview never drifts off the day it belongs to." ;
    pred:description "A toggle in the ASSETS group beside AUTO-EXPAND. When on, every node holding a preview asset renders that preview at once, and the layout makes room so they are all actually visible. It COMPOSES with whichever force is running — free, focus, hub, time, arrange, tree — rather than being a seventh force. Turning it on while in timeline mode should still read as a timeline, with the previews spread vertically along it." ;
    pred:has-status "functional" ;
    pred:estimate-note "PREDICTED 7 FILES / 0 DEPS, recorded before writing any code but AFTER reading the simulation and the asset plumbing — so this is an INFORMED estimate, not a blind one like F97.3, and should not be scored as if it were. Predicted: a new pure module for radius + overlap resolution, its test file, KnowledgeGraph.svelte, GraphNode.svelte, +page.svelte for the toggle, db.ts for the setting, and this graph entry. NAMED CREEP RISKS, so a later reading can tell discovery from drift: (1) OverlayGraph.svelte is a SECOND renderer and may need parity, which would be a real dependency the design did not account for; (2) previewUrlFor currently lives inside +page.svelte, so feeding it to the graph may force a store extraction; (3) texture caching may need its own module if naive per-node loading stutters." ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/components> ;
    pred:touches-file "src/lib/components/GraphNode.svelte" ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/3d/__tests__/preview-collage.test.ts" ;
    pred:known-issue "ON A TIMELINE, 'ALL VISIBLE' IS BOUNDED BY THE DATE WINDOW. Nodes are placed at their date and the viewport is a window onto a range you pan and zoom, so a preview whose date is off-screen is off-screen. Measured: 3 of 8 outside the window at default zoom. This is the timeline behaving correctly rather than a defect in the modifier, but it does mean the feature's name overpromises in that one composition, and the visual test says so in place of asserting a number that would hide it." ;
    pred:touches-file "src/routes/(app)/+page.svelte" ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/threed> ;
    pred:actual-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:estimated-files "7"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/storage> ;
    pred:scope-justification "PREDICTED 7 FILES, ACTUAL 11 — the first estimate here to run over, after F75 (5 predicted, 3 actual) and F97.3 (8 predicted, 3 actual) both came in under. The overrun splits into two different things and conflating them would waste the data point. THREE files are a mid-flight scope ADDITION, not a design miss: Matt asked during implementation to fold auto-expand and all-previews into one dropdown, which added preview-mode.ts, its test, and the settings page. That is a changed requirement and the design was never wrong about it. The remaining ONE file over is a genuine omission: the estimate said 'a new pure module and its test file' and counted only the unit test, forgetting that a feature defined by what a person can see needs a VISUAL test and a fixture graph to drive it — and the visual test is what caught two of the three defects above. The named creep risks did NOT fire: OverlayGraph.svelte needed no parity work (the 2D renderer draws its own previews), previewUrlFor needed no store extraction, and no texture cache was required because previews were already DOM elements rather than GPU textures. Reading the risks correctly and still missing the test files suggests estimates here under-count VERIFICATION rather than implementation." ;
    pred:principle "A MODIFIER CHANGES WHAT A NODE SHOWS; A FORCE CHANGES WHERE IT GOES. Making this a force would have made 'see all my images' and 'arrange by date' mutually exclusive, which is exactly backwards — the reason to look at every preview at once is usually to see them ARRANGED by something. Keeping it orthogonal is why it is worth building as a constraint rather than a layout." ;
    pred:open-question "Texture budget. Every visible preview becomes a GPU texture at once; a graph with hundreds of assets could be a real memory and upload cost. GraphNode already carries a module-level GLTF cache for exactly this reason, so a shared texture cache is the obvious parallel — but whether there needs to be a cap, and what happens past it, is undecided." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:entity-types ;
    pred:honest-note "PLANNED, NOTHING BUILT (2026-08-14). What exists today is the SINGLE-asset path: settings.autoExpandAssets opens the SELECTED node's asset large and closes it when selection moves, and nodeAssetFor resolves one node's asset on demand. There is no multi-preview rendering anywhere, no per-node size in the simulation, and no collision pass at all — the sim has repulsion, edge springs, anchor attraction and centering, none of which know how big a node is." ;
    pred:estimated-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:lesson "THREE DEFECTS SURVIVED EVERY UNIT TEST AND DIED IN A BROWSER. (1) settings.alwaysShowPreviews never actually showed all previews — thumbnails render inside the GraphLabels overlay, which culls by distance, sphere occlusion and a 62px proximity dedup, so on a 9-photo graph exactly ONE label survived and it was the node without a photo. (2) Separating in world space converged while still painting a pile, because depth is precisely the direction that projects to nothing; the fix was to separate within the camera's screen plane. (3) The solver corrected positions but not velocities, so the simulation drove each node back in and they visibly vibrated — reported by Matt from the running app, invisible to tests that step the constraint with no forces in between. Every one needed either a screenshot or a human." ;
    pred:touches-file "src/lib/storage/db.ts" ;
    pred:known-issue "AUTO-EXPAND HAD NO DISCOVERABLE EFFECT and this was only found by Matt using it (2026-08-14): the toggle does nothing visible unless you also select a node that happens to own an asset. Folded into the previews dropdown, where the three modes read as alternatives and the tooltip states what each does." ;
    pred:actual-files "11"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:feature-id "F133" ;
    pred:proof "VERIFIED IN A REAL BROWSER, not only in unit tests, because the claim is about what a person can see. tests/visual/preview-collage.test.ts imports a 9-entity fixture where every node carries a photo and all hang off one hub — the arrangement that piles previews up — then MEASURES the painted thumbnails: total occluded thumbnail area as a fraction of thumbnail area. Result on free-force: 8 thumbnails, 0.0% occluded. On timeline: 8 thumbnails in 8 distinct date columns, 0.0% occlusion among those inside the date window. 25 unit tests cover the geometry, 10 cover the settings migration." .

kb:qr-relevance
    pred:description "Given a question (subject + predicate, object open) and a set of candidate graphs, score each by how RELATED it is to the question: does it contain the subject entity, entities that align to it (cross-kb-align), or heavy use of the question's predicate? Output a ranking — the deterministic core of 'who could answer this'. Script tier: the relatedness signals are countable, no model required for the first cut; embeddings refine it later." ;
    pred:part-of kb:question-router ;
    rdfs:label "Phase 1 — rank which graphs are most related to a question" ;
    pred:proof "routeQuestion(question, graphs) ranks candidates by relatedness (knows-the-subject strongest, then predicate familiarity, then neighbourhood overlap); addressees() thresholds so we route rather than broadcast. 7 tests: the graph that already knows the subject ranks first, a graph with no overlap is never addressed, neighbourhood overlap works when the exact subject is absent. NOT yet wired into the review/reckoning UI — that is the remaining work." ;
    pred:has-file "src/lib/rdf/question-router.ts" ;
    pred:remaining "src/lib/rdf/question-router.ts: score(question, graphs) -> ranked candidates; tests; expose in the review/reckoning UI as 'ask another graph'." ;
    <urn:reckons:nav/order> "1"^^xsd:integer ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Phase> ;
    pred:has-status "scaffolded" ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/question-router.test.ts" .

kb:spatial-force-layout
    pred:description "Add a 'map' / 'place' force mode alongside the existing ones. Nodes with location facts are positioned by their coordinates; nodes without stay related-but-loose (or hidden) so mixed graphs still read. The mode DISPLAYS physical distance (e.g. the 327 mi / 5h drives in the starter graph) rather than spring-relation distance. Scale-aware: GPS is assumed to be on Earth's surface, so render with Earth's curvature + scale; astronomical coordinates place nodes in space (solar-system scale). Also the umbrella for improving how the starter graph reads across ALL force modes (it exercises timeline via scheduled-at/ends-at, type via its custom types, hub via people<->options, and now map via coordinates)." ;
    pred:known-issue "FIXED 2026-07-23 — the TIMELINE mode placed nodes on the WRONG DAY, and then let them drift off that day. Two independent causes, both now closed. (1) DATE PARSING: every date in starter-everyday.ttl is a date-only literal (\"2026-07-10\"^^xsd:date), and `new Date('2026-07-10')` is UTC midnight per the ES spec, while a date-TIME without a zone parses as LOCAL — so in America/Chicago the 10 Jul check-in rendered at 9 Jul 19:00 (a full day early), and date-only vs datetime facts sat 5h apart from each other. Now one shared rule in src/lib/rdf/parse-date.ts: no time-of-day = a CALENDAR date at LOCAL midnight, so it lands on the day it names; a value carrying a time is an instant honoured as written; unparseable returns undefined instead of a guessed position. 7 tests pin it. (2) THE AXIS WAS NEGOTIABLE: x encodes the date, but pairwise repulsion, edge springs (x8) and the centering force all pushed on x too, with the date anchor just one voice at 0.85 — so a node settled NEAR its date and could not be read against its own markers. Tuning those constants can never fix it (and made it worse); the date axis must be authoritative. Both renderers now PIN x to the date anchor each frame and let the other forces spread nodes vertically only." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:entity-types ;
    pred:open-question "Undated nodes on a timeline — UNDECIDED, Matt's call. When timeSource is 'event', a node with no recognized temporal predicate currently falls back to its statement createdAt (INGESTION time), so an undated fact is drawn at a real-looking date and is indistinguishable from a genuinely dated one. The builders already have an 'undated' lane (x=0, dropped below the axis) but the fallback pre-empts it. Options: (a) keep the ingestion fallback, (b) route undated nodes to the undated lane when timeSource='event', (c) hide them. Not guessed — asked." ;
    pred:principle "Some concepts are best seen by RELATION (the current force modes); others by LOCATION. A place with GPS coordinates has a true position — the graph should be able to honour it, and show real physical distance, without forcing every other node onto a map." ;
    pred:open-question "Portable plain-text location format — UNDECIDED. Must be plain text and easily portable. Accept multiple input formats (decimal lat/lon, DMS, geo: URIs, maybe WKT/GeoJSON-as-text) and normalise; the graph assumes bare GPS = Earth surface. Which canonical predicate(s) — reuse p:coordinates, or a typed geo predicate?" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:all-previews-modifier ;
    pred:feature-id "F59" ;
    pred:has-status "speculative" ;
    pred:has-part kb:camera-angle-control ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:open-question "Earth model: how much geodesy is worth it (great-circle distance + a globe mesh) vs. a simple projected plane for small extents? Solar-system / space scale is a stretch goal." ;
    rdfs:label "Spatial / map force layout" ;
    pred:has-part kb:timeline-undated-lane .

