kb:trig-published-side
    pred:description "A named graph is a PUBLICATION: a set of facts attributed to an accountable publisher, at a time, under a policy. That attribution is what converts a self-asserted source into real provenance — because a publisher can be checked, cited, disputed, and held responsible, and a private file cannot. This is where TriG earns its keep, and it is a DIFFERENT PRODUCT from the private side: enterprise, institutional, governed." ;
    pred:decided "PUBLISHING emits TriG and REQUIRES owner/publisher information — a publication with no attributed publisher is a contradiction, so publishing is REFUSED until owner/publisher is set (this gates kb:publish-safety-gate). Refines the F75 'Turtle out' heuristic: the trigger for TriG is PUBLISHING, not source-count — a published graph is TriG even from a single source, because the named graph now carries the PUBLISHER, not merely the source. Private export stays TTL (kb:ttl-private-side). (Matt, 2026-07-15.) Decided spec, not yet wired: publisher identity + the publish-time gate are unbuilt (see remaining)." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:enterprise-3ps ;
    pred:part-of kb:ttl-trig-split ;
    pred:remaining "Publisher identity as a first-class entity; publication as an event (who, when, under what policy); the publish-time gate that refuses to publish without owner/publisher; RBAC + People/Policy/Procedure overlay (kb:enterprise-3ps); a distinct market position and marketing angle from the private side." ;
    rdfs:label "TriG — the published side (accountable provenance)" ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Concept> ;
    pred:principle "The named graph is not 'my note'. It is 'published by X, at time T, under policy P'. That sentence is the provenance; the quad is merely how we store it." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:publish-safety-gate .

kb:integration-orchestration
    pred:open-question "How much orchestration lives IN Reckons.AI vs. delegated to n8n? Lean: model intent + simple schedules in-app, delegate complex multi-service flows to the user's n8n (its job), per the n8n-is-not-a-backend principle (F20)." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:n8n-cloud-sync ;
    pred:feature-id "F64" ;
    pred:principle "Integrations must be INTERCONNECTABLE, with clearly definable trigger events, dependencies, and schedules — not just fire-and-forget. Example: a blogger's web page publishes ONLY AFTER the Facebook post is drafted. Order and timing are first-class." ;
    pred:has-status "speculative" ;
    rdfs:label "Integration orchestration & scheduling" ;
    pred:description "Let a user define trigger events, dependencies BETWEEN integration actions, and schedules — so cross-integration flows are explicit and ordered (publish-after-draft, post-then-notify, nightly-sync). Two layers: (a) the user's own n8n owns the heavy multi-step automation (its native strength); (b) the app models the intent — trigger/dependency/schedule as graph facts — and hands it to n8n or runs light in-app schedules. Scheduling exists in Claude Code routines (CronCreate/ScheduleWakeup) as a pattern; here it is user-facing, per-graph. Plugs into the Integration Plugin SDK (F61) so any plug-in exposes trigger/action hooks." ;
    pred:depends-on kb:integration-plugin-sdk ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> .

kb:tier-claude-code
    pred:stays-here "Architecture and design decisions; roadmap/TTL plan edits; triaging pending proposals; writing the script- and agent-tier harnesses themselves; any change that lands in main." ;
    pred:cost "cloud/session tokens — the scarce resource the other two tiers exist to protect" ;
    pred:hardware-bound "Long-context synthesis (>32k of cross-file context); frontier coding judgment; final review of a local model's proposals. Local VRAM ceiling makes these infeasible offline, so they are NOT candidates for demotion." ;
    rdfs:label "Tier 3 — Claude Code (Opus)" ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Concept> ;
    pred:emits "code that lands, plan/TTL edits, and triage decisions on the queue the other tiers fill" ;
    <urn:reckons:nav/order> "3"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:runs-on "Anthropic cloud; no local hardware ceiling" ;
    pred:part-of kb:work-tiering ;
    pred:admits-when "The task exceeds local hardware, or needs cross-file architectural reasoning, or decides process, or writes code that lands." ;
    pred:description "Some work stays here permanently and that is correct — it is not a failure to offload. The binding constraint is HARDWARE: local models are capped by VRAM (this workstation tops out around a 33B model at Q4), so long-context synthesis across many files, high-fidelity vision, and frontier-quality coding judgment have no local equivalent. Claude Code also OWNS the process: it designs the harnesses the other two tiers run inside, decides the routing, and reviews their output. Delegation is downward-only — Opus decides, the lower tiers execute." .

kb:async-backlog
    pred:tested-by "scripts/agent/__tests__/triage.test.ts" ;
    pred:has-file "scripts/agent/backlog.ts" ;
    rdfs:label "Phase 6 — one classifier, three channels (the real backlog)" ;
    pred:decided "Matt's framing (2026-07-16): the backlog is 'not-confidently-answered open items that have been CARRIED OVER and/or are BLOCKING something' — so carried-over (age) and blocking are first-class, and a stale item that blocks nothing is a drop candidate, not a standing question." ;
    <urn:reckons:nav/order> "6"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:part-of kb:async-orchestration ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Phase> ;
    pred:has-file "scripts/agent/triage.ts" ;
    pred:description "The pending queue was a junk drawer — 83 'questions' of which ZERO were a decision Matt had to make (triaged 2026-07-16): 36 were history-lessons drift-warnings/observations, 17 self-duplicating graph-lint stats, 19 competitor license verdicts, 7 code-review proposals, 2 real fixes. The fix is ONE shared classifier (scripts/agent/triage.ts, extracted from orchestrate.ts so the desk and the orchestrator can never disagree) sorting every item into THREE channels: RE-DERIVABLE (a check regenerates it every run — never a queue item, fix the source), REMEDIABLE (a known job clears the whole cluster — becomes an agent TASK), JUDGMENT (genuinely needs a human — the real backlog). The DESK (F80 p5) now shows only isDeskQuestion = a judgment item of type 'question' (a decision that, answered, unblocks an agent); observations/suggestions/stats are filtered at read time. `npm run backlog` is the human lens: judgment ITEMS ranked by BLOCKING (what they hold up, kpred:blocks) then AGE (how many sweeps carried over, 🕸 stale at 7d+). Emitters fixed at source: code-review + competitor-scan now emit 'suggestion', not 'question'. Effect: desk 83 -> 28 at read time with no data deleted; the 28 are pre-existing mis-typed entries awaiting an explicit prune (Matt's call — a shared-queue delete)." ;
    pred:has-status "functional" ;
    pred:remaining "Explicit prune command Matt runs (delete the pre-existing type:question entries from competitor-scan/code-review now re-typed at source; back up first — an agent must not auto-delete the shared queue). Populate kpred:blocks on more questions so the blocking lane is real, not empty. Fold `npm run backlog` into the routine's report." .

