kb:capability-awareness
    rdfs:label "Capability awareness — what you can do now, and what you are missing" ;
    pred:principle "MUTE IS PERMANENT AND COSTS NOTHING. A user who declines once must not be asked again, and declining must never degrade the local path or hide a capability behind the prompt. The local open-source route stays a first-class answer, not a downgraded one used to motivate an upsell." ;
    pred:honest-note "PLANNED, NOTHING BUILT (2026-07-30). There is no capability model, no purpose taxonomy (F116), no benchmark harness comparing a local path to an integrated one, and no referral relationship with anybody. The measurement discipline above is a design constraint written before the money exists, which is the only time it can be written honestly." ;
    pred:feature-id "F117" ;
    pred:description "Matt (2026-07-30): tell the user what their ACTIVE integrations enable, and what a task would look like with a service they have not connected — deep web scraping without Firecrawl, or building an app without Claude Code or Codex. Factual, mutable, never salesy. Possible referral monetization, approached carefully." ;
    pred:scope-decision "MEASURED, NOT ASSERTED — and where measurement is impossible, SAY SO rather than estimate. A user without a Firecrawl key cannot have the comparison run for them, so the honest output is 'this ran locally and produced N results; we have not measured the alternative on YOUR task', not a number borrowed from a vendor's marketing. This is also the commercially stronger position: a measured claim is defensible and a borrowed one is not." ;
    pred:depends-on kb:integration-purposes ;
    pred:decided "DISCLOSE EVERY REFERRAL AT THE POINT OF THE CLAIM, not in a terms page. If a suggestion carries a referral, the suggestion says so where it is made. An undisclosed one turns every honest capability report in the product into something the user is right to doubt." ;
    pred:remaining "A capability model per purpose (what is connected, what it enables); a local-vs-integrated benchmark harness reusing the tests/bench infrastructure; a report that states measured deltas and explicitly names what was NOT measured; permanent mute; and referral disclosure at the point of claim if referrals are ever taken." ;
    pred:has-status "planned" ;
    pred:principle "A RECOMMENDATION WE EARN FROM IS THE THESIS POINTED AT OURSELVES. 'An unverifiable claim, made by the party it benefits, is not evidence' — and with a referral attached, Reckons.AI IS the party that benefits. The only defensible version of this feature is a MEASURED one: run the task locally, run it with the integration where the user already has access, and report the observed difference. An asserted performance claim carrying a referral link is precisely the thing this product exists to refuse, and shipping it would cost more credibility than the referral could ever return." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:honest-status ;
    pred:relates-to kb:thesis ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> .

kb:type-shape-coupling
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:remaining "A shape editor in the entity-types settings page seeded by entityTypeToShape; per-graph storage of user shapes so they travel with a graph package; and the in-app gate from F128 without which a user shape validates nothing." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:entity-types ;
    pred:decided "COUPLED IN AUTHORING, SEPARABLE IN THE MODEL. sh:targetClass already points a shape at a class and an entity type IS a class, so no new link is needed. Welding them one-to-one would break three real cases: ONE TYPE WITH SEVERAL SHAPES (strict for publishing, lenient for drafting, or differing sh:severity — 'warn here, block there' becomes unsayable); SHAPES THAT TARGET NO CLASS AT ALL (sh:targetNode and sh:targetSubjectsOf are Core SHACL); and SHARED GRAPHS, where a package may arrive carrying shapes for types the receiver never defined, or types whose shapes live in someone else's graph. Requiring the two to travel as one object makes a shared contract un-shippable, which is the whole argument for shapes-as-data." ;
    pred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/type-shapes.ts" ;
    pred:part-of kb:shape-validation ;
    pred:feature-id "F128.1" ;
    pred:has-status "scaffolded" ;
    pred:decided "DERIVATION NEVER PROMOTES 'SUGGESTED' TO 'REQUIRED', and this is the property that makes it safe to offer unprompted. schemaPredicates is a hint that existing data was never checked against; turning each into sh:minCount 1 would declare every node omitting an optional field invalid the moment the user first opened the shape editor. A validation feature whose first act is to condemn the user's existing graph is one they switch off. The derived shape states the PATHS and constrains nothing — verified by a test that runs the derived shape against a bare typed node and asserts it CONFORMS, and a paired test asserting it fails once minCount is added deliberately." ;
    rdfs:label "Custom entity types carry custom SHACL shapes" ;
    pred:honest-note "SCAFFOLDED: the derivation, serialization and safety property are built and tested (12 tests), and NOTHING IN THE APP CALLS IT. The entity-types settings page does not offer shape authoring, no shape is stored per graph, and the in-app validation gate that would give a user-authored shape any effect does not exist (F128). This is the on-ramp, not the road." ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/rdf> ;
    pred:principle "REPORT THE GAP, DO NOT FILL IT SILENTLY. typesWithoutShapes lists types with no shape rather than minting one for each: filling a user's graph with empty shapes they never asked for is the opposite of letting the user decide what their graph promises." ;
    pred:description "Matt, 2026-08-14: 'custom entity types should be coupled with custom SHACL shapes?' They already are, weakly — this makes the coupling real and gives users an on-ramp into authoring shapes." ;
    pred:measured "EntityTypeDef ALREADY CARRIES A SHAPE WITHOUT TEETH. `schemaPredicates` is documented as 'suggested predicates for nodes of this type', and measured 2026-08-14 it is ADVISORY ONLY: rendered in the entity-types settings page and used to suggest fields in the detail panel, enforced nowhere. A SHACL NodeShape is the rigorous version of the same statement, so the question is not whether to couple them but how tightly." ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/type-shapes.test.ts" .

kb:docs-consolidation
    pred:proof "Meta-graph added 2026-07-30: datasetOverlaps() derives which datasets assert the SAME entity, answering \"how do my graphs inter-relate\" from the data rather than from a hand-written claim — a hand-maintained overlap assertion is exactly the unverifiable claim the project's thesis rejects. Measured on the real corpus: 17 entities are asserted in two graphs (features<->starter-guide 8, triples-rdf<->starter-guide 4, llm<->starter-guide 2, and three pairs of 1), confirming what docs-pages.ts documents about starter-guide.ttl holding summary stubs. Deliberately NOT emitted as void:Linkset: a Linkset describes triples whose subject is in one dataset and object in another, whereas two datasets independently asserting facts about the same IRI is a different relation, and a standard term meaning something else would be worse than no term. 36 unit tests across composition and the reader." ;
    pred:has-file "scripts/offline/docs-consolidate.ts" ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/publish/__tests__/website-graph.test.ts" ;
    pred:principle "Consolidating 269 pages into eight is an EDITORIAL act, and the only difference between 'curated' and 'we lost 250 pages of content' is whether the leftovers are visible. So composition always reports what no page claimed, and npm run docs:consolidate exits non-zero when anything would be dropped. Publishing fewer pages is a decision; doing it silently is data loss with a tidier sidebar." ;
    pred:remaining "Wire composePages into scripts/docs-pages.ts behind the same --check discipline md-align already uses; render a composed page (sections grouped by source graph, with the page's purpose as its lede); decide whether an entity claimed by two pages should duplicate or be assigned; and delete the 269 generated per-entity files once the composed pages replace them." ;
    pred:remaining "POLISH, from Matt's first read of the composed site (2026-07-30): SECTION VARIETY — every composed section renders identically (heading + definition), and cards, callouts and richer layouts are not expressible in the design graph yet, which is F111's design-controls half. LINKS INTO THE GRAPH VIEW — a page cannot link a reader to the interactive graph its content came from, and the deep link genuinely does not exist: the app's ?kb= parameter names a local Dexie database, not one of the published docs graphs, so emitting one today would be a broken link. Cross-links BETWEEN docs pages are done (derived from skos:related, 38 links across 8 pages, recomputed every build so they cannot rot)." ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/publish> ;
    pred:has-file "static/website.ttl" ;
    pred:part-of kb:graph-publishing ;
    pred:honest-note "A CHECK THAT WAS CONFIDENTLY WRONG, caught by disbelieving it (2026-07-30). The meta-graph first reported \"no dataset shares an entity with another\" — because the corpus reader deduped entities by IRI before the overlap analysis ran, destroying the only evidence that analysis needs. docs-pages.ts documents the opposite in its own module header, which is what made the clean result suspect. Per-graph assertions and the deduped view are now kept separate. Determinism buys \"the rule fired\", never \"the rule was right\"." ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/publish/__tests__/page-composition.test.ts" ;
    pred:honest-note "THE PROPOSED SITE IS NOT THE LIVE SITE (2026-07-30). scripts/docs-pages.ts still publishes one page per entity and content/ still holds 271 files; nothing rendered by /docs has changed. What exists is the mechanism, the proposed architecture in static/docs-site.ttl, and a report that proves the proposal loses nothing. Wiring composition into the generator — and therefore deleting 269 content files — is a deliberate, reviewed change that has not been made, and the eight-page split is an EDITORIAL proposal that is Matt's to accept or redraw, not mine to impose." ;
    pred:has-file "src/lib/publish/page-composition.ts" ;
    pred:decided "ONE HONEST DEPARTURE FROM STANDARD (2026-07-30): there is no standard term for \"this entity subtree is deliberately not published, and here is why\". schema:creativeWorkStatus and dcterms:accessRights describe a resource's own state, not an editorial rule about a set of them, so exclusions use a local urn:reckons:page/excludes-under with dcterms:description carrying the reason. Bending a standard term into meaning something it does not would be a more expensive mistake than a clearly-labelled local one." ;
    pred:description "scripts/docs-pages.ts publishes one page per ENTITY: any subject with an rdf:type under urn:kbase:type/ becomes its own file. 269 entities therefore produce 269 pages, alphabetical, with no editorial layer — /docs measured 23,407px tall with 815 anchors. This inverts it: a PAGE is authored in static/docs-site.ttl and DECLARES its sources (a graph, a named entity, or a type filter), and composition gathers the matching entities into it. One page can source from SEVERAL graphs, which is the thing resolveHomeFile() cannot express — it awards each entity to one owning file and DROPS the other's assertions rather than merging them." ;
    pred:depends-on kb:webpage-entity ;
    pred:principle "An EXCLUSION is not an ORPHAN. An orphan is an entity nobody decided about and stays reported until someone does; an exclusion is a decision carrying its reason, so the next person can tell whether it still holds. Composition refuses an exclusion with no stated reason." ;
    pred:decided "SVELTIA REFINES THE HAND-AUTHORED PAGES, NOT THE COMPOSED ONES (Matt, 2026-07-30). The landing page and About are NOT replaced. The seam is the generated tag: composed pages carry generated:\"docs-composed\" and are changed by editing the GRAPH, because the next build overwrites anything typed into them; hand-authored pages carry no tag, are skipped by both generators, still counted by website:align, and are safe to edit by hand or through the CMS. content/project/how-this-site-is-built.md is the worked example and says so on the page. What happens when someone edits a COMPOSED page to add their voice is F110 and is not solved." ;
    pred:has-file "src/lib/publish/website-graph.ts" ;
    pred:proof "Scaffolded 2026-07-30: 20 unit tests on the pure core, and npm run docs:consolidate runs against the real corpus — 269 entities compose into 8 pages (Start here 26, What it does 54, How it works 48, Working with AI models 9, Connecting other systems 33, Working safely 20, Where it is going 28, Contributing 29), 22 markdown-migration-tracker entries deliberately excluded with a stated reason, and ZERO orphans. Full suite 1,485 tests, 0 type errors, align green, graph-lint 0 errors." ;
    pred:has-status "scaffolded" ;
    pred:honest-note "A CONTENT BUG THE VOLUME WAS HIDING: 16 pages titled like 'GUIDE.md -> Superseded' and 4 titled 'Gap: …' are internal bookkeeping about this repository's own docs migration, published as public documentation on /docs. They are covered by the migration-tracker exclusion here, but they are live on the site until composition ships — that is a smaller, separable fix worth making on its own." ;
    pred:decided "STANDARD RDF VOCABULARIES, ONE COHESIVE WEBSITE GRAPH (Matt, 2026-07-30). An earlier draft minted bespoke urn:reckons:page/sources-graph terms; static/website.ttl now uses the vocabularies that already exist for this, which is also the house style since dcterms, foaf, owl, prov and skos are already in use across static/*.ttl. schema:WebSite / schema:WebPage for the site and its pages; schema:hasPart / schema:isPartOf for structure, read from BOTH ends so a graph stating only one does not lose pages; schema:isBasedOn (\"a resource from which this work is derived\") as THE source relation, repeatable so one page derives from several datasets; schema:about for subject matter; schema:abstract for the page's purpose; schema:position for order; void:Dataset for each source graph with dcterms:source carrying the file. Everything lives in ONE file so the site can be seen and managed as a whole rather than inferred from 269 generated files. Vocabulary knowledge is confined to src/lib/publish/website-graph.ts so page-composition.ts stays vocabulary-agnostic and its rules are testable without a parser." ;
    pred:feature-id "F27.3" ;
    rdfs:label "Docs consolidation — a page declares the graphs it is made from" ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> .

kb:co-orchestration
    rdfs:label "Co-orchestration — Shelly and Claude Code either side of the user" ;
    pred:description "Two agents, one graph, and a human between them. Claude Code holds the CODE, where truth is verifiable and no one need be asked. Shelly holds the CONVERSATION, where truth lives in the user and can only be attested. Neither is the orchestrator; the graph is, and all three drain it. Shelly is not being grown into a coding agent — it is being given the skills that only make sense with a person in the room." ;
    pred:feature-id "F88" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:agent-orchestration ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:principle "SHELLY IS NOT A SMALLER CLAUDE CODE. It is the agent that can ask. Making it write code would trade the one advantage it has — the user is present — for a capability that is already covered better elsewhere." ;
    pred:principle "AN AGENT MUST NOT ASK WHAT IT CAN VERIFY, AND MUST NOT ASSERT WHAT IT CANNOT. Asking the user something the repo already answers wastes the scarcest resource in the system. Asserting something only the user could know is a fabrication with good manners." ;
    pred:has-phase kb:verifiability-axis ;
    pred:relates-to kb:partial-facts ;
    pred:principle "Where there is no source of truth, say so. A well-formed absence — subject and predicate known, object '?' — is the most useful node in the graph, and it is the only honest thing to write when nobody knows the answer yet." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:shelly-chat ;
