kb:int-odf
    rdfs:label "LibreOffice / ODF (OpenDocument) interchange" ;
    pred:feature-id "F120" ;
    pred:honest-note "SPECULATIVE, NOTHING BUILT (2026-07-31). No ODF reader, no zip/XML handling for documents, and no Collabora or Nextcloud Office code. The claim that Nextcloud Office is Collabora-based is recorded from general knowledge as of the May 2026 cutoff and is worth confirming before it is repeated to a user." ;
    pred:open-question "Does an ODS import stop at read-only, or round-trip? Writing a valid .ods that LibreOffice opens without complaint is meaningfully harder than reading one, and the round-trip is only worth it if the graph ever becomes the write master — which F118 currently recommends against." ;
    pred:has-status "speculative" ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Tool> ;
    pred:scope-decision "LIBREOFFICE IS A FORMAT TO US, NOT A SERVICE. It is a desktop application with no network API a browser tab can call; there is no 'connect your LibreOffice' the way there is for a self-hosted n8n or Grist server. So the integration is the OPEN FORMAT — .ods and .odt, which are zipped XML and therefore readable and writable in the browser with no native dependency — reached through the folder sync and file paths that already exist (F56/F57). Calling this a LibreOffice integration in the UI would overclaim a connection we do not have." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:structured-data ;
    pred:description "Matt (2026-07-31) names LibreOffice alongside Grist and Nextcloud. Recording the shape it can actually take here, because it is not the same shape as the other two." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:drive-folder-sync ;
    pred:evidence "NEXTCLOUD ALREADY DELIVERS MOST OF THE LIVE-EDITING VALUE HERE, with no LibreOffice-specific code: Nextcloud Office is Collabora, which is LibreOffice served over the web, and it sits behind the same self-hosted instance F63 already targets for files, calendar and contacts. So the sequencing is F63 first, ODF import as the offline path beside it, and no separate LibreOffice connector at all." ;
    pred:decided "WE CAN READ VALUES AND FORMULA TEXT; WE CANNOT EVALUATE THEM. An .ods cell stores both its last computed value and its formula, so an importer can faithfully record what the sheet said and what rule produced it — and must present the value as of the file's last save, not as a live figure. Anything that needs recalculation belongs in the tool that owns the file, which is exactly the F118 boundary." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:int-nextcloud ;
    pred:provides "open-format spreadsheet and document interchange" .

kb:prose-as-fact
    pred:principle "A CONFLICT HERE IS ALWAYS THE AUTHOR'S TO SETTLE (F88 verifiability = user, F52 boundary). An agent may PROPOSE a rewording and must never apply one: silently rewriting someone's voice to match the graph is a worse failure than the stale sentence, because the author would never learn their words had changed." ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 3 — section-level regeneration: make the SECTION the unit, so graph-derived sections can refresh while authored sections are untouched. Composition already emits sections grouped by source graph (page-composition.ts), so the seam exists. A regeneration becomes a DIFF PROPOSED FOR REVIEW rather than an overwrite — the same shape every other change in this app takes." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:open-question "When the author rewords a sentence to resolve a conflict, is the OLD wording superseded or kept? Keeping it makes the page's own history queryable — what we used to claim, and when it stopped being true — which is unusually valuable for a project whose thesis is about evidence. It also grows the graph with prose nobody will read again." ;
    pred:honest-note "NOTHING OF THIS IS BUILT (2026-07-30). What exists is the enforcement half for GENERATED prose (F51 generation-grounding.ts, scaffolded) and a shape-only drift check (scripts/md-align.ts), which explicitly does NOT prove truth — its own header says it verifies a file is internally consistent with the export format, not that its frontmatter matches anyone's live graph. So today a page can be perfectly aligned, fully regenerable, and stating something false, and every check in this repository passes." ;
    pred:scope-decision "THREE KINDS OF DEPENDENCY, and the last two are the hard and valuable ones. (1) VALUE — \"Matthew Roe\" breaks if that value changes; a plain triple watch catches it. (2) CARDINALITY — \"solo\", \"the only\", \"three integrations\" break when a member is ADDED or REMOVED; the sentence depends on a COUNT, not a value. (3) ABSENCE — \"no backend\", \"no sync through us\", \"we store nothing\" break when something APPEARS. Absence claims are the most common in honest marketing copy and the most dangerous when they rot, and they are exactly what this graph is built to hold (kb:mission — the shape of what is missing). A watcher that only diffs values will miss (2) and (3) entirely and report a clean page." ;
    pred:decided "STANDARD VOCABULARY (following Matt's 2026-07-30 call on website.ttl): the prose-to-facts link is prov:wasDerivedFrom — PROV-O is already used in this project and \"derived from\" is precisely the relation. Cardinality and absence assumptions have no standard term and will need clearly-labelled local predicates, declared as such rather than bent out of a standard one." ;
    pred:feature-id "F110" ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 1 — claims as facts: parse an authored page into sentence-level claims; let the author attach prov:wasDerivedFrom citations (assisted, never automatic, since a wrong citation is worse than none); store the claim + its wording as statements so the page's prose lives in the graph beside what it asserts." ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 4 — watch the actual page: detect hand edits arriving via Sveltia or a direct commit and offer to adopt them as claims, so editing outside the app enriches the graph instead of drifting from it. This is the piece that makes the CMS a first-class author rather than a fork of the truth." ;
    rdfs:label "Prose as fact — authored sentences are claims the graph keeps checking" ;
    pred:description "Matt (2026-07-30): a sentence a human writes on a page IS A STATEMENT, and should be treated as one. Today content/*.md is either regenerated from the graph or hand-edited outside it, and the two cannot coexist: kb:gp-authoring's recorded 2026-07-03 decision is that edits to generated pages are OVERWRITTEN by the next regeneration, by design. That was coherent while nobody was meant to hand-edit a generated page. The moment the author's VOICE is the point, the premise breaks. This feature makes authored prose a first-class citizen of the graph: the sentence keeps its wording, declares the facts it depends on, and is re-checked whenever those facts change." ;
    pred:depends-on kb:review-anchored-generation ;
    pred:open-question "Does an authored claim that CONTRADICTS the graph block publishing, or publish with the conflict recorded? Blocking makes the site a hostage to an unreviewed triple; publishing anyway means the site can knowingly state something the graph denies. Matt's call — and note kb:publish-safety-gate already refuses rather than silently dropping, which is the precedent." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:verifiability-axis ;
    pred:depends-on kb:docs-consolidation ;
    pred:scope-decision "REUSE F51's CONTRACT RATHER THAN INVENT ONE. generation-grounding.ts already enforces that every GENERATED sentence cites at least one CONFIRMED statement, and validates that the citations exist and are confirmed. Authored prose needs the identical contract pointed the other way: the human writes the sentence and declares what it rests on. That makes F51 the enforcer for both origins and keeps one rule rather than two that drift." ;
    pred:has-status "planned" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:control-model ;
    pred:principle "TWO DIFFERENT LOSSES, TWO DIFFERENT MECHANISMS. Regeneration threatens the VOICE; graph drift threatens the ASSERTION. Conflating them is why the current design has to choose between a stale page and a destroyed one. Voice is protected by making the SECTION, not the file, the unit of regeneration. Assertion is protected by re-checking the claim, which must keep working even when nobody edits anything." ;
    pred:principle "PROSE IS NOT EXEMPT FROM REVIEW BECAUSE A HUMAN WROTE IT WELL. The thesis applied to our own website: an unverifiable claim, made by the party it benefits, is not evidence — and the party that benefits most from Reckons.AI's landing copy is Reckons.AI. A sentence that cannot be traced to a fact is exactly the thing kb:honest-status exists to catch, and writing it by hand rather than generating it does not make it true." ;
    pred:part-of kb:graph-publishing ;
    pred:scope-decision "THE WORKED EXAMPLE, from Matt, is the specification. \"Reckons.AI is the solo development project of Matthew Roe through Data Insight Solutions\" is the exact phrasing he wants — and it becomes UNTRUE if the Data Insight Solutions team expands, without anyone touching the page. Note carefully what breaks: not a value. Nothing contradicts \"Matthew Roe\". The word SOLO asserted a fact about the SHAPE of the graph — that exactly one developer exists — and a second developer falsifies the sentence without contradicting any triple in it. src/lib/rdf/dichotomy.ts cannot see this, because there is no triple-versus-triple disagreement to find." ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 2 — the watcher: re-check every claim when its cited facts change, covering all three dependency kinds. Value drift is a triple comparison; CARDINALITY needs the claim to record the count it assumed; ABSENCE needs it to record the pattern that must stay empty. Output is a pending conflict routed to the author (F88 user lane), never an edit." .

