kb:context-engine
    pred:touches-file "src/lib/integrations/llm/turtle-chat.ts" ;
    pred:remaining "Per-turn retrieval keyed to the request; a measured recall figure before any context is cut; a capture path that proposes triples from judgement and ignores working-out; and the same two on the Claude Code side." ;
    pred:constraint "RETRIEVAL TRADES 'EXPENSIVE BUT COMPLETE' FOR 'CHEAP BUT SILENTLY INCOMPLETE', AND THAT IS THE RISK THAT SINKS THIS IF IGNORED. A long context at least CONTAINS the answer. A query that misses the relevant node produces a confident answer with no signal that anything is missing — and the model cannot tell 'not in the graph' from 'not retrieved'. The existing prompt already says absence is not falsity, but under retrieval that absence becomes dynamic and per-turn. Retrieval quality therefore has to be MEASURED (recall against a known answer set), not assumed, before conversation length is cut." ;
    pred:has-status "planned" ;
    pred:principle "THE CONVERSATION IS NOT THE ASSET; THE JUDGEMENT IN IT IS. Ephemeral working-out — what was tried, what was ruled out mid-thought, the phrasing of a question — is not a fact and should never be captured. What survives a turn is what somebody decided, and that is a triple. This is the RAG-inversion thesis of kb:review-anchored-generation (F51) applied to dialogue rather than to documents." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:passage-grounding ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/rdf> ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/stores> ;
    pred:honest-note "PARTLY BUILT, AND LESS THAN IT LOOKS (verified in code 2026-08-15). Shelly does NOT stuff the whole graph in: buildContextSection sends a fixed sample of FIFTEEN entities plus counts. That is frugal but it is not retrieval — the same fifteen arrive whatever was asked. query_kb exists but is user-driven, requiring the reader to click run. add_triple exists and is confirmed directly on accept, so 'conversation can accept facts' is genuinely half-built. Nothing clears context, nothing distinguishes ephemeral from durable, and the Claude Code side captures nothing at all — every decision recorded in this graph today was typed by hand." ;
    pred:touches-file "src/lib/embed.ts" ;
    pred:estimated-files "12"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:priority "high" ;
    rdfs:label "Context engine — retrieval per request, not accumulated conversation" ;
    pred:estimated-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:open-question "How is 'relevant' computed? BM25 already exists (kb_search), embeddings already exist (BGE-small via embed.ts), and kb_compress already does subgraph selection with a token budget — the pieces are present and unassembled. Whether the retrieval key is the last message, the running topic, or the selected set/graph is undecided." ;
    pred:description "Stop carrying the conversation and start querying the graph. Each turn retrieves the nodes relevant to THAT request from the selected graph, answers from them, captures any judgement reached as triples, and lets the rest go. Applies to Shelly and to Claude Code sessions alike — both currently pay to re-read everything they have ever said." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:review-anchored-generation ;
    pred:decided "NEVER SUMMARISE THE HUMAN SIDE — KEEP IT VERBATIM. At 5,839 tokens for a whole session it is affordable in full, and it is exactly where compression does the most damage: 'collage wasn't a force, it was a force modifier' is eleven words that redirected an entire feature, and no triple carries that. Boil down the ASSISTANT's output, which is 85% of the context and largely re-derivable." ;
    pred:measurement "MEASURED ON THIS SESSION, 2026-08-15, not estimated. Weighted cost was 77% CACHE READS — re-reading the accumulated context on every turn — against 8% output and ~0% fresh input. Of the context itself, ~85% was the assistant's own accumulated output, 13% tool-result text, 2% images, and 0.35% (5,839 tokens across 35 turns) was the human. So carrying the conversation is the cost, and the human's words are the cheapest, most valuable part of it." ;
    pred:feature-id "F135" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:graph-sets ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/llm> ;
    pred:depends-on kb:context-compression .

