kb:verifiability-axis
    pred:has-status "scaffolded" ;
    pred:description "Every fact gains kpred:verifiable-by, naming what would settle it: `code` (a file, a symbol — a script can check it), `test` (a suite asserts it), `source` (a cited passage in an ingested document), `user` (attested by the person; nothing else backs it), or `unknown` (nobody has established this, and pretending otherwise is the failure). This is what lets an agent ROUTE: a `code` fact belongs to Claude Code and the script tier and should never be put to a human; a `user` fact belongs to Shelly and must never be quietly invented." ;
    rdfs:label "Phase 1 — record HOW a fact could be checked" ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Phase> ;
    pred:principle "UNCLASSIFIED FAILS TOWARD THE HUMAN. A fact whose verifiability nobody has established is never machine-approved. An unclassified fact silently auto-accepted at machine speed is the precise quiet failure this axis exists to prevent." ;
    pred:honest-note "SCAFFOLDED, not functional — and the distinction is the point. The axis exists and is tested (17 tests): Statement.verifiableBy, inferVerifiability(), competentGate(), requiresUserAuthority(), gateFor(). NOTHING CONSUMES IT YET. The review queue still puts every fact in front of the user regardless of who is competent to judge it. Calling this `functional` would be exactly the overclaim kb:honest-status forbids." ;
    pred:remaining "kpred:verifiable-by on the statement model; render it in the review queue; graph-lint check that a `code`-verifiable fact actually names a file that exists." ;
    pred:has-file "src/lib/rdf/verifiability.ts" ;
    pred:principle "VERIFIABLE IS NOT APPROVABLE. A script can verify that a principle IS WRITTEN; it cannot approve that it is the RIGHT principle. The roadmap and the tenets are DECISIONS — the user is their author, not their auditor — so authority OVERRIDES verifiability, however checkable the fact happens to be. Conversely `kpred:has-status \"functional\"` is a claim ABOUT THE CODE, and putting it to the user invites them to rubber-stamp their own wishes; a machine should check that one." ;
    pred:example "In the Reckons.AI repo almost every fact is `code` or `test` — which is exactly why this project can be aggressively automated, and why a user's business graph cannot. The tooling must not assume its own conditions generalize." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:claim-settlement ;
    pred:principle "Provenance answers WHERE a fact came from. Verifiability answers WHETHER ANYONE COULD CHECK IT. They are different questions, and the second is the one that decides who should be handling the fact." ;
    <urn:reckons:nav/order> "1"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:part-of kb:co-orchestration ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/verifiability.test.ts" .

