kb:structured-data
    pred:principle "THE EMPTY CELL IS ALREADY OUR BEST PRIMITIVE, and this is the argument for structured data belonging HERE rather than in a separate app the assistant merely links to. A blank cell in a spreadsheet is ambiguous by construction: zero, not yet known, not applicable, or forgotten — the grid cannot tell you which, and no formula over it can. A partial fact (F32) can: subject known, predicate known, object open, with a question attached and whatever is stalled behind it visible. A budget line with no figure for insurance stops being a hole in a table and becomes the one question worth asking this week." ;
    pred:honest-note "PLANNED, NOTHING BUILT (2026-07-31). There is no tabular support anywhere in the app: 'csv' and 'tsv' appear in TEXT_EXTENSIONS in src/lib/ingest/folder-scan.ts, which means a spreadsheet is read as PLAIN TEXT and handed to an LLM for prose extraction. No columns, no types, no units, no rows with stable identity, no recalculation, and no Grist, LibreOffice or Nextcloud code of any kind. A budget in Reckons.AI today would be sentences." ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 3 — two-way reconcile through the shared kb-import path that local folder sync and Drive sync already use, with the content-hash loop guard, so a table behaves like every other synced artifact instead of growing its own semantics." ;
    pred:feature-id "F118" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:graph-sets ;
    pred:principle "WATCHING IS THE DEFAULT DIRECTION; WRITING IS THE EXCEPTION THAT MUST EARN ITS GATE. Because the table is the master, a write from Reckons.AI is not an edit to our own store — it is a mutation of the user's system of record, which other people and other tools may be reading. Ingest can therefore follow the ordinary review spine (arrive as pending, get accepted or rejected, cost nothing when wrong), while any write path needs the F52 boundary applied at full strength. The asymmetry is deliberate: a bad fact in the graph is reviewable, a bad row in someone's live budget is already out in the world." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:integration-plugin-sdk ;
    pred:decided "THE TABLE OWNS THE ROW, BUT THE GRAPH CAN WATCH IT (Matt, 2026-07-31 — settles the question this entry was opened with). The table is the write master for cells and totals because it is the thing that can recompute them; the graph holds the definition, the entity identity, the provenance and the open questions, and pulls computed results in as facts stamped with the document and revision that produced them. The inverse — graph as master, table as a rendered view — would mean either reimplementing recalculation or shipping stale totals, and was rejected." ;
    pred:open-question "TYPED LITERALS DO NOT CARRY UNITS. xsd:decimal will hold 42.30 and say nothing about pounds, kilowatt-hours, or per-month — and a budget that silently adds two currencies is worse than one that refuses. Options: QUDT for quantity/unit/dimension, schema.org PriceSpecification and MonetaryAmount for money specifically, or a small local unit vocabulary. Standard vocabulary wins by the website.ttl and F113 precedent, but the choice needs making before the first numeric column round-trips." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:integration-purposes ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 4 — reckon over it: a reckoning (Situation-Target-Proposal) that can read a computed total and cite the document and revision it came from, and can name the blank cells as the questions they are." ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 2 — read-only import from ONE source, proving the shape end to end before any write path exists. Cells arrive as pending facts for review like every other ingest, blanks arrive as partial facts, and every derived value carries its source document and revision." ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 1 — a table shape in the graph: a dataset entity per table, a row entity with a stable external id, a predicate per column, and column metadata (type, unit, whether the column is entered or derived). Reuses the F113 member/role vocabulary so a table is a graph-set member rather than a new container type." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:personal-assistant ;
    pred:relates-to kb:entity-sets ;
    rdfs:label "Structured data and repeatable calculation" ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:scope-decision "DO NOT BUILD A SPREADSHEET. No formula engine, no recalculation order, no function library, no cell formatting in the app. That is a decade of work with mature open-source implementations already on the user's machine, and building it would break the integration boundary the same way treating n8n as a general backend would. The division of work is the established one: the app owns the SEMANTIC and VISUAL side — what these rows mean, which entities they are about, what is still unknown — and the connected tool owns the cells and the recompute." ;
    pred:has-status "planned" ;
    pred:depends-on kb:partial-facts ;
    pred:principle "A GRAPH ASSERTS; IT DOES NOT RECALCULATE. A number written into a triple is a snapshot taken at the moment somebody typed it, with no recompute path and nothing that notices when its inputs move. Put a monthly total in the graph and it is quietly wrong the next time a line item changes — and it is wrong in the exact way this project exists to refuse, because it reads as a current fact while being an expired one. Any computed value entering the graph must therefore carry what produced it and from which revision, or it is an unverifiable claim made by the party that benefits, with our own numbers in the dock." ;
    pred:description "Matt (2026-07-31): everyone needs structured data and repeatable calculations sometimes, even in daily life — a household budget is the ordinary case. Reckons.AI is not a complete or effective personal assistant without structured data management. This is the CONCEPT entity the tool integrations serve (kb:int-grist F119, kb:int-odf F120, kb:int-nextcloud F63); it exists so those are alternatives serving one purpose rather than three unrelated connectors." .

kb:source-discovery
    pred:principle "WATCHING A SITE FOR NEW POSTS IS NOT WATCHING A PAGE FOR EDITS, and the two need different diffs. A page is text changing inside one document. A blog is ITEMS APPEARING IN A LIST — which is the same keyed diff as the spreadsheet rows in F121, keyed on permalink instead of a row key, with the same duty to tell a genuinely new item apart from a re-ordered or re-rendered one. One diff engine, two key extractors; the row diff and the feed diff should not be written twice." ;
    pred:feature-id "F123" ;
    pred:estimated-files "7"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:relates-to kb:control-model ;
    pred:relates-to kb:source-subscription ;
    pred:has-status "planned" ;
    rdfs:label "Source discovery — search for sources, then choose what to watch" ;
    pred:description "Matt (2026-07-31): a web SEARCH is a different operation from a subscription. Analyze, enrich and add should be able to crawl and search for new SOURCES; that is a ONE-TIME search with no diff, because there is no prior state to diff against. The user then chooses which of the found sources to add to currents — the example being a blog by an engineer they respect, watched for new posts. Currents polls it and diffs against the retained local copy (F122) before suggesting any pending triples." ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/llm> ;
    pred:relates-to kb:source-reference-copy ;
    pred:relates-to kb:currents ;
    pred:honest-note "PLANNED, NOTHING BUILT (2026-07-31). Tavily search exists and is wired into auto-analyze.svelte.ts, ReAnalysisPanel.svelte and source-validation.ts — but it returns facts, not source candidates. There is no source-candidate review surface, no promote-to-current action, no feed discovery, no robots.txt handling, and no HTTP conditional requests anywhere in src/lib (verified 2026-07-31: no ETag, If-None-Match or If-Modified-Since). kb:source-subscription remains a one-line stub." ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/rdf> ;
    pred:constraint "A FEED IS NOT ALWAYS THE WHOLE POST, and the fallback has to be per-ITEM rather than per-SITE. Many feeds carry summaries or truncated content, so 'is there a new item?' is answered by the feed while 'what does it say?' still needs the article fetched — at which point the F122 ladder applies to that one URL. Treating a feed's presence as meaning no fetching is ever needed would silently extract facts from summaries and attribute them to the post." ;
    pred:constraint "POLLING SOMEONE ELSE'S SITE IS OUTBOUND ACTIVITY AGAINST A THIRD PARTY WHO DID NOT AGREE TO IT. Honor robots.txt and any crawl-delay, identify the client, keep intervals conservative by default, and back off on errors rather than retrying on a timer. A local-first app that quietly turns each user's browser into a crawler has externalized a cost onto people with no say in it, and 'the user configured it' does not settle that." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:enrichment-pipeline ;
    pred:decided "SUBSCRIBING IS A DELIBERATE HUMAN ACT, NEVER A SIDE EFFECT OF SEARCHING. Promoting a discovered source into currents commits the user to recurring network activity against somebody else's site, recurring cost, and recurring triage — three things that must not be acquired by accident. Discovery proposes, the human promotes, which is the F52 boundary applied to obligations rather than to facts." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/stores> ;
    pred:decided "PREFER THE FEED, SCRAPE ONLY WHEN THERE ISN'T ONE (confirmed by Matt, 2026-07-31: RSS is meant to deliver this, and where it exists for a given site it is preferred). RSS and Atom give stable item ids, cheap fetches, an explicit invitation to poll, and an answer to 'is this new?' that needs no extraction at all. src/lib/rdf/currents.ts already types CurrentKind as 'rss' | 'url' | 'topic', so the model is in place. Discovery therefore SNIFFS FOR A FEED on any site it proposes watching — the <link rel=\"alternate\"> declaration, then the conventional paths — and records which mode a subscription is using so the user can see whether they are being polite or scraping. HTML index scraping is the documented fallback for sites without a feed: more fragile, more expensive, and worse manners, so it should never be chosen while a feed exists." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:int-firecrawl ;
    pred:principle "THE OUTPUT OF DISCOVERY IS A SOURCE, NOT A FACT, AND IT NEEDS ITS OWN REVIEW SURFACE. The existing search integration goes straight to facts — src/lib/integrations/search/tavily.ts documents itself as finding 'facts about entities that the KB likely has gaps on' — so 'here are six sites that look relevant to your graph, which do you want to watch?' has nowhere to land today. Conflating the two is what makes a one-time search quietly become a permanent obligation, and it makes a subscription's arrivals get triaged as though they were one-off suggestions." ;
    pred:remaining "A discovery pass that returns SOURCE CANDIDATES with why-relevant provenance; a review surface that promotes a candidate to a current (or discards it); feed sniffing so a promoted site is watched by its feed where one exists; and robots/interval politeness on every recurring fetch." ;
    pred:estimated-deps "0"^^xsd:integer .

kb:enterprise-3ps
    pred:landing-label "People · Policy · Procedure" ;
