kb:int-indico
    pred:provides "event ingest" ;
    pred:has-status "functional" ;
    pred:provides "category sync" ;
    pred:honest-note "RESOLVED 2026-07-31, BOTH HALVES — but the shape of the old failure is the lesson worth keeping. Browser-side operation needed a change at the SERVER that no amount of app code could substitute for: Indico sends no Access-Control-Allow-Origin header, so a browser discarded a response the API had answered correctly. Identical requests returned HTTP 200 from Node/curl and failed in Chromium. The prior 'functional' status was measured only at the CLIENT layer by a Node harness, where neither CSP nor CORS is enforced — so it recorded a true fact about the API and an untrue one about the product. kb:honest-status is about matching the claim to the LAYER it was tested at. STILL TRUE AND STILL A LIMIT: the hosted reckons.ai build cannot widen its CSP for a user's arbitrary server, and any OTHER user's Indico will need the same server-side CORS grant — this integration is inherently self-host-to-self-host." ;
    pred:server-config "The Indico server must grant CORS to the app origin; nothing in the app can substitute for it. Applied 2026-07-31 to indico.data-insight.website in /etc/nginx/conf.d/indico.conf: an nginx `map $http_origin $indico_cors_origin` echoing ONLY known origins (reckons.ai, www.reckons.ai, localhost:5173/5174) — never '*', because the export API can expose non-public data to a bearer token and a wildcard would let any site read whatever the caller's token grants — plus a dedicated `location /export/` block (longest-prefix match beats `location /`, so the grant covers the read-only export API and nothing else in Indico) handling the OPTIONS preflight the Authorization header forces. Verified: preflight 204 with the headers, GET 200 echoing the origin, and an UNKNOWN origin correctly receiving no ACAO." ;
    pred:tested-by "scripts/offline/__tests__/csp-connect-src.test.ts" ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Tool> ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/integrations/indico/__tests__/client.test.ts" ;
    pred:progress "2026-07-23 (follow-up): the live verification is now a REPEATABLE SCRIPT, not a one-off manual check — `npx tsx scripts/offline/indico-verify.ts --server=…` (script tier, zero tokens). Ran 6/6 GREEN against indico.data-insight.website: client construction, auth path (1 event returned, token accepted), error surfacing on a deliberately bad token (independently reproduced the 400 'Malformed API key' that PR #149 diagnosed), error surfacing on a bad event id (400 'Event IDs must be numeric'), category coverage, and RDF mapping (1 event -> 12 statements across 2 subjects). The token is never printed; the harness reports WHICH auth scheme was used, because 'the token was sent' and 'the token was sent the way this server accepts' are different claims and the second was the one silently false before #149." ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/integrations/indico/__tests__/reachability.test.ts" ;
    pred:progress "2026-07-31: the app could never reach ANY Indico server from a browser, and nothing said so. TWO stacked blockers, each invisible: (1) CSP — the app.html <meta> policy lists connect-src literally, and a per-user self-hosted origin can never be in it; the request was refused before being sent. A half-finished fix from the previous session had left the literal token '%RECKONS_EXTRA_CONNECT_SRC%' INSIDE the live connect-src directive, referencing a vite plugin that was never written. Completed as src/hooks.server.ts (NOT a vite plugin — app.html is SvelteKit's template, so transformIndexHtml never runs on it, which is why the plugin computed the right answer and silently applied nothing). scripts/offline/csp-connect-src.ts substitutes ORIGINS ONLY, never paths, because VITE_FEEDBACK_WEBHOOK_URL carries a secret-ish path and the CSP ships publicly. (2) CORS — see honest-note. Both refusals reached the user as the same four words, 'TypeError: Failed to fetch'. src/lib/integrations/indico/reachability.ts now distinguishes them (a securitypolicyviolation listener proves CSP rather than guessing) and names the fix for the layer that actually refused — different machines entirely. Also fixed: the three calendar sub-tabs shared ONE error variable, so a Google OAuth failure ('Missing required parameter client_id') rendered under the Indico panel and read as an Indico fault — it cost hours of chasing the wrong integration. VERIFIED END TO END: against a CORS-enabled stand-in the real UI imports '1 events (12 facts)' and the review badge shows 12, with the app pointed at 127.0.0.1 (deliberately NOT covered by the existing 'http://localhost:*' source, so the substitution itself is exercised). 22 unit tests, mutation-checked three ways; 3 e2e tests drive the real panel." ;
    pred:progress "2026-07-31 (server): upgraded indico.data-insight.website from Indico 3.3.8 to 3.3.12 and granted CORS, closing the last blocker. Debian 12, /opt/indico venv already on Python 3.12.12, so the docs' upgrade-python step was a verified no-op rather than run blind; pinned to 'indico<3.4' so an unattended run could never cross a major line. Three alembic migrations applied (event reminder end_delta, attachment annotations, paper file types). Also applied the nginx change Indico 3.3.10 REQUIRES and which a point upgrade silently depends on — relaying $upstream_http_content_security_policy through the internal /.xsf/indico/ location, which otherwise drops the app's own CSP headers. VERIFIED FROM THE BROWSER, which is the only layer that counts here: a fetch from the app's origin with the Bearer token returns HTTP 200 and 1 event ('Christmas'). Backups kept (pg_dump + the pre-change nginx conf)." ;
    rdfs:label "Indico" ;
    pred:progress "2026-07-23: VERIFIED against a live Indico 3.x server (indico.data-insight.website) and FIXED the auth path that was silently broken. The client sent the API token as the legacy `?ak=` query param; modern Indico PERSONAL tokens (`indp_…`) reject that with 400 'Malformed API key' and must travel in the Authorization: Bearer header. client.ts now routes by token shape (indp_ -> Bearer, else -> ak=), proven by a Bearer GET returning a real event. Also added VITE_INDICO_SERVER_URL / _API_TOKEN / _CATEGORY_ID to .env.example + DEFAULT_TURTLE_SETTINGS. NB: a personal token is NOT an OAuth app — no callback URL is needed (a wrong turn that costs setup time)." ;
    pred:tested-by "tests/e2e/indico-diagnostic.test.ts" ;
    pred:remaining "Deliberately NOT done: routing the request through r.jina.ai/corsproxy.io, which would hand a personal Indico token to a third party and violate the local-first stance; the self-hosted alternative is the user's own n8n (kb:n8n-cloud-sync), which is server-side and has no CORS problem. The hosted reckons.ai build still cannot widen its CSP for a user's arbitrary server — that path needs runtime-configurable origins (an HTTP CSP header per deployment) or the n8n relay, and is unbuilt. RESOLVED 2026-07-31 and now PROVEN rather than assumed: the root category export does NOT recurse into subcategories — eight trip events in category 1 are invisible to /export/categ/0.json, so indicoCategoryId is REQUIRED, not optional, whenever events live below root. Also fixed a silent correctness bug the new events exposed: Indico renders times in the CATEGORY's timezone (US/Central here), not the event's, and reports which zone it used — indicoDateTimeToISO threw that field away, so a 23:00 America/Los_Angeles deadline entered the graph as a bare 01:00, two hours off with nothing recording the zone meant. It now resolves the wall clock plus zone to a UTC instant via Intl (DST-correct on the event's own date), so two sources describing one meeting compare EQUAL. 5 tests, mutation-checked. Superseded note: category-sync coverage beyond root (the test server reports NO categories from the root listing, so this is unproven rather than working), and whether background/periodic sync is wanted vs manual import from the ingest 'calendar' tab. SEPARATELY (bigger, see kb:personal-assistant / the timeline): events want to be a FIRST-CLASS, user-CREATABLE type managed inside Reckons — not only imported — and the timeline layout needs a clearer temporal force. The type (ktype:Event / ktype:CalendarEvent, meta:scheduled-at) and the timeline's date-reading (TIMELINE_PREDICATES_2D includes meta:scheduled-at) ALREADY EXIST; the gaps are in-app event authoring and layout clarity, plus simply having more dated data (the test server holds one event)." .

