kb:graph-db-connectors
    pred:decided "Matt (2026-07-16): Enterprise features must connect to open-source graph databases, with TriG in/out working for several options. Recorded as one-protocol-covers-many (SPARQL GSP) rather than N bespoke connectors." ;
    rdfs:label "Graph-database connectors (TriG in/out to open-source stores)" ;
    pred:feature-id "F84.3" ;
    pred:has-status "speculative" ;
    pred:remaining "A SPARQL 1.1 GSP + Update client (fetch-based; auth via a settings token / basic-auth): export -> PUT named graph, import -> GET / CONSTRUCT. Round-trip TriG test against a local Fuseki or Oxigraph in CI (docker). Neo4j (n10s) + TerminusDB adapters as follow-ons. UI: pick store type + endpoint + credentials in graph settings, exactly like the n8n / Indico integrations. Reuses the existing TriG serialize (F75)." ;
    pred:part-of kb:trig-enterprise-overlay ;
    pred:description "An OPTIONAL, per-user Enterprise integration: sync a Reckons graph in and out of a standard RDF/graph database, so a team's store-of-record and the local-first app speak the same triples. THE KEY ARCHITECTURAL DECISION — do NOT build one connector per product. Most open-source RDF stores implement the SAME two W3C protocols: the SPARQL 1.1 Graph Store HTTP Protocol (GSP) for whole-graph GET/PUT/POST, and SPARQL 1.1 Query/Update for everything else. ONE GSP+SPARQL connector therefore covers Apache Jena Fuseki, RDF4J, Oxigraph, Blazegraph, GraphDB, Virtuoso and QLever at once — 'several options' from a single adapter. TriG is the wire format: its named graphs map directly onto GSP named-graph slots (a published graph carries its PUBLISHER in the 4th position, F84), so TriG-in/out is lossless where a bare-triples POST would drop the graph context. Outliers get thin adapters: Neo4j via the neosemantics (n10s) RDF plugin; TerminusDB via its own document/WOQL API. Stays inside integration-boundaries: opt-in, per-user, behind settings.* and the F61 Integration Plugin SDK — never a hard dependency of the offline-first core." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> .

kb:reviewable-graph-patches
    pred:depends-on kb:document-evidence-anchors ;
    pred:decided "STAGE AUTHORITY. The extractor proposes candidate facts. A critic may identify missing support, inconsistent direction, suspicious object kind or likely vocabulary drift, but it emits diagnostics and replacement proposals, never edits the candidate in place. Deterministic validators may repair only meaning-preserving form such as canonical IRI spelling or datatype syntax, recording before and after; any semantic change is a new visible proposal." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:relates-to kb:review-workbench ;
    pred:feature-id "F136.2" ;
    pred:remaining "DONE WHEN the octopus fixture produces an inspectable insert-only patch; critic findings are visible without changing facts; invalid shape/object/direction cases are attributed to their stage; accepting a subset applies exactly that subset transactionally; stale-base application is refused; and a proposed new predicate cannot become schema without a separate human action." ;
    pred:decided "PATCH CONTRACT. A GraphPatch has patchId, extractionRunId, target graph identity and base fingerprint plus ordered operations. An insert carries a complete pending Statement draft and evidence anchors. A delete must name an existing statement id, its observed base hash and a reason. Every operation carries validator outcomes and critic diagnostics; applying accepted operations re-checks the base fingerprint inside one transaction." ;
    pred:priority "high" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:entity-normalization ;
    pred:depends-on kb:shape-validation ;
    pred:relates-to kb:control-model ;
    rdfs:label "Reviewable graph patches — extract, criticise, validate, then propose" ;
    pred:depends-on kb:extraction-run-ledger ;
    pred:has-status "planned" ;
    pred:part-of kb:vocabulary-grounding ;
    pred:decided "SCHEMA PATCHES ARE SEPARATE FROM FACT PATCHES. A newly minted predicate or SHACL/ontology change is offered in a schema patch with its motivating facts, never applied just to make the same run validate. The fact patch can remain pending against the proposed schema. An extractor may not change the rules that judge its own output." ;
    pred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/semantic-diff.ts" ;
    pred:measurement "Benchmark each layer separately: fact recall, vocabulary agreement, predicate direction, object kind/datatype, excerpt/anchor validity, shape validity, novel-predicate precision, accepted-over-proposed yield, latency and token cost. No blended score may let better recall hide semantic corruption or lost provenance." ;
    pred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/diff.ts" ;
    pred:description "Make an extraction result a bounded GraphPatch rather than an unstructured batch of statements. Existing Diff remains the review presentation and comparison engine; GraphPatch becomes the execution artifact that says exactly which operations were proposed, why, from which run, and which validations each operation survived." ;
    pred:touches-file "src/lib/stores/ingest.svelte.ts" ;
    pred:decided "FIRST RELEASE IS INSERT-ONLY. Document extraction has no authority to delete established knowledge. Delete operations enter only through an explicit refresh/conflict workflow after stale-source behavior is specified, and are always human-settled. This prevents adopting OntoCast's insert/delete vocabulary as accidental deletion authority." .

kb:reference-graph-catalogue
    pred:principle "THE PREREQUISITE IS THE MECHANISM, NOT THE DATA. Writing the format facts is a morning's careful sourcing. Having anywhere to offer them from is the actual feature — today the app can hold exactly one official graph, chosen at build time, so a second reference set has nowhere to appear." ;
    pred:estimated-files "5"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:estimated-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:has-status "planned" ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:part-of kb:content-operations ;
    rdfs:label "Reference graph catalogue — more than one importable graph" ;
    pred:feature-id "F134.1" ;
    pred:description "A manifest of bundled reference graphs (title, description, entity count, checked-on date) that the app can list and import from, replacing the single hardcoded OFFICIAL_KB_FILE. Post formats are the first entry; the starter guide is the second; design principles and any future reference set follow without another code change." ;
    pred:touches-file "src/lib/stores/official-kb.svelte.ts" ;
    pred:decided "THE MANIFEST IS ITSELF A GRAPH, SERVED FROM THE DOCS SITE, WITH A BUNDLED FALLBACK. A catalogue of TTLs described by JSON would put the index in a different format from everything it indexes, for no reason — the entries are entities with a title, a description, a URL, an entity count and a checked-on date, which is a graph. Serving it from the documentation site means new reference sets appear without an app release; shipping a copy in the build means the list still works on a plane. content/ is already generated FROM these graphs by scripts/docs-pages.ttl-driven tooling, so a catalogue page for humans and a manifest for the app come off the same source rather than being maintained twice." ;
    pred:open-question "Import as a NEW graph, or merge into the current one? Merging puts formats beside the user's own content so relationships can be drawn, but mixes reference data with authored data; importing separately keeps provenance clean but pushes the user into cross-graph leaps to use it. The archive/restore and cross-graph-alignment work both bear on this." .

kb:claim-settlement
    pred:has-status "planned" ;
    pred:decided "TWO AXES, NOT A LONGER ENUM. WHAT would settle it (the existing Verifiability) is independent of WHETHER WE CAN RUN THAT SETTLEMENT: 'a cited passage backs it' is decidable when the reference copy was retained (F122) and undecidable when it was not, and the fact has not changed in kind. Proposed second axis — now (a deterministic local check, free, e.g. does this path exist); runnable (a job or suite we own, costing time, e.g. run the test); external (only a third party can settle it, so it carries a source and a checked-on date and it rots — every F134 platform dimension is this); none (no procedure exists, and the honest label is judgement)." ;
    pred:depends-on kb:verifiability-axis ;
    pred:description "The axis F88 introduced answers WHAT KIND of thing would settle a fact (code / test / source / user / unknown / external-graph). It does not answer whether that settlement is available to us, now, at what cost — and it is never recorded, only inferred at read time. This adds the second axis and makes the classification a written property of the statement rather than a guess made later." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:feature-id "F135.2" ;
    pred:decided "SETTLE THE CALCULABLE AUTOMATICALLY; NEVER LET IT SETTLE THE QUALITATIVE. Matt, 2026-08-15: \"High confidence should be automatic, such as ‘this unit test passes’ does not need verification by humans... However, this is a finished feature, likely needs human UX review. Qualitative response, not calculatable.\" So a claim decomposes: the calculable part is settled by running the check and never enters the queue at all, while the qualitative part cannot be settled by any number of green checks and must reach a person. The failure mode is not that we lack the checks — it is letting a passing check IMPLY the judgement it cannot make." ;
    pred:evidence "MEASURED 2026-08-15, and it is worse than it reads. Running inferVerifiability over real graphs: static/knowledge.ttl is 100% 'unknown' (221 of 221 statements, all routed to the user). static/reckons-roadmap.ttl is 95.6% 'unknown' (3,433 of 3,590), with 150 'code' and 7 'test'. So the taxonomy exists, is tested, and classifies almost nothing — the queue asks a human about essentially everything, which is precisely the failure verifiability.ts says in its own header it exists to prevent." ;
    pred:estimated-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:open-question "Is 'external' one class or two? A platform's own spec page and a vendor's marketing claim about its own product are both external, but only one is disinterested. F134 needs the first; competitor-scan already refuses to trust the second." ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/llm> ;
    rdfs:label "Settlement — every claim carries how it could be checked, and whether we can" ;
    pred:part-of kb:context-engine ;
    pred:open-question "Does the EXTRACTOR ask the model to classify, or does a deterministic pass classify afterwards? Asking the model is what forces it to name a falsification method and is the point of the feature — but it also lets a model self-certify its own claim as 'now'-checkable, which is the thing being guarded against. Likely both: the model proposes, a script verifies the proposal by actually running the named check, and disagreement downgrades to unknown. Matt's call." ;
    pred:priority "high" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:fact-trust-tiers ;
    pred:relates-to kb:passage-grounding ;
    pred:honest-note "PLANNED. The F88 axis is BUILT and tested (verifiability.ts, 202 lines, with review-routing.ts, entity-review.ts and agent-edit-boundary.ts consuming it), and the review page reads it. What does not exist: nothing WRITES Statement.verifiableBy anywhere in src/ — the only reference outside the module is archive.ts reading it back — so the class is re-inferred on every read and never recorded, never corrected, and never improved by a human's decision. No extractor emits it. There is no availability axis at all." ;
    pred:evidence "F133 IS THE WORKED EXAMPLE, FROM THE SESSION THAT PLANNED THIS. The all-previews modifier shipped with every calculable signal green: 25 unit tests passing, svelte-check 0 errors, a visual test measuring 0.0% thumbnail occlusion, 19/19 script-tier checks clean. Matt then used it and found FOUR defects in minutes — the nodes vibrated as the constraint fought the simulation, clicking a thumbnail left no way to open node details, the filters did not apply to previews, and the expanded image covered both side panels. Not one was visible to any check that passed, because each was a question about how the thing FEELS to use. Had ‘tests pass’ been allowed to settle ‘the feature is finished’, all four would have shipped." ;
    pred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/verifiability.ts" ;
    pred:principle "A CLAIM MUST CARRY ITS OWN FALSIFICATION METHOD, AND THAT IS THE ANTI-OVERSTATEMENT MECHANISM. Matt's diagnosis is exact: a model overstates because nothing ever asks it HOW the claim could be checked. Requiring the answer changes what gets produced — a model made to name the procedure emits fewer unfalsifiable sentences, and the ones it does emit arrive labelled as opinion instead of blending in. Confidence cannot do this job: it is self-reported, which makes it an unverifiable claim made by the party it benefits." ;
    pred:touches-file "src/lib/integrations/llm/extractor.ts" ;
    pred:estimated-files "8"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:decided "STATUS IS A QUALITATIVE CLAIM EVEN WHEN IT RESTS ON CALCULABLE EVIDENCE. scripts/offline/status-evidence.ts already reached this conclusion for one case and corrected itself in writing: an untested `production` feature is a RISK, not a false claim, because kpred:has-status describes maturity and deployment while test coverage is an orthogonal fact. Generalising that: kpred:has-status ‘functional’ can never be auto-derived from a green suite. What CAN be automated is the surrounding calculable furniture — does the linked test exist, does it pass, does the file it names still exist — and the honest output of automating it is a better-prepared question for a human, not an answer instead of one." ;
    pred:touches-file "src/lib/rdf/types.ts" ;
    pred:decided "'NONE' MAY NEVER BE STATED AS FACT — it is attributed opinion, and it must render as 'X holds that Y', never as Y. This is the same rule kb:honest-status applies to features and the thesis applies to self-attested claims, extended to the statement level. It is also the class most of this repo's own prose belongs to: 'this file looks fragile' is a judgement wearing a script's clothes." ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/rdf> .

