kb:byo-static-host
    pred:data-egress "user-export" ;
    pred:decided "TARGET PRIORITY (Matt, 2026-07-16): CLOUDFLARE PAGES is the primary user publish target — unlimited bandwidth, ~20k files, and it is already the operator host (F33), so the team knows it. Via the Cloudflare Direct Upload API (or connect-your-repo). GitHub Pages is acknowledged FILE-SIZE-LIMITED (100 MB/file, ~1 GB site) — supported but not ideal for asset-heavy graphs; keep it, do not lead with it. GITLAB is the second alternative (F55.3, better file storage). CAVEAT: Cloudflare Pages caps files at 25 MB, so large GLB/video assets must OFFLOAD to object storage (Cloudflare R2 or a user bucket) and be referenced by URL, not baked into the bundle — this is the F55.2 'storage beyond GitHub' work. Today's REALITY: only publishSiteToGitHub + the offline .zip exist; the swappable host-adapter is unbuilt. Enabling architecture = the Integration Plugin SDK (F61) provider abstraction. Also parameterize the Sveltia CMS backend (github|gitlab) so in-CMS editing follows the chosen host, and emit the CORS header config (F72) for cross-graph fetch." ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/publish/__tests__/targets.test.ts" ;
    pred:remaining "Host adapters (GitHub Pages / Netlify / Cloudflare / plain zip); a guided publish flow; per-shape templates (corpus · portfolio · blog); a preflight that runs the F66 gate before handing the artifact over." ;
    pred:landing-note "Publish a corpus, portfolio, or blog to GitHub Pages, Netlify or Cloudflare — free, yours, no lock-in" ;
    pred:open-question "How far do we go into the host's setup? A generated GitHub Action + a 'Deploy' button is a handful of steps; an OAuth app that pushes for them is fewer steps but makes us a mediated-distribution path (see kb:data-egress-model) and would pull the F66 gate into a flow we would rather keep as pure user-export. Where exactly is the line?" ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/publish/__tests__/site-generator.test.ts" ;
    pred:has-file "scripts/publish-site.ts" ;
    pred:landing-label "Bring-your-own host" ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    rdfs:label "Bring-your-own static host (publish in a handful of steps)" ;
    pred:principle "This is what makes Option C fair. 'Export it and self-host it' is only a real answer if self-hosting is genuinely easy — so ease of publishing is a SAFETY-POLICY dependency, not just a convenience feature." ;
    pred:has-file "src/lib/publish/site-generator.ts" ;
    pred:feature-id "F76" ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/publish> ;
    pred:decided "GIT IS OPTIONAL — TWO DEPLOY MODELS (Matt, 2026-07-16). Publishing does NOT require git. (a) CLI-DIRECT: a local CLI uploads the built folder — Wrangler `wrangler pages deploy` for Cloudflare, `netlify deploy` for Netlify. No repo, no CORS, one command; local installs are acceptable. (b) GIT-BASED: the site lives in a repo the host deploys from — GitHub Pages, and GITLAB PAGES (a repo + .gitlab-ci.yml publishing public/), which is the cleanest FULL git-CI/CD option. (c) MANUAL zip. So GitLab = easiest full-git option; Cloudflare/Wrangler = easiest no-git option; the user picks. Modelled in src/lib/publish/targets.ts (DeployModel = cli | git-watched | manual). OPEN QUESTION the GitLab path surfaces: does publishing emit SOURCE that the host's CI builds, or PRE-BUILT static HTML? Today site-export emits content/*.md + admin (the Sveltia/docs SOURCE, built by the main app's adapter-static). A standalone deploy needs either a build step in CI (GitLab .gitlab-ci.yml runs the build) or the app to export pre-built HTML. Resolve before wiring GitLab CI generation." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:docs-pipeline ;
    pred:decided "PRE-BUILT HTML + GRAPH-DRIVEN DESIGN + TARGETED REGEN (Matt, 2026-07-16). site-generator.ts turns WebPage nodes into SELF-CONTAINED static HTML (marked-rendered body + nav + prev/next + the F72 knowledge.ttl link + one stylesheet + graph.json) — droppable on any host, no framework, no build step on the host. DESIGN COMES FROM THE GRAPH: a ktype:SiteTheme entity carries urn:reckons:site/* facts (logo image, colour palette bg/surface/ink/accent, font, title); extractTheme reads them with dark defaults, so 'add a Logo entity, attach an image, define theme + palette, then generate' works. Authoring loop: refine the graph -> regenerate; edit specific pages in Sveltia -> round-trips. TARGETED REGEN, not whole-site: regenerateChangedPages rebuilds ONLY changed pages; siteShellSignature (theme + nav: title/slug/section/order/prev/next) decides — stable shell => body-only edits rewrite just those pages (consistency once design settles); shell moved => full rebuild. 20 tests (targets + generator). REMAINING: the `reckons publish` CLI (build -> host deploy tool); wiring the theme entity + publish flow into the app UI; large-asset offload." ;
    pred:proof "END TO END from a CLI (2026-07-16): `npm run publish graph.ttl --target=cloudflare-pages --project=NAME` parses the .ttl, generates the self-contained site (clean URLs, graph-driven theme applied — verified --accent reached the CSS, markdown rendered), writes the folder, previews per-file sizes vs the host cap, and emits (or with --deploy runs) `wrangler pages deploy <dir> --project-name=NAME` plus the DNS guidance. Verified on a 2-page test graph: 4 files written, theme + markdown correct, deploy command emitted." ;
    pred:show-on-landing "true" ;
    pred:principle "No registrar, no hosting, no accounts, no lock-in. The user's site lives on the user's host under the user's name. We remove obstacles to self-hosting; we do not become the host." ;
    pred:has-status "in-progress" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:graph-publishing ;
    pred:description "Turn a graph into a deployable static site and hand it to whichever free host the user already has — GitHub Pages, Netlify, Cloudflare Pages, Vercel, or a plain folder they drag anywhere. Reckons.AI is NOT a domain registrar and NOT a file host, and never will be: it generates the artifact and gets out of the way. Target: a handful of steps from graph to live URL. Three shapes must work equally well — a complicated corpus of information (docs/wiki, the F27.2 pipeline already does this), a portfolio of visual art (asset-heavy, F74 asset viewer), and a blog (chronological WebPage nodes). All three are the SAME machinery: WebPage entities in the graph -> content/*.md -> a static build. The host is a swappable target, not a dependency." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:adult-content-policy ;
    pred:has-file "src/lib/publish/targets.ts" .

