kb:design-vs-output
    pred:principle "THIS IS THE ROADMAP-VERSUS-PRODUCTION PATTERN, REUSED. reckons-roadmap.ttl states intent and reckons-production.ttl states deployed reality, and F26 scores the gap. Design intent versus observed output is the same relation one level down, so it should reuse that machinery and its vocabulary rather than invent a parallel one." ;
    pred:scope-decision "THE OBSERVED HALF ALREADY EXISTS IN EMBRYO, and this is a generalization rather than a new idea. F34 phase 1 (scripts/offline/button-crawl.ts) already emits tests/visual/button-crawl.ttl — a TestWorkflow -> TestStep story graph, one workflow per route and one step per click, each carrying its screenshot and a kpred:result verdict plus kpred:violates rubric citations. That is an observed-output graph produced from a real browser. What is missing is (a) recording page COMPOSITION rather than only interaction outcomes, and (b) anything that compares it to a stated intent." ;
    pred:depends-on kb:gp-style-system ;
    pred:relates-to kb:docs-consolidation ;
    pred:depends-on kb:deep-testing ;
    pred:decided "GRAPH-SET GROUPING SHIPPED AS THE FIRST VISIBLE PIECE (2026-07-30). The graphs page listed thirty registry rows in one flat column ordered by import time, so nothing on screen said which graphs belong together. src/lib/storage/graph-sets.ts now buckets them by PURPOSE — Website, Documentation sources, This project, Starter graphs, Other — composing with archive-gallery so an archive is never sorted away from its parent, and bucketing AFTER the page's own filter/sort so every existing ordering rule still applies. Membership is DERIVED FROM THE NAME and each set says so on screen ('by name'), because F113's declared dcat:Catalog membership does not exist yet and a grouping whose basis a user cannot see is one they cannot correct. `declaredSet` on a registry row already takes precedence, so the migration needs no rewrite. It also surfaced FIVE duplicated graph names in Matt's own registry — re-importing a source mints a new database rather than updating the old one, so each name covered two independent copies that disagree the moment either is edited. Reported, never auto-removed: which copy is real is a judgement about content." ;
    pred:open-question "How is a component IDENTIFIED in the rendered DOM? A class name is fragile and a data attribute means instrumenting every component. Marking components at build time is reliable but adds a build-shaped dependency to a local-first app, and an unmarked third-party component (bits-ui, shadcn-svelte) may not be identifiable at all — in which case the honest answer is to report it as unobservable rather than to guess." ;
    pred:honest-note "PLANNED, NOTHING BUILT (2026-07-30). Both halves are partial and neither is connected: F55.1 is speculative with no theme triples in any graph, and button-crawl.ttl records what happened when things were clicked, not what the page is MADE of. There is no design-source graph, no composition observer, and no comparison. Do not read the button-crawl story graph as an output-state graph — it is the closest existing thing and it is a different thing." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 3 — the comparison: a drift report in the shape kb-align already uses, scoring conformance and naming each divergence, emitted as pending findings for review rather than as an auto-fix. A design divergence may be a mistake OR a deliberate exception, and only a human can say which." ;
    rdfs:label "Design intent vs observed output — two website graphs, compared" ;
    pred:remaining "SCREENSHOTS ON WEBSITE-GRAPH NODES (Matt, 2026-07-30). URLs are DONE — every page in both static/website.ttl and static/website-status.ttl carries a resolvable schema:url, so a reviewer reading either graph can open the live page. Screenshots are not: the visual suite writes to tests/visual/screenshots/ and nothing attaches those images to the page nodes they depict. The convention already exists (kmeta:gif carries per-step screenshots on the button-crawl story graph), so this is wiring rather than invention — a per-page visual capture whose output lands on the status graph's node, which would also make the status graph an observation of RENDERING rather than only of files, closing the blind spot it currently declares." ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 1 — a design-source graph: express each page's intended composition as triples (which components, which tokens), reusing schema.org/standard terms where they exist and declaring local ones where they do not, per the website.ttl precedent." ;
    pred:description "Matt (2026-07-30): what F110 does for a page's PROSE, do for its DESIGN. Which accordion style a page uses is a choice like any other, and today it is expressed only in component code where nothing can check it against an intent. Two graphs: a SOURCE DESIGN graph holding the intended composition of each page down to the element — this page uses this accordion, this heading scale, this card — and an OBSERVED OUTPUT graph recording what the built site ACTUALLY rendered. Drift between them is then computable, the same way scripts/kb-align.ts already computes drift between the roadmap's intent and the deployed system." ;
    pred:open-question "Should the output graph be RETAINED per build, or only the latest? Retention makes design regressions queryable over time and is the natural use for the F97 archive; keeping every build's element graph is also exactly the unbounded-snapshot growth F97's retention policy exists to bound." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:ci-cd-kb-watch ;
    pred:relates-to kb:prose-as-fact ;
    pred:has-status "planned" ;
    pred:principle "THE OUTPUT GRAPH MUST BE OBSERVED, NEVER DECLARED. If the build writes the output graph from the same source that produced the page, it proves exactly nothing — it is the renderer certifying its own render, which is the thesis violated at the last step: an unverifiable claim, made by the party it benefits, is not evidence. The output graph has to be extracted from the REAL rendered DOM and pixels, in a browser, after a build. Anything cheaper is a graph that agrees with itself." ;
    pred:feature-id "F111" ;
    pred:scope-decision "THE INTENT HALF IS F55.1 (kb:gp-style-system, speculative), which already scopes theme tokens as graph data and 'a way to bind styles/components to page nodes'. This feature is what makes that binding WORTH having: a declared component choice nobody checks is a comment. F55.1 should therefore be built with this comparison as its reason, not as a styling convenience." ;
    pred:part-of kb:graph-publishing ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 2 — a composition observer: extract what actually rendered from the live DOM in the visual harness (element type, component identity, token values in use) into an output-state graph, one per build/environment so dev and production can be compared as well as intent and actual." ;
    pred:principle "DECOMPOSE TO THE ELEMENT, REPORT AT THE PAGE. Per-element observation is what makes a finding actionable ('the accordion on /docs is not the declared one'), but a per-element report on a 269-page site is the same wall of noise the docs consolidation exists to fix. The unit of OBSERVATION is the element; the unit of REVIEW is the page." .

kb:document-evidence-anchors
    rdfs:label "Document evidence anchors — page, block, span and geometry provenance" ;
