kb:mobile-uiux
    pred:decided "iOS local-AI policy (Matt, 2026-07-17). PROBLEM: loading a WASM LLM crashes the tab on iOS Safari/WebKit (tab OOM — the default Qwen2.5-0.5B q4 is ~500MB; iOS caps per-tab memory). It cannot be caught in JS, and every iOS browser is WebKit (Firefox/Chrome on iOS crash identically — CONFIRMED on Firefox/iOS), so switching browsers does not help and window.ai (Chrome AI) is unavailable there. Chrome AI also needs chrome://flags enabled (desktop/Android only). POLICY: (1) TRY TINY first — default the in-browser model to a genuinely small one on constrained devices (HuggingFaceTB/SmolLM2-135M-Instruct, ~117-182MB ONNX; the app already used HuggingFaceTB SmolLM historically, see db.ts STALE_MODELS), offered behind a clear 'experimental on this device' warning, NEVER auto-loaded silently. (2) Then GRACEFULLY offer the reliable local/private paths: point at your own OLLAMA server (already a provider — local, no WASM, works on iOS as plain HTTP) and add an API KEY. Lead with local-first (tiny / Ollama) over cloud, per the thesis. Crash-safe draft (PR #111) already means a bad outcome never loses the note." ;
    pred:description "Make Reckons.AI genuinely usable on mobile by fixing how the app uses its UI libraries. DIAGNOSIS (measured 2026-07-07): no shared breakpoint system — 23 hand-rolled @media blocks across ~10 arbitrary px breakpoints (600/640/500/900/380/420...) vs a single Tailwind responsive prefix; Tailwind v4 is installed but its responsive system is effectively unused; no useMediaQuery/viewport store and no @theme screens tokens. SnapPanel is a px-width position:fixed floating window dragged via MOUSE events (clientX/clientY, no touch) that snaps to corners computed from window.innerWidth — unusable on a phone. 18 fixed/absolute floating panels stacked by z-index (300-500) overlap instead of reflowing. bits-ui Dialog/Popover/Select/Tooltip are used as desktop patterns (center modals, hover tooltips) with no mobile sheet/drawer equivalent. ARCHITECTURE (phased): (1) FOUNDATION — one breakpoint source of truth: Tailwind @theme screens tokens + CSS custom-media + a Svelte 5 viewport rune store (isMobile / isTouch / breakpoint via matchMedia + pointer:coarse). (2) ADAPTIVE PRIMITIVES — a Sheet/Drawer primitive (bits-ui Dialog styled as a bottom sheet) and an AdaptivePanel wrapper that renders SnapPanel on desktop but a bottom sheet / full-screen drawer on mobile; Select -> sheet/native picker on mobile; Tooltip -> tap-to-reveal or dropped on coarse pointers. (3) INPUT — replace SnapPanel mouse-drag with Pointer Events (touch + mouse) and disable free-drag on mobile in favor of the sheet. (4) LAYOUT — single-column reflow below 640px, primary actions in the thumb zone, touch gestures on the 2D/3D graph, >= 44px targets. Verified by F34 deep tests against the mobile-tagged guidelines in kb:web-uiux-rubric (touch-targets, responsive-layout, progressive-disclosure). Prioritized over F35 steering-committee." ;
    rdfs:label "Mobile UI/UX Architecture" ;
    pred:decided "PWA orientation = 'any' (was 'portrait', vite.config.ts). Reckons.AI is a force-directed GRAPH app — locking portrait is exactly wrong for the thing users most want to do on a tablet. Consequence Matt flagged: MORE visual tests to run per device type — the device matrix (F34 deep testing) must now cover BOTH orientations per device, not just portrait. (Matt, 2026-07-15.)" ;
    pred:feature-id "F36" ;
    pred:remaining "BUILD (F36 iOS local-AI safety): device-capability detection (isIosWebkit / memory-constrained), a tiny-model manifest + iOS default, a graceful 'try tiny / connect Ollama / add API key' offer replacing any silent big-model auto-load, and a CRASH-CLASS TEST HARNESS (Matt: 'these crashes are bad news for UX'). Honest test scope: Playwright's WebKit has no iOS memory cap, so it cannot reproduce the OOM itself — the gate instead asserts the PREVENTION (on an iOS-like device we never attempt a >threshold model; the graceful offer appears; the risky model-load flows raise no page crash/console error across the mobile device matrix). Real-iOS OOM-avoidance is device-verified by Matt." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:priority "high" ;
    pred:has-status "planned" ;
    pred:decided "BENCHMARK EVIDENCE (bench:llm, 2026-07-17; matches a 2026-06-18 run) — performance vs size for the in-browser (transformers.js, dtype q4, CPU) tier. SmolLM2-135M (~180MB): ingest F1 0%, chat 37.8%, combined 18.9%, and it took 187s AND echoed the system prompt instead of extracting (doesn't follow the format). Qwen2.5-0.5B (~500MB): ingest F1 0%, chat 70.7%, combined 35.3%, 152s, empty extraction. HONEST CONCLUSION: in-browser WASM LLMs are NOT viable for EXTRACTION (every one scores F1≈0) and are very SLOW on CPU (2.5-3 min/extraction) — they are a weak, slow CHAT-ONLY fallback. This validates leading real work to Ollama/API-key; the 'try tiny' path must be honestly framed as chat-only + experimental, never implied to extract. Efficiency (combined per 100MB): 135M 10.5 vs 0.5B 7.1 — tiny wins per-MB but its absolute quality is poor. The bench:llm comparison table now shows Size + Score/100MB so this stays measurable." ;
    pred:depends-on kb:visual-testing ;
    pred:relates-to kb:deep-testing .

kb:graph-sets
    pred:remaining "PHASE 2 — observation activities as first-class nodes: every generator that writes an observed graph also writes its prov:Activity — method, tool, version, timestamp, and the method's stated blind spots. Retrofit npm run website:align first, since its blind spot (content layer, not rendered DOM) is already written down in prose." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:design-observed-archive ;
    pred:scope-decision "MANY OBSERVATION GRAPHS PER ARTIFACT, ONE PER METHOD. A visual test observes rendered pixels; a code test observes behaviour; a VLM scores appearance against a rubric; the content-layer reader observes files. These disagree legitimately and must not be merged — a page can pass every code test and render blank. Each observation graph therefore carries its activity: what method, what tool and version, when, and CRUCIALLY what that method cannot see. src/lib/rdf/multi-graph-query.ts already refuses to merge disagreement across graphs and computes whether agreement is independent, so several observation graphs answering one question is a solved read problem." ;
    pred:principle "PROPOSED IS A ROLE, NOT A FILE. An agent's generated page, asset or fact is neither intent nor observed reality, and today all of it lands in knowledge.pending.jsonl regardless of kind — which is why that queue is 586 entries deep and has never been drained. As a graph role a proposal keeps its provenance (which agent, from which activity, against which design), can be queried and ranked rather than only appended, and stays inside the F52 boundary: promoting a proposal into the design graph remains a human act." ;
    pred:scope-decision "THE RELATION IS NOT 'DESIGN DERIVES OBSERVATION' — that framing is the failure mode, not the model. If an observation graph derived from the design, it would be a restatement of intent and prove nothing. The observation derives from the ARTIFACT. In PROV-O the chain is: design is prov:used by a GENERATION activity which prov:generated the artifact; the artifact is then prov:used by an OBSERVATION activity which prov:generated the observation graph. Two activities, one artifact between them, and the design and the observation never touch. Writing it this way makes the honest-observation rule structural instead of a convention someone has to remember." ;
    pred:feature-id "F113" ;
    pred:depends-on kb:multi-kb ;
    pred:has-status "planned" ;
    pred:principle "AN OBSERVATION WITHOUT ITS METHOD IS AN ASSERTION. 'The page renders correctly' is worth nothing without 'observed by headless chromium at 1280px, which cannot see font loading or animation'. F90's recorded trap is the proof: Blender renders a BLACK FRAME AND EXITS 0, so an observer that only checks the exit code reports success on an empty artifact. The method's blind spots belong IN the graph, beside its findings." ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 1 — a set vocabulary: dcat:Catalog listing member void:Datasets, each with a role (design / observed / archive / proposed), expressible in TriG so a whole set round-trips through the F84.3 GSP connector." ;
    pred:decided "A DISAGREEMENT BETWEEN OBSERVERS IS A DISCREPANCY TO INVESTIGATE, NOT A VERDICT TO SCORE (Matt, 2026-07-30 — supersedes an earlier suggestion here that the set should record each method's TRUST). Ranking observers by trust decides in advance which one is wrong, which is the whole question. Observation is imperfect at every level: a VLM can misjudge, a deterministic check can test the wrong thing, and a HUMAN can mis-report a pass or a fail through a simple misunderstanding of what the test intended. The discrepancy is therefore raised and routed, never auto-resolved. See kb:discrepancy-backpropagation (F114)." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:design-vs-output ;
    pred:relates-to kb:graph-db-connectors ;
    pred:open-question "Does an archive graph exist per SET or per MEMBER? Per member is precise and multiplies graph count by the number of observations; per set is compact and makes 'what did this observation say last week' a query over a mixed journal. F97's retention already bounds snapshot growth, so the per-member cost may be affordable — but nobody has measured a set with several observers over time." ;
    pred:decided "STANDARD VOCABULARY, following the website.ttl precedent. dcat:Catalog for the SET (a catalog of datasets is exactly this), void:Dataset for each member, dcterms:type or a small local role vocabulary for the ROLE, and PROV-O for every relation between them. TriG carries the whole thing losslessly because its named graphs map onto the members, and F84 already puts the PUBLISHER in the 4th position — so provenance travels with the data rather than in a sidecar nobody ships." ;
    pred:evidence "THE AI-ORCHESTRATION SPACE HAS CONVERGED ON THE SAME SHAPE, which is corroboration rather than a template to copy. Experiment trackers and evaluation harnesses (MLflow-style runs and artifacts, LangSmith/Weave-style traces and evaluations, OpenTelemetry's GenAI conventions) all separate THE ARTIFACT from THE RUN THAT PRODUCED IT from THE EVALUATOR THAT JUDGED IT, and all record the evaluator's identity rather than only its verdict. That is prov:Entity / prov:Activity / prov:Agent under other names. NOTE THE HONEST LIMIT OF THIS CITATION: it is a pattern observation, not a verified survey of current product features, and it should not be quoted as one." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 4 — set-aware queries: scope a multi-graph query or a reckoning to a SET and a ROLE ('every observation of the website'), which is what makes F112's situation-as-scope selectable rather than a list of raw graph names." ;
    pred:has-part kb:multi-graph-viewport ;
    rdfs:label "Graph sets — roles, relations, and how each observation was made" ;
    pred:honest-note "PLANNED, NOTHING BUILT (2026-07-30). Instances of the shape exist and none of them declares itself: static/website.ttl and static/website-status.ttl are a design/observed pair with no set, no roles and no recorded observation activity — the status graph documents its own method in a PROSE COMMENT, which is exactly the thing this feature would make queryable. There is no dcat:Catalog anywhere, no role vocabulary, and proposals remain in JSONL." ;
    pred:description "Matt (2026-07-30): organize graphs into SETS the way facts are organized into entity sets (F65), with explicit ROLES and RELATIONS between members — and with each observation graph declaring HOW the observation was made, because visual tests and code tests observe the same artifact differently and are therefore different graphs, not one merged one. Also moves agent PROPOSALS out of knowledge.pending.jsonl and into a graph role, which is where they always belonged." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:entity-sets ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 3 — proposals as a graph role, replacing the pending JSONL path: same drain semantics, but queryable, provenance-carrying, and rankable by the F88 gate and blast radius the review pipeline already computes." .

kb:graph-as-navigation
    pred:description "Make published pages first-class citizens of the graph so the graph itself is the site map. PARTS: (1) WebPage nodes (ktype:WebPage, F27) RENDER as graph nodes — visible alongside concepts, distinguishable (icon/type), selectable. (2) In-graph site structure: define a LANDING/home page and navigation to SUBPAGES as graph edges (reuse skos:broader for parent/child + nav:next/prev for sequence, the same hierarchy vocabulary the hnav trees use), created and rearranged in the graph. (3) LIGHT-FORCE nav edges — CRITICAL: page/nav edges must carry a much lower force-directed spring weight than concept edges, because a nav hub connected to many published pages (or pages linked across every concept) would otherwise collapse/warp the main graph shape. Introduce per-edge-kind force weights (concept edges normal; page/nav edges very light, or excluded from the main force pass and laid out in a secondary/orbit pass) so adding the whole published site never distorts the knowledge layout. (4) In-graph PAGE METADATA editing: edit slug, section, template, status, title from the node panel (writes the urn:kbase:page/* triples; round-trips via F27 site-export + md-align). (5) Publishing map: from any concept, see which pages reference/derive from it and jump to them; from a page node, open the published /docs URL. Builds on F27 graph publishing and relates to F46 (question-context pages, another in-graph page attach), the 2D/3D graph (per-edge force weights), and the /docs route." ;
    pred:feature-id "F49" ;
    pred:proof "The part the roadmap flags CRITICAL — per-edge-kind force weights — is built and tested (6 tests, 2026-07-15). edge-force.ts classifies a predicate as concept / nav / page and returns a spring-weight multiplier (concept 1, nav 0.15, page 0.1), so a densely-linked site hub pulls far more weakly than concept edges and cannot warp the knowledge layout. isSiteEdge() marks structure edges for a secondary layout pass. Pure/deterministic. SCAFFOLDED not functional: the force simulation does not yet CALL edgeForceWeight (the 2D/3D graph still springs every edge equally), WebPage nodes do not yet render as graph nodes, and in-graph page-metadata editing is not built. This is the enabler, not the feature." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:question-context-pages ;
    pred:depends-on kb:graph-publishing ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:has-status "scaffolded" ;
    pred:has-file "src/lib/rdf/edge-force.ts" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:knowledge-graph ;
    rdfs:label "Graph-as-Site-Navigation" ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/rdf> ;
    pred:priority "high" ;
    pred:principle "The graph is the PRIMARY navigation of what pages are published and where they're accessed — not a separate site-map UI. Publishing structure lives in, and is edited from, the graph." ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/edge-force.test.ts" .

kb:auto-merge
    pred:relates-to kb:predicate-manager ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/pending-dedup.test.ts" ;
    pred:feature-id "F80.1" ;
    pred:has-file "src/lib/stores/workspace.svelte.ts" ;
    pred:auto-merge-threshold "0.9" ;
    pred:part-of kb:async-orchestration ;
    rdfs:label "Auto-merge of accumulated facts" ;
    pred:has-file "src/lib/rdf/merge-band.ts" ;
    pred:description "Facts pile up while the user is away, and many are the same fact said twice by different agents. Where two pending statements are confidently identical after entity normalization (F22) they should MERGE rather than both queue for review — the user should never triage the same finding twice. Merging is confidence-gated and always reviewable; an auto-merge is a suggestion the graph made, not a fact the graph invented." ;
    pred:has-file "src/lib/rdf/lexical-similarity.ts" ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/merge-band.test.ts" ;
    pred:has-status "scaffolded" ;
    pred:suggest-merge-floor "0.5" ;
    pred:decided "One merge band, applied to BOTH entity merges AND predicate-sameness (is this predicate link the same as that one): auto-merge at >=0.90 similarity (after F22 normalization for entities); SUGGEST a merge (queued for review, never applied silently) in the 0.50-0.90 band; below 0.50 no action. The suggest band IS the answer to the silent-destruction risk — nothing merges unreviewed unless it is >=90% identical, and everything less confident becomes a reviewable suggestion, not a silent action. (Matt, 2026-07-15.) Status stays planned: this is the decided spec, not yet wired; kb:predicate-manager today does manual, exact-match merge only." ;
    pred:has-file "src/lib/rdf/pending-dedup.ts" ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/lexical-similarity.test.ts" ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/rdf> ;
    pred:proof "The deterministic CORE is built and tested (16 tests, 2026-07-15). merge-band.ts is the one executable copy of the decided band (auto >=0.90 / suggest 0.50-0.90 / else none) governing merge, link AND predicate-sameness. pending-dedup.ts applies it: findPendingDuplicates() folds exact-identical pending facts (similarity 1.0 -> auto, highest-confidence kept, datatype/lang respected, deterministic); findMergeSuggestions() surfaces same-predicate semantic near-dupes in the suggest band without acting (similarity injected, so the core stays model-free). WIRED (type-checked) into drainAndImportPending (workspace.svelte.ts): the MCP pending drain now folds exact duplicate COMPLETE facts within a batch before review — partial facts (F32 questions) are deliberately left alone so their blocks/question metadata is never silently dropped. STILL SCAFFOLDED, not functional: the drain wiring is not yet OBSERVED running live (needs a connected workspace, not drivable headlessly); cross-batch dedupe against already-imported pending is not built (needs a graph read); the review UI does not yet show a fold count; and the semantic tier's similarity source (F22 embeddings) is not connected." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> .

kb:wikipedia-enrich
    pred:open-question "Which API surface: the Wikidata SPARQL endpoint (rich, structured, rate-limited), the REST summary API (cheap, prose), or an offline dump (no network, large)? And does this stay a per-user opt-in integration like every other network source, or is a read-only public API acceptable in the core given local-first constraints?" ;
    pred:description "Use Wikipedia/Wikidata as a first-class source when analyzing or enriching a graph: given an entity the graph already holds, look it up, and propose facts from the article's structured data (Wikidata claims, infobox, categories, coordinates, dates) as PENDING statements carrying the article URL and revision id as provenance. Complements F100: source-validation CHECKS a fact the graph already asserts, this ADDS candidate facts the graph is missing. Also the natural source for entity DISAMBIGUATION (two 'Mercury' nodes) and for the images the starter-graph work just proved it serves well." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:principle "Prefer WIKIDATA over scraping article prose. Wikidata is already triples — item, property, value, with qualifiers and references — so it maps onto RDF without an LLM in the loop at all. That makes most of this SCRIPT tier (F74.3): deterministic, free, and incapable of hallucinating a fact the source does not contain. Reserve the LLM for the article prose an infobox does not cover." ;
    pred:raised-by "Matt (2026-07-23)" ;
    pred:has-status "speculative" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:int-firecrawl ;
    pred:relates-to kb:source-validation ;
    pred:principle "Wikipedia is a TERTIARY source and must be recorded as one. Its own policy is that it is not a reliable source for citation — the value is that each claim carries REFERENCES to primary sources, so the enrichment worth having is often the reference, not the sentence. A fact sourced from Wikipedia must never be presented as corroborated evidence merely because an encyclopaedia repeated it; that would be the thesis failing in our own pipeline." ;
    pred:depends-on kb:enrichment-pipeline ;
    rdfs:label "Wikipedia / Wikidata as an enrichment source" ;
    pred:feature-id "F109" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:enrichment-pipeline .

