kb:one-graph-canvas
    pred:priority "high" ;
    pred:has-status "planned" ;
    pred:open-question "PROGRESSIVE RENDER (Matt, 2026-07-15): render the ghostly connections (kb ghost-graph) first and lazily bring the filtered-out nodes in later, so the first frame is cheap. RISK he flagged: lazy pop-in can FLICKER, and worse, it can BEAT against the framerate-driven LOD at specific frame rates (each triggers the other — shed detail, frame recovers, load detail, frame drops, shed again). If built, the lazy load and the LOD controller must share one budget and one hysteresis, not fight; settle a node's presence for a minimum dwell time before reconsidering it. Decide whether progressive load is worth that coupling or whether adaptive LOD alone suffices." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:undersea-pod-ui ;
    pred:principle "REVIEW IS A MODE, NOT A PAGE (Matt, 2026-07-15). It is the same one canvas with a DIFFERENT DEFAULT panel layout and its own settings (a Shelly panel, etc.). Panels are manually CONFIGURABLE — the default is a starting point, not a cage. What makes review 'review' is not a separate render; it is what it DOES." ;
    rdfs:label "One graph canvas — review as overlays, adaptive LOD" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:graph-legibility ;
    pred:principle "REVIEW'S JOB IS ADDING PENDING + QUESTION TRIPLES (Matt, 2026-07-15). The review mode's essential function is proposing facts (pending) and partial facts (F32 questions) into the graph for human settling — everything else (which panels, which layout) is presentation around that. Design the mode around that verb." ;
    pred:description "Retire the SECOND graph render. /review currently imports its own KnowledgeGraph/2D/Overlay/Compare instances and reimplements labels, node-details and mode toggles — a divergent copy of the main graph at / (measured 2026-07-15: both pages import the render directly; there is no shared canvas). Extract ONE GraphCanvas component that owns the render, adaptive level-of-detail, labels, node-details and a panel/overlay SLOT API; / and /review both mount it. Review's incoming/deletions/merges/align become panels/sidebars/overlays over that one canvas, highlighting pending nodes IN PLACE, instead of a separate preview render." ;
    pred:depends-on kb:graph-legibility ;
    pred:starting-with "Small shared pieces first (Matt, 2026-07-15): extract the drift-prone bits — node-details panel, then the label overlay — into shared components both / and /review mount, before the full GraphCanvas. Lowest risk, and it directly retires the divergence that caused the review-graph bug cluster. DONE first: GraphLabels.svelte (the label overlay) extracted from the main page with no behaviour change and adopted on both / and /review — the drift that lost review its 3D labels can no longer recur. Verified: 0 type errors, 6/6 review e2e pass." ;
    pred:remaining "Extract GraphCanvas (render + LOD + labels + node-details + overlay slots) from the main page; adopt it on / with no behaviour change; migrate /review to mount it with review panels as overlays; add framerate-driven LOD tiers (full-3D / low-poly-3D / 2D) with hysteresis; retire /review's duplicate render components." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:review-workbench ;
    pred:principle "Two renders of the same thing WILL drift, and a bug fixed in one stays live in the other. The review-graph bug cluster (3D labels, overlay node-details, doubled toggles) was drift, not three bugs. One canonical render, reused, is the fix — and it is cheaper than maintaining the divergence." ;
    pred:principle "Degrade by FRAMERATE, not by a hard 2D/3D switch. A graph that drops frames should shed detail gracefully — full 3D -> lower-poly 3D (simpler geometry/materials, fewer labels) -> 2D — measured from real frame timing with hysteresis so it does not flap. Falling all the way to 2D on the first stutter throws away depth the user wanted." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:feature-id "F92" .

