kb:proposal-yield
    pred:constraint "IT NEVER FAILS THE BUILD. A low yield is information, not a broken invariant — failing CI on it would make the honest number the inconvenient one and create pressure to stop measuring. It also cannot distinguish 'rejected because wrong' from 'rejected because already known'; both are a human declining to accept, which is the triage cost either way." ;
    pred:honest-note "ATTRIBUTION BEGAN 2026-08-13, so every earlier proposal is unattributable BY CONSTRUCTION and this number becomes meaningful only as the queue is drained from here. It is not retroactive, and reporting it as though it were would be the overclaim this feature exists to prevent." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:graph-sets ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:actual-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:description "A script-tier job computing accepted-over-ruled-on per agent, plus the fraction of each agent's output that has been looked at. npm run offline (scripts/offline/proposal-yield.ts)." ;
    pred:actual-files "5"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:decided "TWO NUMBERS, AND THE SECOND IS THE HONEST ONE. YIELD is accepted over RULED-ON; TRIAGED is ruled-on over proposed. A brilliant yield across 3 of 273 proposals is not evidence of anything, so yield is never reported without the coverage that qualifies it, and the job says 'unknown rather than good or bad' when nothing has been ruled on." ;
    pred:measured "FIRST RUN, 2026-08-13: 736 proposals, 0 EVER RULED ON. The queue has grown from the 586 that kb:graph-sets already recorded as 'never drained', against 8 recorded answers. Top producers: offline:branch-align 273, offline:code-review with qwen3-coder 119, offline:history-lessons 65, button-crawl 53, offline:competitor-scan 46. Nothing establishes whether any of those 273 were right. An unmeasured queue is not a backlog, it is an unpriced liability." ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/proposal-attribution.test.ts" ;
    pred:priority "high" ;
    pred:remaining "BUILT: the job, the attribution round trip, registration in jobs.json. NOT DONE: nothing yet measures TRIAGE COST in time or tokens, so 'offloading moved cost rather than removing it' is still only half-measurable; and the 736-deep queue remains undrained, which is the actual work this metric is arguing for." ;
    pred:evidence "THE METRIC WAS NOT COMPUTABLE UNTIL ATTRIBUTION WAS FIXED, which is why it did not exist. drainAndImportPending attached the proposing agent ONLY to partial facts (askedBy, for routing an answer back), so measured against the real queue 55% of entries — the 402 carrying an object — lost their agent on import, folded into one batch source titled 'MCP (agent-a, agent-b) — N notes'. Statement.proposedBy now carries it for every proposal, toTurtleFull emits meta:proposed-by and importTurtleFull reads it back, so the number is computable from exported graphs by a script rather than only inside the browser. 5 round-trip tests." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:work-tiering ;
    pred:has-status "functional" ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/rdf> ;
    rdfs:label "Proposal yield — was running that agent worth it?" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:local-offload ;
    pred:principle "THE WORK-TIERING DOCTRINE RESTS ON A NUMBER NOBODY WAS COMPUTING. CLAUDE.md states that 'a local job that emits 30 findings of which 25 are noise moves cost from generation to TRIAGE rather than removing it', and the promotion ladder says 'anything an agent gets right RELIABLY is demoted to a script'. Both are claims about YIELD. Neither could be evaluated, so the choice between script tier and agent tier has been made on judgement alone." ;
    pred:feature-id "F129" .

kb:merge-synonyms
    pred:evidence "TRANSITIVITY IS FREE, and that is why this is a statement rather than a side table. Aliases are statements whose subject is the kept entity, so a later merge of that entity into a third redirects them along with everything else. A node merged three times still carries every name it has ever had, with no special handling." ;
    pred:has-status "functional" ;
    pred:decided "skos:altLabel, NOT A NEW PREDICATE. SKOS is already this project's vocabulary for concept relations (skos:broader and skos:narrower drive rdf/hierarchy.ts; skos:definition appears in ghost-graph.ts and dichotomy.ts) and altLabel is SKOS's own term for alternate lexical labels beside a single preferred one. Minting kpred:synonym would be the private-vocabulary mistake graph-lint already warns about — 73 of 315 predicates used exactly once — and would not survive export to any other tool." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:entity-normalization ;
    pred:actual-files "3"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/rdf> ;
    pred:evidence "THE BUG WAS LOSSY AND SILENT. src/routes/(app)/review/+page.svelte redirects the dropped entity's statements onto the kept one, and MergeReview.svelte classifies two different rdfs:label values as a CONFLICT whose loser is set to 'rejected' (default resolution is 'a'). So merging 'Ava Farmers Market' into 'Ava Growers Market' destroyed the first name — the very name a source used, a search would look for, and the next import will arrive as, at which point the entity duplicates again and a human merges it a second time." ;
    pred:remaining "BUILT: src/lib/rdf/merge-aliases.ts (pure — value derivation, case/whitespace normalisation, dedupe, IRI-slug recovery, primary-label resolution) wired into onMergeConfirm, 12 tests. NOT BUILT: search does not yet match an entity by its altLabel, and the node panel does not display or let you edit the synonym list. entityAnswersTo() exists for the first of those and has no caller." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/merge-aliases.test.ts" ;
    rdfs:label "Synonyms survive a merge" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:entity-sets ;
    pred:description "Matt (2026-08-13): 'we need some way to track all synonyms within a node. When merging multiple nodes into one that list should automatically occur.' Merging now preserves every name the merged entities answered to, as skos:altLabel on the surviving node, automatically and without a review step." ;
    pred:decided "THE LOSING NAME IS PRESERVED WHICHEVER SIDE LOSES. If the user resolves the label conflict in favour of the DROPPED entity, the KEPT entity's original name is the one about to be rejected — so the alias pass reads the surviving primary label and preserves everything else, rather than assuming the kept entity's name always wins. Tested explicitly." ;
    pred:principle "A MERGE IS A CLAIM THAT TWO NAMES DENOTE ONE THING, so discarding the losing name throws away the evidence for the claim being made. It is a lossy edit dressed as a cleanup." ;
    pred:actual-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:priority "high" ;
    pred:feature-id "F126" .

kb:local-model-bench
    pred:description "Evidence-driven selection of LOCAL models for agent grunt work: keep the strong cloud model (Opus) on the main/orchestration thread and delegate bulk work to local Ollama models via `ollama launch` integrations (claude/opencode/etc.) on a local multi-GPU Ollama host — zero API cost, private. Two reproducible benchmarks pick the right local model per role. (1) VLM visual-gate bench — tests/visual/run-vlm-gate-bench.ts + vlm-golden.ts — scores local vision models as 'does this appear' gates against human-verified screenshot labels (10 images, 48 checks across 7 capability types: toast/mobile/graph/blank/settings/kb/align-tab); the gate score weights recall highest (a miss waves a real regression through — the one intolerable error) plus precision, accuracy, and latency. A cheap local VLM screens every screenshot; only its flags escalate to the expensive reviewer (Opus/Claude Vision). Result: qwen2.5vl:7b selected (94%, 0 misses); granite3.2-vision brittle (44%, 2 misses) despite a perfect 6/6 smoke test — a caution against trusting smoke tests; moondream is prose-only and unusable for yes/no gating. (2) Coding-correctness bench — tests/bench/run-coding-bench.ts + coding-golden.ts — self-contained edit/implement/fix/parse tasks graded by writing the model's code block to a temp dir and running an assert driver via tsx (pass@1 + latency + tokens + output cleanliness). Result: qwen3-coder selected (4/4, fastest full-passer, terse); devstral-small-2 and gpt-oss:20b also 4/4; the large general model nemotron3:33b was the worst coder here (3/4, slowest, ~10x tokens) — reserve big reasoning models for planning, not code grunt work. Operational lesson baked into both harnesses: local models default to very large context windows (up to 128k) which balloon KV-cache VRAM and can crash larger vision runtimes under contention, so the benches pin a small num_ctx (sized to hold one screenshot / prompt) and unload each model between runs. SHIPPED 2026-07-07. Phase 2 SHIPPED 2026-07-07: tool-loop agentic coding bench — tests/bench/run-agentic-bench.ts + agentic-tasks.ts — drives each local model through a real tool-calling loop (list_files/read_file/write_file/run_tests/finish) in a sandbox, measuring pass@1, turns, self-verification, scope discipline, and spiral rate. Result: devstral-small-2 top (0.98, all pass, fewest turns, ~366 tokens), qwen3-coder and gpt-oss:20b also perfect; nemotron3:33b failed a third time (33% pass, spiraled, 6225 tokens, self-verified once) — consistent across all three benches that big reasoning models are wrong for coding/agentic grunt work. Cost bench SHIPPED 2026-07-07: tests/bench/run-cost-bench.ts + cost-golden.ts — compares Opus-token cost of full-system vs Opus-alone on graph-grounded generation, three arms (A0 Opus+raw graph, A1 Opus+kb_compress-style compressed subgraph, A2 local drafts + Opus reviews), inferring $ from live API usage. Result (Opus 4.8, ~$0.48 spend): A1 and A2 both 79% cheaper than A0 at identical 100% quality — the graph compression is the dominant lever (6x fewer input tokens); local delegation adds little on short outputs and pays only when OUTPUT is large (Opus output tokens at $25/Mtok are the expensive ones). Extended 2026-07-07: a long-form (~700-word) generation task confirmed the delegation win — A2 (local writes, Opus reviews/approves) 77% cheaper than A0 vs A1's 66%, because the expensive OUTPUT is generated free locally; and raw context proved LOWER quality here — the full raw graph scored 60% fact-coverage vs 80% for the compressed subgraph (needle-in-haystack). Capacity note: the whole static graph is ~116k tokens — fits comfortably in Opus's 1M window, but feeding it raw costs ~50x more per query than a compressed subgraph and can ground worse; compression is a cost+quality lever, not a capacity workaround. Harness comparison (OpenCode, Qwen Code): both are built for interactive use and their headless local-model paths are immature — OpenCode ships no local-Ollama provider (only ollama-cloud; needs manual provider config), Qwen Code defaults to qwen-oauth cloud — observed opening a browser to chat.qwen.ai for login even in headless -p mode — and even wired neither completed a trivial fix headlessly in 120s via the OpenAI-compatible /v1 path (~13s cold per call), whereas the minimal custom tool-loop and Claude Code complete the same tasks in seconds. Qualitative conclusion (matching hands-on experience): Claude Code's harness has materially lower friction/overhead for local-model agent work; a clean headless pass-rate comparison would need long timeouts and per-tool auth/config work not yet done." ;
    rdfs:label "Local Model Benchmarking & Orchestration" ;
    pred:in-dev "2026-07-07"^^xsd:date ;
    pred:relates-to kb:ci-cd-kb-watch ;
    pred:tested-by "tests/bench/run-agentic-bench.ts" ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/test-bench> ;
    pred:tested-by "tests/bench/run-coding-bench.ts" ;
    pred:tested-by "tests/visual/run-vlm-gate-bench.ts" ;
    pred:has-status "functional" ;
    pred:feature-id "F31" ;
    pred:tested-by "tests/bench/run-cost-bench.ts" ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:depends-on kb:mcp-server .

