    pred:relates-to kb:predicate-economy ;
    pred:has-status "in-progress" ;
    pred:principle "A literal earns a node by being SHARED. Appearing once means it connects nothing." ;
    pred:measured "reckons-roadmap.ttl: 1888 triples, 1234 nodes -> 271 (78% fewer). 985 attributes moved to the node panel. 27 shared literals earned category nodes (production, planned, functional, high...). 903 edges drawn instead of 1888." ;
    pred:feature-id "F83" ;
    pred:done "Graph package & sync moved OUT of the canvas filter panel and INTO the Graphs tab (2026-07-13). It had been buried behind a disclosure, collapsed 'so the panel stays short' — which was the tell that it did not belong there. A filter panel should filter. Graph management has its own top-level tab precisely because it matters." ;
    pred:principle "A graph is a picture of RELATIONSHIPS. A value that relates nothing to nothing is not part of that picture, however important it is to read." ;
    pred:description "Make a large graph readable rather than merely rendered. (1) LITERALS ARE ATTRIBUTES, NOT NODES, unless shared: a value used once connects nothing and belongs in the node panel; a value used 53 times is a category worth seeing. (2) FILTER BY PREDICATE — 'just show me the dependency graph' — with facets sorted by count so structural relations surface first. (3) FILTER BY TIME, so a graph can be read as it grew. (4) PROGRESSIVE DISCLOSURE via top-by-degree, keeping only edges whose BOTH endpoints survive, so the canvas never shows an edge vanishing into nothing." ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/rdf> ;
    pred:idea "EXTREME CONDENSATION + PROACTIVE ANALYZE SUGGESTIONS (Matt, 2026-07-15). Analyze (maybe Review) should offer AGGRESSIVE condensation of the graph, driven by HIGH-LEVEL HUMAN DECISIONS AT THE HUB LEVEL: for each hub, GO (drop it), SUMMARIZE (collapse it to one summary node — reuse kb_compress / entity-review), or STAY. Condensing this way REFINES the graph's focus. And the app must NUDGE: when an indicator fires (e.g. >1000 nodes — large, hard to take in visually or intellectually, the same context-window argument F83 already makes for people), proactively SUGGEST the relevant analyze/condense option rather than leaving the user to drown. Large graphs will happen (timelines especially) — those are filterable by time (F83 time filter), so the answer to size is FOCUS (filter + condense), not a hard cap. Ties to kb_compress (condense for an LLM = condense for a person), entity-review (things not rows), and F96 Shelly graph-control (Shelly could apply the suggested condensation on request)." ;
    pred:has-file "src/lib/rdf/graph-view.ts" ;
