kb:rdf-dataset-migration
    pred:decided "TRIG IN, TURTLE OUT (2026-07-12). Readers accept TriG; files stay .ttl with a default graph; a KB becomes .trig only when it genuinely has more than one source. Justified by kb:provenance-model below — not a marketing compromise: for a single-source graph a named graph is ceremony carrying zero information, so default-graph Turtle is the CORRECT model, and 'your data is a plain .ttl file' stays true for the overwhelming majority of cases." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:work-tiering ;
    pred:actual-files "3"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:remaining "Wire toTriG into the export UI; adopt .trig per-file only when a KB genuinely carries >1 source. OPEN (asked): whether a pure-text multi-KB meta-file is wanted enough to overload the graph term." ;
    pred:estimated-files "5"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:touches-module <urn:reckons:code/storage> ;
    pred:decided "ITEM 2 OF THE REMAINING LIST WAS DECLINED ON EVIDENCE, NOT DEFERRED. 'Adopt .trig per-file only when a KB genuinely carries >1 source' has a condition that never fires here: measured 2026-08-14, ZERO of the 10 workspace graphs carry more than one source. Building a conditional writer for a condition no local graph meets would be untestable speculation, and the standards review had already decided the on-disk default stays Turtle. A real user graph built from five PDFs WOULD meet it, so this is not dead — it is unexercisable here, which is a different thing and worth saying." ;
    pred:refined "SUPERSEDED IN SCOPE by kb:ttl-trig-split (Matt, 2026-07-13). The question 'what does g mean?' was the wrong question, because it assumed one answer for one product. It has TWO answers, because there are two products. PRIVATE (.ttl): g means almost nothing — a source the user controls and can rewrite is self-attestation, not evidence, and a private graph needs no provenance because it makes no claim on anybody. PUBLISHED (.trig): g is a PUBLICATION — facts attributed to an accountable publisher, at a time, under a policy. THAT is what turns a source into provenance, because a publisher can be checked, cited, disputed and held responsible, and a private file cannot. So the format split is not a serialization preference; it is the boundary between the individual product and the institutional one." ;
    pred:done "toTriG (src/lib/rdf/serialize.ts) — one named graph per SOURCE, lossless. Round-trip tested: parse our own output and every fact still knows which source it came from. A companion test PINS THE LOSS in toTurtle (its output round-trips with everything in the default graph), so nobody mistakes it for a lossless export." ;
    pred:bug "toTurtle()'s provenance block is ORPHANED: it emits <urn:kbase:stmt/{id}> prov:wasDerivedFrom <source>, but the triples above carry no statement id and there is no rdf:Statement reification linking stmt/{id} back to its (s,p,o). The block is therefore unrecoverable decoration — it costs bytes and conveys nothing. toTurtleFull (proper reification) and toNQuads (native quads) are correct; plain toTurtle is the lossy path and must say so." ;
    pred:done "Readers switched to format 'TriG' (import-ttl.ts, multi-graph-parse.ts, mcp-server/kb-reader.ts) — zero behavior change, 383 RDF tests green, since every .ttl is already legal TriG." ;
    pred:estimated-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:feature-id "F75" ;
    pred:description "Honor the n-quad model (src/lib/rdf/types.ts: 'a knowledge base is a set of n-quads', g = provenance) WITHOUT giving up Turtle's editability. TriG is a strict superset of Turtle — every .ttl is already legal TriG — but the superset runs ONE WAY: serializing back to Turtle/N-Triples silently drops the graph term. Verified with N3: reckons-roadmap.ttl parses identically under format 'TriG' (1526 quads, all default-graph), while the Turtle parser rejects a named graph outright. DECISION (2026-07-12): make every READER TriG-format (accepts both, zero behavior change) but keep the hand-authored KBs as default-graph .ttl. Rationale: default-graph Turtle concatenates textually and every triple lands in one place, so combining and editing graphs stays trivial; named graphs would make a merge semantic rather than textual. The .trig extension is reserved for files that genuinely need named graphs — the EXTENSION IS A CONTRACT, so a .ttl containing a graph block would be a lie that breaks any format:'Turtle' consumer." ;
    pred:actual-deps "0"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:decided "THE GRAPH TERM IS THE SOURCE — not the KB. TriG has ONE graph slot; spending it on KB-bundling would silently destroy per-source provenance. File = KB, named graph = source. Bundle multiple KBs with a graph package (which also carries assets, as TriG cannot)." ;
    pred:measured "18 of 19 repo .ttl files declare 0-1 sources, so a named graph carries ZERO information for them — they stay .ttl forever. TriG earns its place at EXPORT, not in the repo: today toTurtle drops g, so after ingesting 5 PDFs and exporting, 'where did this fact come from?' is unanswerable." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:provenance-model ;
