kb:situation-as-scope
    pred:principle "AN INCOMPLETE SITUATION MUST SAY SO. If a graph in scope could not be read, the reckoning must state that before it states a conclusion — the same rule multi-graph-query already enforces on its result sentence. A confident proposal built on four of six graphs, with no mention of the missing two, is worse than no proposal, because nothing about it looks wrong." ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 3 — a registry of situation reports, so a graph can declare which measurements describe it (the website graph declares website:align; the roadmap declares kb-align) and adding a new measurement makes it available to every reckoning over that scope." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:question-router ;
    pred:scope-decision "SCOPE IS A SET OF GRAPHS PLUS AN OPTIONAL FOCUS. src/lib/rdf/multi-graph-query.ts already merges answers across a chosen set of graphs and — critically for this — reports what it could NOT read, marks agreement as independent or merely repeated, and surfaces cross-graph disagreement instead of resolving it. A situation assembled from it therefore carries its own gaps and contradictions rather than presenting a tidy summary that hides them, which is what a typed situation always does." ;
    pred:principle "A TYPED SITUATION IS AN UNVERIFIABLE CLAIM, and it is the party that benefits doing the typing. Whoever writes the situation frames the answer, and a reckoning grounded on a hand-written premise inherits every error and every convenient omission in that premise — the thesis, applied to the first step of our own reasoning loop. A situation ASSEMBLED FROM GRAPHS can be wrong, but it can be checked, and it says where each part came from." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:prose-as-fact ;
    pred:open-question "What happens when the scoped graphs CONTRADICT each other? multi-graph-query surfaces the disagreement rather than resolving it, so the situation would honestly contain a contradiction. Does the reckoning refuse to propose until it is settled, or propose while naming the contradiction as a risk? The second is more useful and more dangerous." ;
    rdfs:label "The Situation step is a scope selection, not free text" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:design-vs-output ;
    pred:description "Matt (2026-07-30): in Situation-Target-Proposal, the SITUATION is currently typed as free-form prose. It should be a SCOPE SELECTION — which graphs is this reckoning concerned with — and the situation itself should then be established by the graphs, by real research, and by testing. The user picks what is in scope; the system states what is true about that scope; only the TARGET and the PROPOSAL are things a person composes." ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 1 — a scope picker in the Reckoning UI: choose graphs (default the active one), with the F91 router suggesting graphs likely to be relevant rather than making the user remember what they have." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:honest-note "PLANNED, NOTHING BUILT (2026-07-30). The Reckoning UI still takes a typed situation, and none of the current-state reports feed it. The pieces exist and are unconnected: multi-graph-query.ts (scoped federated read, mutation-checked), website-alignment.ts and docs-consolidate (current-state measurement), question-router.ts (ranking which graph can answer, F91). What is missing is a scope PICKER and the assembly step that turns a chosen scope into a situation document." ;
    pred:has-status "planned" ;
    pred:open-question "Is free-text situation removed entirely, or kept as an ANNOTATION on an assembled one? Removing it is cleaner and risks losing context only the user holds — the thing that is true but not yet in any graph. Keeping it risks the annotation quietly becoming the situation again. A middle answer: free text is accepted and immediately offered as pending facts, so context the user holds ENTERS the graph rather than bypassing it." ;
    pred:principle "SCOPE IS THE ONLY THING THE USER IS UNIQUELY QUALIFIED TO SUPPLY. Which graphs matter for this decision is judgment. What those graphs currently say is measurement. Asking a person to type the measurement wastes the judgment and invites them to assert what they wish were true." ;
    pred:feature-id "F112" ;
    pred:remaining "PHASE 2 — situation assembly: run the scoped read and any current-state reports registered for those graphs, and render the result as the situation, each part citing the graph it came from and the unreadable ones named." ;
    pred:scope-decision "THE MEASUREMENTS ALREADY EXIST AND ARE THE SITUATION. Every current-state report built for the website work is exactly a situation over a declared scope: npm run website:align (design vs observed output, per-page divergences), npm run docs:consolidate (coverage, orphans, deliberate exclusions, cross-dataset overlap), npm run brief (branch, PR, queue depth, in-flight roadmap status), and scripts/kb-align.ts (code vs roadmap). None of these is prose and all of them are checkable. The feature is to make selecting a scope PRODUCE one of these rather than asking a human to summarise it from memory." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:multi-kb .

