kb:orch-task-vocab
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Phase> ;
    pred:has-status "functional" ;
    pred:remaining "Define the predicates in the graph; graph-lint checks (no task blocked forever, no task without a tier, no due-at in an unparseable format); render tasks in-app." ;
    pred:tested-by "src/lib/rdf/__tests__/agent-task.test.ts" ;
    pred:part-of kb:agent-orchestration ;
    rdfs:label "Phase 1 — the task vocabulary (a task is a triple)" ;
    <urn:reckons:nav/order> "1"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:has-file "src/lib/rdf/agent-task.ts" ;
    pred:description "ktype:AgentTask with a defined vocabulary, so a schedule is a fact and not a config file: kpred:goal (what to do, in words a harness can act on), kpred:tier (script | local-agent | frontier — routes to the cheapest harness that can do it), kpred:harness (a PREFERENCE, not a requirement — 'any' is valid and is the default), kpred:due-at / kpred:every (drain-not-schedule, per F81), kpred:blocked-by (a partial fact, a question, or another task), kpred:writes-to (where the outcome lands: pending.jsonl, a branch, a PR), kpred:outcome (written back on completion, INCLUDING 'did nothing, and here is why')." ;
    pred:proof "The loop closes, demonstrated end to end on reckons-workspace/tasks.ttl. Run 1: three tasks claimed, executed, INDEPENDENTLY verified, outcomes written back. A task with no done-when was REFUSED; a task with an unmet dependency was HELD. Run 2: the finished tasks were skipped and the blocked task UNBLOCKED ITSELF and ran, because its dependency had completed. Run 3: idle. An expired lease with no outcome is detected and requeued." ;
    pred:has-file "scripts/agent/runner.ts" ;
    pred:schedules "Scheduling lives in the GRAPH, as kpred:every — an INTERVAL, not a cron expression. Cron says 'at 03:40 on Tuesdays', which assumes a machine that is awake with a daemon that fires; we know what that promise is worth, because the cloud cron fired exactly on schedule, produced nothing, reported nothing, and went on showing a future run time. An interval claims only 'not more often than this', and whichever runner wakes next drains what is due. The next due time is computed from when the run ACTUALLY happened, so a machine asleep for a week wakes owing one run, not seven. DRAIN, DO NOT SCHEDULE." .

