kb:search-consistency
    pred:decision "POOLPARTY IS INTEROP, NOT A DEPENDENCY. PoolParty is a SKOS thesaurus manager, and SKOS is exactly what steps 2 and 3 produce — so the integration is IMPORT/EXPORT of a SKOS concept scheme, letting an enterprise bring a thesaurus they already maintain. It must never become required to search your own graph. ELASTICSEARCH IS REJECTED FOR CORE: Reckons.AI is local-first with no general backend (see the integration-boundaries rule), and a search server would make a network service mandatory for the most basic operation in the product. As an optional per-user integration for very large graphs it is defensible; as the search layer it is not." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:agent-context-dogfooding ;
    pred:feature-id "F104" ;
    pred:remaining "Not built. (1) workspace symlink coverage + a script-tier check that every static/*.ttl is reachable by MCP; (2) skos:altLabel authoring UI + BM25 query expansion; (3) embedding-backed fallback ranking; (4) SKOS concept-scheme import/export for PoolParty interop." ;
    pred:honest-note "A THESAURUS NOBODY MAINTAINS IS WORSE THAN NONE — it silently expands queries along relationships that are stale, and the user cannot see why a search returned what it did. So altLabels must be inspectable, attributable, and reviewable like any other fact (they ARE facts), and query expansion must be able to show which term matched via which alias. Unexplained recall is not an improvement over unexplained silence." ;
    pred:measured "CAUSE 1, COVERAGE (the big one, measured 2026-07-19): the MCP server sees 6 of 23 graphs. mcp-workspace/kbs symlinks only roadmap, production, codebase, docs-architecture, docs-features and docs-testing — kb_stats reports ~3.9K triples against a static/ corpus of ~7.3K quads. INVISIBLE to kb_search: reckons-shipped.ttl (the SHIPPED, production-status features — so a search for completed work can return nothing and invite a rebuild), reckons-competitive.ttl, knowledge.ttl (3.4K lines), and 8 of the docs-*.ttl graphs including docs-coding-workflow.ttl. The 2026-07-19 case study documenting how well the graph prevents duplication was itself written into a graph kb_search cannot see." ;
    pred:open-question "Is a user-defined taxonomy an enterprise feature or a core one? UNDECIDED (Matt, 2026-07-19). Argument for core: the synonym problem bites a single user with one graph, and a personal vocabulary ('my clients' = 'accounts') is exactly the personal context this product claims to hold. Argument for enterprise: maintaining a controlled vocabulary is real work most individuals will not do, and it overlaps RBAC/governance territory. Leaning core-but-optional, with PoolParty import as the enterprise on-ramp." ;
    rdfs:label "Search consistency — coverage first, then a user taxonomy" ;
    pred:description "Make kb_search reliable enough that grep is not needed as a backstop. Two independent causes were measured on 2026-07-19 and they need fixing in cost order: a COVERAGE gap (the MCP server is symlinked to only 6 of 23 graphs, so shipped features and most docs are simply unindexed) and a VOCABULARY gap (BM25 does no synonym expansion, so 'set' cannot match 'group'). Coverage is a symlink change with no new concepts; vocabulary needs skos:altLabel plus query expansion, then embeddings for the synonyms nobody listed. A user-defined taxonomy sits on top, with PoolParty as SKOS interop rather than a dependency." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:priority "high" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:predicate-economy ;
    pred:relates-to kb:entity-normalization ;
    pred:has-status "planned" ;
    pred:measured "CAUSE 2, VOCABULARY: BM25 (mcp-server/src/search.ts) indexes subjectSlug + predicateSlug + object with no expansion, so a synonym gap is fatal — 'group nodes into a set' and 'group the queue into one decision' share no term. This is the class scripts/offline/shared-deps.ts also cannot detect (see kb:entity-sets honest-note), and it is what a thesaurus fixes." ;
    pred:decision "FIX IN COST ORDER, AND DO NOT START WITH THE INTERESTING ONE. (1) COVERAGE — symlink the remaining graphs into the MCP workspace (scripts/setup-reckons-workspace.sh). No taxonomy, no new dependency, likely removes most grep fallbacks on its own. Ship this before anything else, or the taxonomy work will be measured against a broken baseline. (2) SKOS THESAURUS — skos:altLabel / skos:prefLabel / skos:hiddenLabel on entities, plus query expansion in BM25. Note the graph is ALREADY SKOS: skos:definition 517 uses, skos:broader 420, skos:related 233 — but altLabel, prefLabel and hiddenLabel are used ZERO times. The thesaurus half of the standard we already run is simply unused, so this needs no new vocabulary and no new tool. (3) EMBEDDINGS — BGE-small already ships in-app (src/lib/embed.ts) and catches synonyms nobody thought to list, which is the limit of any hand-written thesaurus." ;
    pred:principle "A SILENT EMPTY RESULT IS THE DANGEROUS FAILURE. Search returning nothing looks identical whether the fact is absent or merely unindexed — and an agent that reads 'not found' as 'does not exist' rebuilds what already exists. That is precisely the duplication this graph is supposed to prevent, so a coverage gap in search is not a search bug, it is a hole in the product's core claim." .

