kb:standards-review
    pred:relates-to kb:shape-validation ;
    pred:measured "THE LOSSLESS STANDARD FORMAT IS ALREADY BUILT AND NOTHING CALLS IT. src/lib/rdf/serialize.ts exports toTriG, documented as the lossless format for F75 and covered by tests; a repo-wide search finds NO caller outside serialize.ts and its own tests. Meanwhile every path that actually ships uses toTurtleFull: workspace sync, backup, auto-save, graph snapshots. The readers are already TriG-tolerant — import-ttl.ts and multi-graph-parse.ts both parse with format 'TriG' because every .ttl is legal TriG — so the gap is WIRING, not capability or design." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Decision> ;
    pred:has-status "functional" ;
    pred:remaining "ORDERED BY WHAT EACH UNBLOCKS. (1) Wire toTriG into export and the graph package, so provenance survives leaving the app — the only item that is pure wiring of something already built and tested. (2) Decide reification versus RDF-star for statement metadata, which needs the term model verified through Dexie and back, not just a parse. (3) The in-app SHACL gate, where shacl-engine is the better candidate than rdf-validate-shacl because its Comunica weight is opt-in (only lib/sparql.js imports it) and it is browser-tested — measured 1.5x faster on this corpus, not the advertised 15-26x. (4) User-authored shapes, which is the whole argument for shapes-as-data and remains unavailable to the users it was argued for." ;
    pred:feature-id "F132" ;
    pred:decided "REIFICATION IS A CONSEQUENCE OF A DELIBERATE CHOICE, NOT AN OVERSIGHT — so 'replace it with TriG' is the wrong reading. TriG has ONE graph slot and serialize.ts spends it on the SOURCE, because that is what types.ts says the graph term means and overloading it to bundle KBs 'would silently destroy the provenance that is the entire point of the product'. Statement-level metadata — status, confidence, gloss, the review state this product is built around — therefore has nowhere native to go, and toTurtleFull emits rdf:Statement reification whose own header admits it is 'invisible to standard RDF tooling'. That is the real cost, and it is paid knowingly." ;
    pred:open-question "RDF-star (RDF 1.2 quoted triples) is the standard that annotates a triple directly and would retire the reification blocks. PARTIALLY CHECKED, NOT ADOPTED: n3 2.1.1 — already shipped here — accepts the syntax, but the parse produced blank-node subjects rather than a quoted-triple term, so it desugars rather than modelling it natively. Whether the term model is usable end to end (parse, store in Dexie, re-serialize) is unverified, and claiming it works on one successful parse would be exactly the overclaim this project forbids." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:rdf-dataset-migration ;
    rdfs:label "Standards review — SHACL and TriG against what the app actually does" ;
    pred:description "Matt, 2026-08-14: 'we need to review most of the app considering the better technologies of both SHACL and TriG. The premise remains the same, a personal TTL editor. However as we get into any integrated usage we need those more robust standards.' Reviewed 2026-08-14 by measurement rather than principle." ;
    pred:measured "F75 WAS RECORDED AS 'planned' WHILE ITS READER WORKED AND ITS WRITER EXISTED. Corrected to 'scaffolded' in this review. Worth noting what this says about the new machinery: the SHACL shape validates that has-status is a value from the lifecycle, and cannot know whether the value is TRUE. Form checking does not catch drift, which is exactly the distinction F130 draws." ;
    pred:decided "THE PREMISE HOLDS: a personal TTL editor stays Turtle-first on disk, because a human opening one file and reading it is the product. Standards earn their place at the INTEGRATION boundary — what leaves the app or arrives from elsewhere — which is where the graph meets tools that were not written here. So the sequence is: wire TriG for EXPORT and sync (lossless, standard, already built), keep Turtle for the single-graph human-readable case, and leave the on-disk default alone." .

