kb:orch-claim-lease
    rdfs:label "Phase 3 — claims, leases, and surviving a usage limit" ;
    pred:learned-from "unsnooze exists ENTIRELY because agent sessions die on usage limits and nothing notices. Our queue currently has no concept of a harness running out of budget — a task handed to Claude Code at a limit boundary would simply stop, and the graph would say it was in progress forever. That is the same silent-failure class as the cron that fired and produced nothing." ;
    <urn:reckons:nav/order> "3"^^xsd:integer ;
    pred:remaining "HOST RUNNER FUNCTIONAL, BROADER RUNTIMES REMAIN (2026-08-17). scripts/agent/runner.ts now claims under Linux kernel flock, rereads attempts/due/state inside the lock, fences renew/completion with a random claim token plus an unexpired lease, persists WAITING question keys, fails closed on corrupt state, and atomically replaces state/receipt/context files. Deterministic process tests hold the lock until two runners have both selected one task, then prove exactly one command executes. Remaining: the in-app Worker has no equivalent claim store; multi-host claims need a shared backend rather than a host file; graph-lint still needs the abandoned-claim check." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Phase> ;
    pred:description "The moment more than one runner drains one queue, two of them will take the same task. A task therefore carries kpred:claimed-by + kpred:claim-expires (a LEASE, not a lock — a runner that dies must not hold a task forever). When a harness stops on a usage limit, the lease lapses and the task returns to the queue for a different harness. This is the concrete mechanism behind 'a harness must be replaceable mid-task'." ;
    pred:part-of kb:agent-orchestration ;
    pred:has-status "functional" .

