kb:auto-archive
    pred:relates-to kb:currents ;
    pred:has-status "in-progress" ;
    pred:honest-note "planned -> in-progress (2026-07-30): a user can now archive aged-out events by hand from /kb, previewed before it moves, so the feature is startable for the first time. The status covers exactly that. The word AUTO is still unearned — no schedule, no threshold trigger, no proactive nudge — and the production delete/merge/prune paths still bypass the archive entirely. Do not read in-progress as 'auto-archive works'." ;
    pred:scope-decision "FULL SNAPSHOTS for revert (Matt, 2026-07-18). Each destructive operation stores a complete pre-operation graph snapshot rather than a diff or a per-statement log. Chosen for trivially-correct revert and true time-travel (graph as-of a date). ACCEPTED COST: snapshot storage grows with graph size × operation count, which cuts against the node-count goal that motivates the feature — so RETENTION IS LOAD-BEARING here, not an optional extra, and ships with phase 1 rather than after it." ;
    rdfs:label "Auto-archive by age — a 'current' graph trimmed to the relevant timeframe" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:multi-kb ;
    pred:principle "Archive is reversible and non-destructive — trimming the CURRENT view must never lose the fact. An auto-archive that quietly deleted history would be the silent-destruction failure again; it MOVES facts, it does not erase them, and the threshold is the user's to set." ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:scope-decision "EDIT JOURNAL, not just aged-out facts (Matt, 2026-07-18). The archive records EVENTS — deletes, merges, prune runs, age-drop sweeps — each as a reified entity carrying timestamp, actor (human vs agent), and payload. That turns the archive from a graveyard into the graph's edit history, and is what makes revert possible at all." ;
    pred:description "Easy auto-archive of time-stamped events older than a configurable threshold (X days, or however the user sets it), so a graph stays a CURRENT graph — adjusted to context, trimmed to the most relevant timeframe (Matt, 2026-07-15). Archived is NOT deleted: the facts move out of the active/current view into an archive the user can restore or query, so history is preserved but the working graph stays focused. This is the TIME axis of the F83/condensation focus story: large graphs (timelines especially) are answered by trimming to a window, not a hard cap. Ties to kb:currents (the 'current' concept), the F83 time filter, and F97-style proactive suggestion (nudge to archive when the graph spans far more time than the user is working in)." ;
    pred:remaining "The word AUTO. A manual, previewed age sweep exists on /kb (F97.1 functional), and nothing runs on its own: no schedule, no age-threshold trigger, no proactive nudge when a graph spans far more time than the user works in. The sweep is CURRENT-GRAPH-ONLY — sweeping an unloaded graph needs its statements read from its own Dexie database rather than the in-memory store, which is a more dangerous operation against something the user is not looking at. The production DELETE, MERGE and PRUNE paths still bypass the archive entirely; only age-drop is wired. Phases F97.4 recurrence, F97.5 time-travel and F97.7 archive search are unstarted." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:graph-legibility ;
    pred:scope-decision "SEPARATE ARCHIVE GRAPH, not an archived flag (Matt, 2026-07-18). The archive is its own graph in the registry, named '<parent name> (archives)' and shown alongside the parent on the graph settings page, linked back by the parent's stableId. This is what makes the node-count win real: archived facts leave the working graph's Dexie store entirely rather than being filtered out of a view. ON BY DEFAULT — the reduction in node count is the point, and a default-off archive would be a feature nobody discovers." ;
    pred:feature-id "F97" .

