kb:local-ocr
    rdfs:label "Local OCR (Ollama / WASM)" ;
    pred:has-status "functional" ;
    pred:feature-id "F70" ;
    pred:relates-to kb:local-offload ;
    rdf:type <urn:kbase:type/Feature> ;
    pred:tested-by "tests/visual/user-stories/ocr-ingest.test.ts" ;
    pred:description "Offline text-from-image, complementing the existing CLOUD OCR (Mistral, src/lib/integrations/parsers/mistral-ocr.ts — PDFs/images → markdown in ingest). Two local paths, both viable: (a) OLLAMA VLMs — PROVEN 2026-07-10: qwen2.5vl:7b (already installed for the VLM visual gate) OCR'd a full UI screenshot verbatim, every line correct, in ~78s at temp 0; qwen2.5vl:3b / granite3.2-vision are faster options. Reuses the VLM-gate plumbing (tests/visual/vision-local.ts). Slow for big images. (b) WASM — Tesseract.js (classic in-browser OCR, small ~2-4MB lang data, fast, fully offline) OR transformers.js OCR models (TrOCR / Florence-2 / GOT-OCR2 via @huggingface/transformers, already a dep). Use cases: an OFFLINE ingest OCR option (no Mistral key), and text extraction for the visual tests. SHIPPED 2026-07-11 with Tesseract.js (WASM): src/lib/integrations/parsers/local-ocr.ts (ocrImageLocal, lang data from cdn.jsdelivr — CSP-allowed); ingest images now try Mistral then FALL BACK to local OCR (so images always read offline) — also fixed a latent bug where api.mistral.ai was missing from the CSP connect-src (Mistral OCR was blocked for everyone). Visual harness: ocrScreenshot / normalizeOcr in tests/visual/vision-local.ts (invert+2x-upscale preprocessing recovers dark-UI text; assert substrings, not exact, on stylized fonts). qwen2.5vl VLM path remains for layout/semantic reads." ;
    pred:evidence "alaamroue/pdf-parser-bench (MIT, 9 stars — small, but the METHODOLOGY is the value) benchmarks eight open PDF parsers including MinerU 2.5, Granite-Docling, PaddleOCR-VL and Chandra against synthetic PDFs built to trigger specific failure modes. Its scoring rule is directly adoptable by tests/bench: handwriting ground truth is accepted only where TWO INDEPENDENT READINGS AGREE, and illegible words are marked as such so a model is never credited for inventing text. That is kb:thesis expressed as a benchmark rule, and F70 shipped Tesseract.js with no comparative baseline." ;
    pred:relates-to kb:visual-testing .

